(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e2a.7ea3b0fe51.js"]={"27":[{"id":"sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the cellular mechanism of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which glucose transporter mediates glucose entry into pancreatic beta cells, and why is its kinetic property essential for linking blood glucose concentration to insulin secretion?","choices":{"D":"GLUT2 transports glucose into beta cells with high capacity and low affinity, so intracellular glucose rises in proportion to blood glucose and drives ATP production for secretion.","A":"GLUT1 transports glucose into beta cells with low capacity and high affinity, saturating at low glucose levels so that any further rise in blood glucose cannot increase intracellular ATP.","B":"GLUT4 transports glucose into beta cells by insulin-dependent translocation from intracellular vesicles, creating a positive feedback loop that amplifies glucose-stimulated secretion.","C":"GLUT3 transports glucose into beta cells with very high affinity and low capacity, ensuring near-maximal glucose uptake occurs at low blood glucose levels during fasting.","E":"SGLT2 transports glucose into beta cells via sodium co-transport, making glucose entry dependent on the sodium gradient rather than the blood glucose concentration."},"explanation":"Beta cells express GLUT2, a high-capacity, low-affinity bidirectional transporter whose transport rate is proportional to blood glucose concentration across the physiological range. Because GLUT2 does not saturate at normal blood glucose levels, glucose entry — and therefore intracellular glucose metabolism and ATP generation — directly reflects blood glucose. This proportionality is what makes glucokinase (the next rate-limiting step) an effective glucose sensor. GLUT4 is insulin-dependent and found in muscle and adipose. GLUT1 is high-affinity (low Km) and saturable at low glucose, making it unsuitable for glucose sensing. SGLT2 is a renal cotransporter targeted by gliflozin drugs. GLUT3 is a brain and placenta transporter; beta cells use GLUT2, which lets glucose influx track blood glucose across the physiological range.","boardPrep":"Step 1: BRICK LIPS = insulin-independent glucose uptake tissues: Brain, RBCs, Intestine, Cornea, Kidney, Liver, Islet beta cells, Placenta, Spermatocytes. GLUT2 in beta cells and liver is bidirectional. Classic trap: GLUT4 requires insulin and is NOT present in beta cells. COMLEX: SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin) reduce renal glucose reabsorption and are cardioprotective in type 2 DM — know the mechanism difference from sulfonylureas.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — G protein-coupled receptor signaling and cAMP second messenger amplify calcium-mediated exocytosis in the beta cell","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"GLUT1 is high-affinity (low Km","B":"GLUT4 is insulin-dependent and","C":"GLUT3 is a brain and placenta transporter; beta cells use GL","E":"SGLT2 is a renal cotransporter"}},{"id":"sdl27_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the cellular mechanism of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"After glucose enters the pancreatic beta cell via GLUT2 and is phosphorylated by glucokinase, what is the immediate downstream event that couples glucose metabolism to membrane depolarization?","choices":{"A":"Elevated intracellular ATP generated from glucose oxidation closes ATP-sensitive potassium channels, preventing K+ efflux and causing membrane depolarization.","B":"Elevated intracellular cAMP generated from adenylyl cyclase activation opens ATP-sensitive potassium channels, causing K+ influx and gradual membrane hyperpolarization.","C":"Elevated intracellular glucose-6-phosphate directly binds and opens voltage-gated calcium channels in the beta cell membrane, triggering exocytosis of secretory granules.","D":"Elevated intracellular NADH generated from glucose oxidation activates phospholipase C, producing IP3 that releases calcium from the endoplasmic reticulum to trigger exocytosis.","E":"Elevated intracellular ATP directly opens voltage-gated calcium channels in the beta cell membrane without requiring intermediate closure of potassium channels."},"explanation":"The core glucose-stimulated insulin secretion (GSIS) mechanism: glucose enters via GLUT2 → glucokinase phosphorylates it to glucose-6-phosphate (rate-limiting step, the true glucose sensor) → oxidative metabolism generates ATP → the rising ATP:ADP ratio closes ATP-sensitive K+ channels (K-ATP channels) → K+ can no longer leave the cell → the membrane depolarizes → voltage-gated calcium channels open → Ca2+ influx triggers fusion of insulin-containing secretory granules with the plasma membrane → exocytosis. This entire sequence is the pharmacologic target of sulfonylureas, which block K-ATP channels independently of ATP, mimicking elevated glucose. Insulin secretion is triggered by ATP closing K-ATP channels, not by cAMP opening them; K+ efflux is blocked so the membrane depolarizes. The course links glucose metabolism to depolarization via ATP closing K-ATP channels, not NADH activating phospholipase C and IP3 release. ATP closes ATP-sensitive potassium channels, causing depolarization, which then opens voltage-gated calcium channels; ATP does not open the calcium channels directly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The K-ATP channel is encoded by Kir6.2 (channel) and SUR1 (sulfonylurea receptor subunit). Mutations in SUR1 cause congenital hyperinsulinism (persistent K-ATP closure = persistent insulin secretion = neonatal hypoglycemia). Sulfonylureas (glyburide, glipizide) block this same channel to stimulate insulin in type 2 DM. COMLEX: In the osteopathic management of type 2 DM, sulfonylureas carry hypoglycemia risk and weight gain — both relevant to COMLEX wellness and metabolic syndrome counseling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — G protein-coupled receptor signaling and cAMP second messenger amplify calcium-mediated exocytosis in the beta cell","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Insulin secretion is triggered by ATP closing K-ATP channels","D":"The course links glucose metabolism to depolarization via AT","E":"ATP closes ATP-sensitive potassium channels, causing depolar"}},{"id":"sdl27_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the cellular mechanism of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which ion and which channel type mediate the final step in glucose-stimulated insulin secretion — the step that directly triggers fusion of insulin-containing granules with the beta cell plasma membrane?","choices":{"E":"Calcium influx through voltage-gated calcium channels, opened by ATP-induced membrane depolarization, triggers exocytosis of insulin-containing secretory granules.","A":"Sodium influx through voltage-gated sodium channels, opened by ATP-induced membrane depolarization, triggers exocytosis of insulin-containing secretory granules.","B":"Potassium efflux through ATP-sensitive potassium channels, activated by the rising ATP concentration, triggers exocytosis of insulin-containing secretory granules.","C":"Chloride influx through GABA-gated chloride channels, activated by somatostatin released from adjacent delta cells, triggers exocytosis of insulin-containing secretory granules.","D":"Proton efflux through H+/K+-ATPase pumps, driven by glucose-induced acidification of the beta cell cytoplasm, triggers exocytosis of insulin-containing secretory granules."},"explanation":"After membrane depolarization caused by K-ATP channel closure, voltage-gated Ca2+ channels (L-type) open. The resulting Ca2+ influx raises intracellular calcium concentration, which activates the exocytotic machinery — specifically SNARE proteins that mediate fusion of insulin-containing secretory granules with the plasma membrane. Insulin and C-peptide are co-secreted in equimolar amounts. This Ca2+-dependent exocytosis mechanism is shared with neurotransmitter release. Incretins (GLP-1, GIP) amplify this step by raising cAMP, which further opens Ca2+ channels and sensitizes the exocytotic machinery. Insulin granule exocytosis is triggered by calcium influx through voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, not sodium influx. K-ATP channels are closed by rising ATP, which stops K+ efflux and depolarizes the cell; exocytosis is triggered by Ca2+ entry. Exocytosis is triggered by Ca2+ influx through voltage-gated channels, and somatostatin inhibits rather than triggers insulin release. Glucose-induced exocytosis depends on Ca2+ influx through voltage-gated calcium channels, not proton efflux through H+/K+-ATPase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The final step is Ca2+ influx through voltage-gated calcium channels. Diazoxide opens K-ATP channels (opposite of sulfonylurea) to treat insulinoma or congenital hyperinsulinism by preventing depolarization and blocking insulin release. Ca2+ channel blockers can thus impair insulin secretion. COMLEX: Diazoxide, a K-ATP channel opener, is used to suppress hypoglycemia in insulinoma patients awaiting surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — G protein-coupled receptor signaling and cAMP second messenger amplify calcium-mediated exocytosis in the beta cell","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Insulin granule exocytosis is triggered by calcium influx th","B":"K-ATP channels are closed by rising ATP, which stops K+ effl","C":"Exocytosis is triggered by Ca2+ influx through voltage-gated","D":"Glucose-induced exocytosis depends on Ca2+ influx through vo"}},{"id":"sdl27_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the insulin receptor structure and signal transduction pathway.","batch":1,"stem":"The insulin receptor is a heterotetrameric protein held together by disulfide bonds. Which subunit arrangement correctly describes its structure and the location of intrinsic enzymatic activity?","choices":{"C":"The receptor has two transmembrane alpha subunits that span the membrane and two extracellular beta subunits that bind insulin on the outside of the cell.","A":"The receptor has two extracellular alpha subunits that bind insulin and two transmembrane beta subunits whose cytoplasmic tails contain intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity.","B":"The receptor has one extracellular alpha subunit that binds insulin and one transmembrane beta subunit with serine kinase activity in its cytoplasmic tail.","D":"The receptor has two extracellular alpha subunits that bind insulin and two transmembrane beta subunits whose cytoplasmic tails contain intrinsic serine/threonine kinase activity.","E":"The receptor has four identical transmembrane subunits with cytoplasmic kinase domains that are activated by dimerization following insulin-induced conformational change."},"explanation":"The insulin receptor is a receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK) that exists as a preformed heterotetramer: two alpha (α) subunits entirely extracellular that contain the insulin-binding site, linked by disulfide bonds to two beta (β) subunits that each span the membrane once and extend into the cytoplasm where they carry intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity. When insulin binds the alpha subunits, conformational changes cause the beta subunits to undergo transautophosphorylation on tyrosine residues in their kinase domain, fully activating the kinase. The activated beta subunit then phosphorylates downstream insulin receptor substrate (IRS) proteins. The insulin receptor is a heterotetramer with two alpha and two beta subunits, and the kinase is a tyrosine kinase, not serine. The alpha subunits are entirely extracellular and bind insulin, and the transmembrane beta subunits carry the kinase; this option reverses them. The insulin receptor is a receptor tyrosine kinase, so the beta subunit cytoplasmic domains have tyrosine kinase activity, not serine/threonine kinase activity. The receptor is a preformed heterotetramer of two alpha and two beta subunits, not four identical transmembrane subunits activated by dimerization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The insulin receptor is the prototypical enzyme-linked receptor tyrosine kinase — distinct from GPCR (glucagon uses Gs-cAMP) and from nuclear receptors (steroid hormones). Type A insulin resistance = mutation in insulin receptor itself (e.g., Rabson-Mendenhall syndrome). Type B insulin resistance = blocking antibodies against the alpha subunit (autoimmune). COMLEX: Tyrosine kinase receptor signaling is the mechanism behind insulin's growth-promoting effects that synergize with IGF-1 and growth hormone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — Tyrosine kinase receptor: ligand binding, receptor dimerization, and autophosphorylation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"The insulin receptor is a heterotetramer with two alpha and ","C":"The alpha subunits are entirely extracellular and bind insul","D":"The insulin receptor is a receptor tyrosine kinase, so the b","E":"The receptor is a preformed heterotetramer of two alpha and "}},{"id":"sdl27_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the insulin receptor structure and signal transduction pathway.","batch":1,"stem":"After autophosphorylation of the insulin receptor beta subunit, what is the immediate downstream substrate that propagates the metabolic and mitogenic signal to multiple intracellular pathways?","choices":{"A":"Insulin receptor substrate proteins (IRS-1, IRS-2, IRS-3) are tyrosine-phosphorylated by the activated receptor kinase and serve as docking platforms for downstream signaling molecules.","B":"Adenylyl cyclase is directly phosphorylated by the activated receptor kinase, increasing cAMP production which then activates protein kinase A to mediate all downstream metabolic effects.","C":"Phospholipase C-gamma is directly phosphorylated by the activated receptor kinase, producing IP3 and DAG that activate protein kinase C and raise intracellular calcium concentration.","D":"JAK2 kinase is transactivated by the activated insulin receptor kinase, which then phosphorylates STAT5 to mediate all downstream gene transcription effects of insulin.","E":"Ras GTPase is directly phosphorylated by the activated receptor kinase, causing GTP exchange and initiating the MAPK cascade that mediates all metabolic and growth effects."},"explanation":"Following beta subunit autophosphorylation, the insulin receptor phosphorylates a family of adapter proteins called insulin receptor substrates (IRS-1 through IRS-4). The phosphorylated IRS proteins serve as scaffolds that recruit SH2 domain-containing proteins including PI3-kinase (PI3K). PI3K activation leads to PIP3 production, Akt phosphorylation, and ultimately GLUT4 vesicle translocation to the membrane. A parallel pathway through Ras/MAPK mediates the mitogenic and growth effects. Adenylyl cyclase/cAMP is the glucagon pathway (Gs-GPCR), not insulin. PLC-gamma is used by some RTKs but is not the primary insulin signaling intermediate. JAK-STAT is the leptin receptor pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PI3K-Akt is the main metabolic arm of insulin signaling; Ras-MAPK is the growth/mitogenic arm. Serine phosphorylation of IRS-1 (not tyrosine) by inflammatory kinases like IKK-beta inhibits insulin signaling — this is a key mechanism of obesity-induced insulin resistance. The FA mnemonic for insulin effects: increases glucose transport, glycogen synthesis, TG synthesis, Na+ retention, protein synthesis, K+ uptake; decreases glucagon, lipolysis. COMLEX: IRS serine phosphorylation by inflammatory pathways connects to COMLEX concepts of chronic inflammation as a driver of metabolic syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — Tyrosine kinase receptor: ligand binding, receptor dimerization, and autophosphorylation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"C":"PLC-gamma is used by some RTKs","D":"JAK-STAT is the leptin recepto"}},{"id":"sdl27_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the insulin receptor structure and signal transduction pathway.","batch":1,"stem":"Which glucose transporter is translocated from intracellular vesicles to the plasma membrane of skeletal muscle and adipose cells in response to insulin receptor activation, and what is the effect on cellular glucose uptake?","choices":{"B":"GLUT4 is translocated from intracellular storage vesicles to the plasma membrane following PI3K-Akt pathway activation, increasing insulin-dependent glucose uptake in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue.","A":"GLUT2 is translocated from intracellular storage vesicles to the plasma membrane following PI3K-Akt pathway activation, increasing glucose uptake in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue.","C":"GLUT1 is translocated from intracellular storage vesicles to the plasma membrane following PI3K-Akt pathway activation, increasing insulin-dependent glucose uptake in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue.","D":"GLUT3 is translocated from intracellular storage vesicles to the plasma membrane following IRS-1 phosphorylation, increasing insulin-dependent glucose uptake in hepatocytes and adipose tissue.","E":"GLUT5 is translocated from intracellular storage vesicles to the plasma membrane following PI3K-Akt pathway activation, increasing fructose uptake in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue."},"explanation":"GLUT4 is the insulin-sensitive glucose transporter expressed in skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle, and adipose tissue. In the basal (no insulin) state, GLUT4 is sequestered in intracellular vesicles and the membrane is relatively impermeable to glucose. Insulin receptor activation → IRS phosphorylation → PI3K activation → PIP3 production → Akt phosphorylation → AS160 phosphorylation → GLUT4 vesicle trafficking to the plasma membrane → up to 15-fold increase in glucose uptake. Exercise independently triggers GLUT4 translocation via AMPK activation, explaining why exercise lowers blood glucose even in the absence of insulin. GLUT2 is in liver and beta cells; GLUT1 is in RBCs and brain (insulin-independent). GLUT3 is not the insulin-sensitive transporter; GLUT4 translocates in muscle and adipose tissue, not hepatocytes. GLUT5 is a fructose transporter that is not insulin-regulated; GLUT4 is the transporter translocated by insulin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLUT4 translocation is the key insulin-mediated mechanism for glucose disposal. In type 2 diabetes, defective GLUT4 translocation is the primary mediator of reduced glucose uptake in muscle and fat. Exercise mimics insulin by activating AMPK to translocate GLUT4 — this is why exercise is therapeutic for type 2 DM independent of weight loss. COMLEX: Physical activity prescriptions for type 2 DM utilize the AMPK-GLUT4 pathway; OMM and exercise physiology principles underlie osteopathic management of metabolic syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — Tyrosine kinase receptor: ligand binding, receptor dimerization, and autophosphorylation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"D":"GLUT3 is not the insulin-sensitive transporter; GLUT4 transl","E":"GLUT5 is a fructose transporter that is not insulin-regulate"}},{"id":"sdl27_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify factors that increase and decrease insulin secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Sulfonylurea drugs (e.g., glyburide, glipizide) stimulate insulin secretion in type 2 diabetes by what specific cellular mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Sulfonylureas bind the alpha subunit of the insulin receptor, mimicking insulin binding and triggering tyrosine kinase autophosphorylation to increase glucose uptake and insulin gene expression.","B":"Sulfonylureas bind the SUR1 regulatory subunit of ATP-sensitive potassium channels, blocking K+ efflux and causing membrane depolarization that triggers calcium influx and insulin exocytosis.","C":"Sulfonylureas inhibit glucokinase phosphorylation of glucose in beta cells, forcing glucose oxidation through alternative pathways that generate more ATP per molecule for channel closure.","D":"Sulfonylureas activate adenylyl cyclase in beta cells, raising intracellular cAMP that activates protein kinase A to phosphorylate and open voltage-gated calcium channels directly.","E":"Sulfonylureas block GLUT2 glucose transporters in beta cells, concentrating intracellular glucose and forcing maximal ATP production that closes potassium channels even at low blood glucose."},"explanation":"Sulfonylureas bind to SUR1, the regulatory subunit of the ATP-sensitive K+ channel (K-ATP channel, composed of Kir6.2 + SUR1). By blocking the channel independently of ATP, sulfonylureas cause persistent membrane depolarization → voltage-gated Ca2+ channels open → Ca2+ influx → insulin exocytosis. This mechanism is glucose-independent, explaining why sulfonylureas can cause hypoglycemia even in fasting states. They are only effective if functional beta cells are still present (ineffective in type 1 DM). The K-ATP channel has two subunits: Kir6.2 (the pore) and SUR1 (the regulatory subunit that binds ATP and sulfonylureas). Sulfonylureas bind and block ATP-sensitive potassium channels in beta cells, not the insulin receptor alpha subunit. Sulfonylureas block the K-ATP channel independently of ATP and do not inhibit glucokinase. Sulfonylureas block ATP-sensitive potassium channels to depolarize the cell rather than activating adenylyl cyclase to open calcium channels. Sulfonylureas act directly on the K-ATP channel independent of glucose metabolism, not by blocking GLUT2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sulfonylurea mechanism = K-ATP channel blocker = insulin secretagogue. Risk: hypoglycemia and weight gain. Meglitinides (repaglinide) work by the same mechanism but at a different binding site on SUR1, with shorter duration. Diazoxide (K-ATP channel opener) is the antidote for sulfonylurea-induced hypoglycemia and is used for insulinoma. COMLEX: Sulfonylurea toxicity (hypoglycemia, weight gain) is a COMLEX clinical management topic; dextrose and octreotide are used for severe sulfonylurea toxicity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — Negative and positive feedback regulation of hormone secretion with clinical examples","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Sulfonylureas bind and block ATP-sensitive potassium channel","C":"Sulfonylureas block the K-ATP channel independently of ATP a","D":"Sulfonylureas block ATP-sensitive potassium channels to depo","E":"Sulfonylureas act directly on the K-ATP channel independent "}},{"id":"sdl27_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify factors that increase and decrease insulin secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Two gastrointestinal hormones are collectively called incretins because they enhance glucose-stimulated insulin secretion from beta cells. Which pair correctly identifies these two hormones and their common cellular mechanism?","choices":{"B":"Secretin and motilin are the two major incretins; both act via Gq-coupled receptors on beta cells to activate phospholipase C and raise intracellular IP3 and DAG for insulin secretion.","A":"Gastrin and cholecystokinin are the two major incretins; both act via tyrosine kinase receptors on beta cells to phosphorylate IRS proteins and stimulate GLUT4 translocation to the membrane.","C":"GLP-1 and GIP are the two major incretins; both act via Gs-coupled receptors on beta cells to raise cAMP, which potentiates Ca2+ influx and insulin exocytosis in a glucose-dependent manner.","D":"Glucagon and somatostatin are the two major incretins; both act via Gs-coupled receptors on beta cells to raise cAMP and potentiate ATP-independent insulin secretion at low glucose levels.","E":"GLP-1 and neurotensin are the two major incretins; both act via nuclear receptors on beta cells to increase PDX-1 transcription factor expression and upregulate insulin gene synthesis."},"explanation":"GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1, from L-cells of the distal intestine) and GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide, from K-cells of the duodenum/jejunum) are the two primary incretins. Both act on Gs-coupled receptors on beta cells, raising intracellular cAMP via adenylyl cyclase. Elevated cAMP activates PKA and Epac2, which sensitize the Ca2+ exocytotic machinery and close additional K-ATP channels. Critically, incretins only amplify insulin secretion when glucose is elevated — they have minimal effect at low blood glucose, explaining the low hypoglycemia risk of GLP-1 agonist drugs. The incretin effect explains why oral glucose raises insulin more than IV glucose (same blood glucose level). Guyton (course reading) groups GLP-1, GIP, glucagon and acetylcholine as hormones that raise beta-cell calcium through other signaling pathways and enhance the effect of glucose; it does not name Gs or cAMP, which is the mechanism given here. Gastrin, secretin and CCK moderately raise insulin in Guyton's Table 79.1 but are not the incretins. Somatostatin inhibits insulin; Guyton lists glucagon as increasing insulin secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The incretin effect: oral glucose causes greater insulin secretion than IV glucose at the same blood glucose level — because gut hormones (GLP-1, GIP) are released only with oral intake. This effect is diminished in type 2 DM. GLP-1 receptor agonists (liraglutide, semaglutide) mimic GLP-1 to treat type 2 DM and obesity. DPP-4 inhibitors (sitagliptin) prevent GLP-1 degradation. COMLEX: GLP-1 agonists cause weight loss (delayed gastric emptying, CNS satiety) and are high-yield for both USMLE and COMLEX pharmacology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — Negative and positive feedback regulation of hormone secretion with clinical examples","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"D":"Somatostatin inhibits insulin;"}},{"id":"sdl27_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify factors that increase and decrease insulin secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly identifies a factor that decreases insulin secretion and explains the physiologic or pharmacologic mechanism by which it does so?","choices":{"C":"Alpha-2 adrenergic receptor stimulation during stress activates Gi-coupled signaling that lowers cAMP in beta cells, inhibiting insulin exocytosis as part of the counterregulatory stress response.","A":"Beta-2 adrenergic receptor stimulation during stress activates Gi-coupled signaling to close voltage-gated calcium channels in beta cells, inhibiting insulin exocytosis during the fight-or-flight response.","B":"Parasympathetic stimulation via acetylcholine acting on muscarinic receptors activates Gi-coupled signaling in beta cells, reducing cAMP and inhibiting insulin secretion during the postprandial state.","D":"Glucagon secreted from alpha cells activates Gi-coupled receptors on adjacent beta cells, directly inhibiting insulin secretion through paracrine signaling within the islet of Langerhans.","E":"Elevated blood free fatty acids activate KATP channels in beta cells through fatty acid oxidation products, reducing membrane depolarization and inhibiting calcium-triggered insulin exocytosis."},"explanation":"Alpha-2 (α2) adrenergic receptors are Gi-coupled receptors. When activated by epinephrine or norepinephrine during stress, they lower cAMP in beta cells, reduce Ca2+ channel activity, and directly inhibit insulin exocytosis. This is the physiologic rationale for why stress/hypoglycemia suppresses insulin — the body needs to preserve glucose for the brain. Conversely, beta-2 (β2) stimulation weakly promotes insulin secretion. Acetylcholine (parasympathetic, via muscarinic M3 receptors on beta cells) stimulates insulin secretion via Gq-PLC-Ca2+ — the opposite of inhibition. Somatostatin (from delta cells), leptin, and fasting are the major inhibitors. Guyton's Table 79.1 lists glucagon among the factors that increase insulin secretion, so glucagon does not inhibit beta cells. Elevated FFAs acutely stimulate insulin secretion (they increase ATP), not inhibit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Table of insulin secretion regulators — Increase: glucose, amino acids (arginine/lysine), GLP-1, GIP, glucagon, GH, cortisol, parasympathetic, beta-2 adrenergic, sulfonylureas; Decrease: somatostatin, alpha-2 adrenergic (epinephrine), leptin, fasting. Classic trap: epinephrine stimulates glucagon (beta-2 in alpha cells) while simultaneously inhibiting insulin (alpha-2 in beta cells). COMLEX: The mnemonic for alpha vs beta adrenergic actions on the pancreas: alpha-2 inhibits insulin (Gi), beta-2 stimulates glucagon — relevant to managing hypoglycemia in beta-blocked patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — Negative and positive feedback regulation of hormone secretion with clinical examples","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Conversely, beta-2 (β2) stimul","E":"Elevated FFAs acutely stimulat"}},{"id":"sdl27_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the cellular mechanism of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman presents with recurrent episodes of diaphoresis, tremor, and confusion occurring during fasting and after exercise. Plasma glucose during an episode is 38 mg/dL. Serum insulin is 48 uU/mL and C-peptide is 4.2 ng/mL (both elevated). A 72-hour fast provokes hypoglycemia with inappropriately elevated insulin and C-peptide. CT abdomen shows a 1.8 cm pancreatic head mass. Which step in the glucose-sensing mechanism is most directly responsible for the relationship between cellular glucose metabolism and the threshold for insulin secretion in normal beta cells?","choices":{"D":"Glucokinase phosphorylates glucose to glucose-6-phosphate as the rate-limiting step in beta cell glucose metabolism, establishing the glucose threshold above which ATP rises sufficiently to close K-ATP channels and trigger secretion.","A":"GLUT2 transport of glucose into beta cells is the rate-limiting step that determines the glucose threshold for secretion because its low affinity prevents glucose entry until blood levels exceed 100 mg/dL.","B":"Voltage-gated calcium channel opening is the rate-limiting step for insulin secretion because L-type channels only open above a specific membrane voltage threshold regardless of glucose concentration.","C":"ATP-sensitive potassium channel closure is the rate-limiting step for secretion because basal channel conductance determines the minimum glucose concentration needed to depolarize the beta cell membrane.","E":"Insulin granule docking to the plasma membrane via SNARE proteins is the rate-limiting step because newly synthesized insulin must be packaged and docked before any glucose stimulus can trigger exocytosis."},"explanation":"This patient has an insulinoma — an autonomous insulin-secreting beta cell tumor — confirmed by elevated insulin and C-peptide during hypoglycemia (ruling out exogenous insulin use, which suppresses C-peptide). In normal beta cells, glucokinase (hexokinase IV) is the glucose sensor and rate-limiting enzyme: it has a high Km for glucose (about 5–6 mM in Marks Ch 9; ~10 mM in Marks Ch 44) and is not inhibited by its product glucose-6-phosphate, making its activity directly proportional to glucose concentration across the physiological range. This property defines the glucose threshold for ATP generation and K-ATP channel closure. Insulinoma cells bypass this normal glucose-sensing threshold. GLUT2 is fast and not rate-limiting. The other steps (Ca2+ channels, K-ATP channels, granule docking) occur downstream of the metabolic sensing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Insulinoma = endogenous hyperinsulinism. Key lab: high insulin + high C-peptide during hypoglycemia. Exogenous insulin abuse = high insulin + low C-peptide. Sulfonylurea abuse = high insulin + high C-peptide + positive sulfonylurea screen. Glucokinase is also called the glucose sensor enzyme and is the target of activating mutations causing congenital hyperinsulinism and inactivating mutations causing MODY type 2. COMLEX: Whipple triad for insulinoma (FA): low blood glucose, symptoms of hypoglycemia, and resolution of symptoms after glucose normalizes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — G protein-coupled receptor signaling and cAMP second messenger amplify calcium-mediated exocytosis in the beta cell","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"GLUT2 is fast and not rate-lim","B":"The other steps (Ca2+ channels","C":"The other steps (Ca2+ channels"}},{"id":"sdl27_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the cellular mechanism of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus is started on a new drug that blocks ATP-sensitive potassium channels in pancreatic beta cells. His fasting glucose is 110 mg/dL. Two hours after taking the drug before breakfast, he becomes confused, diaphoretic, and unresponsive. His plasma glucose is 32 mg/dL. What is the most direct explanation for why this drug class carries a hypoglycemia risk independent of the current blood glucose concentration?","choices":{"C":"K-ATP channel blockade causes membrane depolarization and calcium influx regardless of intracellular ATP levels, stimulating insulin secretion even when blood glucose is low or falling.","A":"K-ATP channel blockade prevents glucose sensing by inhibiting glucokinase activity, causing the beta cell to secrete insulin at a fixed basal rate that cannot be adjusted to match blood glucose levels.","B":"K-ATP channel blockade activates voltage-gated calcium channels in a sustained manner, but hypoglycemia results from the drug causing simultaneous inhibition of glucagon secretion from alpha cells.","D":"K-ATP channel blockade causes glucose-independent insulin secretion by activating phospholipase C, generating IP3 that releases calcium from the endoplasmic reticulum to trigger exocytosis continuously.","E":"K-ATP channel blockade raises intracellular ATP by preventing potassium-coupled glucose export, trapping glucose in the beta cell and forcing maximal insulin secretion regardless of plasma glucose."},"explanation":"Sulfonylureas (and other K-ATP blockers) act independently of blood glucose concentration. Normally, K-ATP channels only close when glucose is high and ATP rises — this is the physiological coupling between blood glucose and insulin secretion. When a drug blocks K-ATP channels pharmacologically, the membrane depolarizes and insulin is secreted regardless of the ATP:ADP ratio, meaning regardless of blood glucose. If the patient skips a meal, has late food, or exercises, blood glucose falls but insulin secretion continues — causing hypoglycemia. This is the fundamental mechanism of sulfonylurea-induced hypoglycemia and why it can be severe. The risk is particularly high in elderly patients and those with renal impairment. Sulfonylureas block K-ATP channels directly and do not inhibit glucokinase; their insulin release is glucose-independent, which is what causes the hypoglycemia. Hypoglycemia results from continued glucose-independent insulin secretion, not from the drug inhibiting glucagon secretion. K-ATP blockade depolarizes the membrane and opens voltage-gated calcium channels; activation of phospholipase C and IP3 is not the mechanism. K-ATP blockade acts independently of ATP and does not raise intracellular ATP or trap glucose; the K-ATP channel is not a glucose exporter.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sulfonylurea hypoglycemia is most dangerous in elderly patients and those with renal dysfunction (impaired drug clearance). Treatment of acute sulfonylurea hypoglycemia: IV dextrose; if persistent or severe, IV octreotide (somatostatin analog that inhibits insulin secretion directly). Diazoxide (K-ATP channel opener) can also be used. COMLEX: Sulfonylureas are contraindicated in patients with significant renal impairment due to accumulation of active metabolites; glipizide is preferred in renal disease due to inactive metabolites.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — G protein-coupled receptor signaling and cAMP second messenger amplify calcium-mediated exocytosis in the beta cell","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Sulfonylureas block K-ATP channels directly and do not inhib","B":"Hypoglycemia results from continued glucose-independent insu","D":"K-ATP blockade depolarizes the membrane and opens voltage-ga","E":"K-ATP blockade acts independently of ATP and does not raise "}},{"id":"sdl27_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the cellular mechanism of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes receives 10 units of regular insulin subcutaneously before dinner. Thirty minutes later she develops palpitations, diaphoresis, and her plasma glucose is 42 mg/dL. A blood sample from this episode shows insulin of 210 uU/mL (markedly elevated) and C-peptide of less than 0.1 ng/mL (undetectable). Which statement best explains the significance of the undetectable C-peptide in interpreting this insulin level?","choices":{"A":"C-peptide is absent because exogenous insulin lacks it; proinsulin is cleaved into insulin and C-peptide only within beta cell granules, so detectable C-peptide confirms endogenous secretion.","B":"C-peptide is absent because exogenous insulin suppresses all remaining endogenous beta cell secretion via negative feedback on pancreatic beta cells, inhibiting any residual proinsulin cleavage.","C":"C-peptide is absent because C-peptide is rapidly degraded in the bloodstream within 30 minutes of insulin secretion and cannot be detected when blood was drawn 30 minutes after the hypoglycemic event.","D":"C-peptide is absent because in type 1 diabetes the immune destruction of beta cells eliminates the enzyme that cleaves proinsulin, causing only full-length proinsulin to be secreted without C-peptide.","E":"C-peptide is absent because recombinant insulin preparations contain C-peptide inhibitors that prevent measurement of endogenous C-peptide in patients receiving insulin therapy."},"explanation":"Proinsulin (A chain + B chain + C peptide) is synthesized in the rough ER and cleaved in the Golgi apparatus and secretory granules to produce insulin and C-peptide in equimolar amounts. Both are co-secreted by exocytosis. Commercially manufactured (exogenous) insulin is purified and contains no C-peptide. Therefore: high insulin + low/absent C-peptide = exogenous insulin administration. High insulin + high C-peptide = endogenous source (insulinoma, sulfonylurea use, or normally functioning beta cells). C-peptide is cleared more slowly than insulin, whose plasma half-life is only about 6 minutes (Guyton Ch 79) or 3–5 minutes (Katzung Ch 41), making C-peptide a better marker of beta cell secretory activity. In type 1 DM, C-peptide is low because beta cells are destroyed, not because of enzyme loss. Exogenous insulin contains no C-peptide, so high insulin with undetectable C-peptide marks injected insulin rather than suppressed endogenous release. Commercial insulin contains no C-peptide, and C-peptide inhibitors are not part of its formulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: C-peptide is equimolar with insulin in endogenous secretion. High insulin + low C-peptide = exogenous insulin or insulin antibodies. High insulin + high C-peptide = insulinoma, sulfonylurea, type 2 DM. Low insulin + low C-peptide = type 1 DM or late type 2 DM. C-peptide itself has physiologic effects via Na/K-ATPase and eNOS. COMLEX: Factitious hypoglycemia from self-administration of insulin is a diagnostic challenge; C-peptide measurement is the key discriminating test.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — G protein-coupled receptor signaling and cAMP second messenger amplify calcium-mediated exocytosis in the beta cell","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Exogenous insulin contains no C-peptide, so high insulin wit","C":"C-peptide is cleared more slow","D":"In type 1 DM, C-peptide is low","E":"Commercial insulin contains no C-peptide, and C-peptide inhi"}},{"id":"sdl27_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the insulin receptor structure and signal transduction pathway.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher adds insulin to cultured skeletal muscle cells and records the responses over time. Within seconds, glucose uptake rises sharply. Over the next 10 to 15 minutes, the activity of many intracellular metabolic enzymes changes. Still other effects keep developing for hours and even days. Which process accounts for these slowest effects?","choices":{"B":"Changed rates of mRNA translation and DNA transcription, which form new proteins and remodel the cell's enzymatic machinery","A":"Translocation of GLUT4-containing vesicles to the plasma membrane, which keeps raising glucose uptake as more vesicles fuse each day","C":"Changed phosphorylation of existing enzymes, which builds up slowly because each step takes several hours","D":"Movement of insulin-receptor complexes into the nucleus, where the receptor alpha subunit acts directly as a transcription factor","E":"Release of cAMP from the activated insulin receptor, which accumulates slowly and activates protein kinase A over several days"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 79) describes three phases of insulin action after it binds its receptor. Within seconds, the membranes of about 80 percent of the body's cells, especially muscle and adipose cells, markedly increase glucose uptake as vesicles carrying glucose transporters move to the cell membrane; they withdraw within 3 to 5 minutes when insulin is gone. Slower effects over the next 10 to 15 minutes change the activity of many more enzymes, mainly by changing their phosphorylation. Much slower effects continue for hours and even several days and result from changed rates of mRNA translation at the ribosomes and changed rates of DNA transcription, by which insulin remolds the cell's enzymatic machinery. GLUT4 translocation is the seconds-scale effect, and enzyme phosphorylation is the minutes-scale effect. The insulin receptor alpha subunits lie outside the cell and do not act as transcription factors. The insulin receptor is a tyrosine kinase receptor and does not generate cAMP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Insulin receptor = enzyme-linked receptor; insulin binds the alpha subunits, the beta subunits autophosphorylate, and the tyrosine kinase phosphorylates IRS proteins. Time course: seconds (glucose uptake; also amino acids, K+, phosphate), 10 to 15 minutes (enzyme phosphorylation), hours to days (translation and transcription) (Guyton Ch 79). COMLEX: Insulin's plasma half-life is about 6 minutes, and it is cleared mainly by insulinase in the liver (Guyton Ch 79).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — Tyrosine kinase receptor: ligand binding, receptor dimerization, and autophosphorylation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"GLUT4 translocation is the sec"}},{"id":"sdl27_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the insulin receptor structure and signal transduction pathway.","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old woman presents with severe acanthosis nigricans, markedly elevated fasting insulin of 850 uU/mL, and fasting glucose of 310 mg/dL. She has no diabetes in her family. Laboratory evaluation reveals antibodies directed against the extracellular domain of the insulin receptor. Which mechanism best explains how these antibodies cause hyperglycemia?","choices":{"E":"Antibodies bind and block the alpha subunit extracellular domain, preventing insulin binding and the conformational change needed for beta subunit transautophosphorylation and downstream signaling.","A":"Antibodies bind and activate the beta subunit tyrosine kinase domain constitutively, mimicking insulin signaling and causing paradoxical hypoglycemia rather than hyperglycemia in affected patients.","B":"Antibodies bind insulin itself in the circulation, forming large immune complexes that prevent insulin from reaching its receptor on target cells and rapidly clearing insulin from the plasma.","C":"Antibodies activate complement-mediated destruction of muscle and adipose cells, eliminating GLUT4-expressing tissue and reducing overall glucose disposal capacity independent of receptor signaling.","D":"Antibodies cross-react with GLUT4 intracellular vesicles in muscle cells, preventing vesicle translocation to the plasma membrane even when the insulin receptor is fully activated by insulin."},"explanation":"Type B insulin resistance is caused by autoantibodies against the insulin receptor alpha subunit. These antibodies physically block insulin binding — because insulin must bind the alpha subunit to induce the conformational change that causes beta subunit transautophosphorylation. Without insulin binding, the kinase domain is not activated, IRS proteins are not phosphorylated, and GLUT4 is not translocated. This causes severe insulin resistance and hyperglycemia despite very high endogenous insulin levels (compensatory hypersecretion). Acanthosis nigricans is a skin manifestation of chronic insulin resistance/hyperinsulinemia. Some anti-receptor antibodies can paradoxically act as partial agonists, occasionally causing hypoglycemia, but blockade is the dominant effect in type B insulin resistance. These autoantibodies are directed at the insulin receptor, not at circulating insulin. The antibodies block insulin binding to the receptor alpha subunit; complement destruction of muscle and adipose cells is not the mechanism. The antibodies target the extracellular alpha subunit and block insulin binding, so the block occurs before GLUT4 vesicle trafficking, not by antibodies to intracellular GLUT4 vesicles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type A insulin resistance = mutation in insulin receptor gene (genetic, more common). Type B insulin resistance = anti-insulin receptor antibodies (autoimmune, associated with other autoimmune conditions like SLE). Both present with acanthosis nigricans, hyperinsulinemia, and hyperglycemia. COMLEX: Acanthosis nigricans at skin folds (axilla, posterior neck, groin) is a physical examination finding indicating insulin resistance; it is reversible with glycemic control and weight loss.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — Tyrosine kinase receptor: ligand binding, receptor dimerization, and autophosphorylation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"B":"These autoantibodies are directed at the insulin receptor, n","C":"The antibodies block insulin binding to the receptor alpha s","D":"The antibodies target the extracellular alpha subunit and bl"}},{"id":"sdl27_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the insulin receptor structure and signal transduction pathway.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with a 12-year history of type 2 diabetes mellitus has fasting glucose of 220 mg/dL and HbA1c of 9.8% despite a maximally tolerated metformin dose. Fasting insulin is 48 uU/mL (significantly elevated). Adipose tissue biopsy shows abundant insulin receptors with normal structure. The receptors can bind insulin but exhibit markedly diminished tyrosine kinase activity upon insulin stimulation. Which downstream defect best explains his persistent hyperglycemia despite high insulin levels?","choices":{"D":"Impaired receptor tyrosine kinase activity reduces IRS phosphorylation, PI3K activation, and Akt-mediated GLUT4 translocation, so muscle and adipose glucose uptake stays inadequate despite high insulin.","A":"Impaired receptor tyrosine kinase activity reduces glucokinase gene transcription in pancreatic beta cells, lowering glucose-sensing capacity and causing secondary beta cell dysfunction with reduced insulin secretion.","B":"Impaired receptor tyrosine kinase activity activates glucagon secretion from alpha cells by removing insulin-mediated paracrine inhibition, causing glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis that overwhelm peripheral glucose disposal.","C":"Impaired receptor tyrosine kinase activity prevents GLUT2 translocation to hepatocyte membranes, eliminating first-pass hepatic glucose uptake after meals and causing postprandial hyperglycemia predominance.","E":"Impaired receptor tyrosine kinase activity opens K-ATP channels in beta cells by lowering intracellular ATP levels, causing paradoxical hyperglycemia from the resulting impairment of insulin secretion."},"explanation":"The insulin receptor in type 2 DM often shows downregulation and reduced intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity due to receptor desensitization from chronic hyperinsulinemia and lipotoxicity. When the beta subunit kinase is underactive, it cannot efficiently phosphorylate IRS-1, preventing PI3K recruitment, PIP3 generation, and Akt activation. Without Akt activity, AS160 cannot be phosphorylated to allow GLUT4 vesicle movement, so GLUT4 remains trapped intracellularly. Despite abundant circulating insulin, glucose uptake in muscle and adipose is impaired — the hallmark of peripheral insulin resistance. This explains why very high insulin levels (hyperinsulinism) coexist with hyperglycemia in type 2 DM. Reduced receptor kinase activity impairs IRS phosphorylation and glucose transporter translocation in muscle and fat, not glucokinase transcription in beta cells. Reduced receptor kinase activity impairs IRS phosphorylation and glucose transporter translocation in muscle and fat; glucagon overwhelming glucose disposal is not the mechanism. GLUT2 is not translocated by insulin; the insulin-regulated transporter is GLUT4 in muscle and adipose tissue. Impaired receptor kinase in peripheral tissues does not open beta cell K-ATP channels; insulin levels are high in this patient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In type 2 DM, insulin resistance has two main molecular mediators: (1) serine phosphorylation of IRS-1 by inflammatory kinases, and (2) receptor downregulation from chronic hyperinsulinemia. GLUT4 translocation defect = inability to dispose of glucose in the periphery. Thiazolidinediones (pioglitazone) improve insulin sensitivity by activating PPAR-gamma, which increases GLUT4 expression and reduces serine phosphorylation of IRS-1. COMLEX: Weight loss, exercise, and metformin all improve insulin receptor kinase activity and IRS function — mechanistic basis for lifestyle interventions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — Tyrosine kinase receptor: ligand binding, receptor dimerization, and autophosphorylation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Reduced receptor kinase activity impairs IRS phosphorylation and glucose transporter translocation in muscle and fat, no","B":"Reduced receptor kinase activity impairs IRS phosphorylation and glucose transporter translocation in muscle and fat; gl","C":"GLUT2 is not translocated by insulin; the insulin-regulated ","E":"Impaired receptor kinase in peripheral tissues does not open"}},{"id":"sdl27_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify factors that increase and decrease insulin secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with a history of well-controlled type 2 diabetes presents with new-onset hyperglycemia, hypochlorhydria, and cholelithiasis. Fasting glucose is 195 mg/dL despite unchanged medications. CT abdomen shows a 2.4 cm pancreatic tail mass. Serum somatostatin is markedly elevated. Which mechanism most directly explains the new hyperglycemia in this patient?","choices":{"E":"Excess somatostatin inhibits insulin secretion from beta cells by reducing cAMP and blocking calcium-mediated exocytosis, causing net hyperglycemia because loss of insulin outweighs the simultaneous suppression of glucagon.","A":"Excess somatostatin stimulates glucagon secretion from alpha cells, which activates hepatic phosphorylase b kinase and causes glycogenolysis that overwhelms the available insulin in peripheral tissues.","B":"Excess somatostatin directly activates alpha-2 adrenergic receptors on pancreatic beta cells, reducing intracellular cAMP and opening K-ATP channels to allow K+ efflux and membrane hyperpolarization.","C":"Excess somatostatin crosses the blood-brain barrier to stimulate POMC neurons in the hypothalamus, activating glucocorticoid release that causes systemic insulin resistance and secondary hyperglycemia.","D":"Excess somatostatin blocks GLUT2 transporters in pancreatic beta cells, preventing glucose sensing and eliminating glucose-stimulated insulin secretion while leaving basal insulin secretion unaffected."},"explanation":"This patient has a somatostatinoma — a rare pancreatic delta cell tumor. Somatostatin inhibits virtually all gastrointestinal hormones and pancreatic secretion. Regarding glucose metabolism: somatostatin inhibits both insulin (from beta cells) and glucagon (from alpha cells). However, the net effect is hyperglycemia because insulin deficiency is the dominant glycemic effect. Without insulin, glucose cannot enter muscle and adipose efficiently, and hepatic glucose output continues unopposed. The classic somatostatinoma triad is hyperglycemia, hypochlorhydria (suppression of gastric acid via inhibition of gastrin), and cholelithiasis (somatostatin inhibits gallbladder contraction via CCK inhibition). Somatostatin acts via Gi-coupled receptors on beta cells, reducing cAMP and K+ channel activity. Somatostatin acts locally in the islets to depress insulin secretion; it is not an alpha-2 adrenergic agonist. Hyperglycemia in somatostatinoma results from somatostatin directly inhibiting insulin secretion, not from stimulation of hypothalamic POMC neurons and glucocorticoid release. Somatostatin depresses insulin secretion by acting locally in the islets, not by blocking GLUT2 while sparing basal secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Somatostatinoma triad = hyperglycemia + hypochlorhydria + cholelithiasis. Somatostatin inhibits GH, TSH, insulin, glucagon, gastrin, secretin, and CCK. Octreotide (somatostatin analog) is used therapeutically to suppress these same hormones in conditions like acromegaly, carcinoid syndrome, and bleeding esophageal varices. COMLEX: Somatostatin from delta cells acts in a paracrine manner on adjacent alpha and beta cells — an important example of islet cell-to-cell communication.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — Negative and positive feedback regulation of hormone secretion with clinical examples","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Regarding glucose metabolism: ","B":"Somatostatin acts locally in the islets to depress insulin s","C":"Hyperglycemia in somatostatinoma results from somatostatin d","D":"Somatostatin depresses insulin secretion by acting locally i"}},{"id":"sdl27_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify factors that increase and decrease insulin secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman with Cushing syndrome caused by a pituitary corticotroph adenoma develops a fasting glucose of 148 mg/dL and a 2-hour post-glucose of 238 mg/dL. Her fasting insulin is 64 uU/mL (elevated). She is told she has steroid-induced diabetes. Which mechanism best explains how chronic glucocorticoid excess progressively impairs both insulin action and insulin secretion?","choices":{"D":"Chronic cortisol excess causes peripheral insulin resistance requiring compensatory hyperinsulinemia; sustained beta cell hypersecretion eventually leads to beta cell exhaustion and impaired glucose-stimulated insulin secretion.","A":"Chronic cortisol excess directly binds and occupies K-ATP channels in pancreatic beta cells, preventing channel closure in response to glucose and causing a permanent reduction in insulin secretory capacity.","B":"Chronic cortisol excess stimulates somatostatin secretion from delta cells, which then acts in a paracrine manner to progressively downregulate insulin receptor expression on adjacent beta cells.","C":"Chronic cortisol excess activates glucokinase inhibitory proteins in pancreatic beta cells, raising the glucose threshold for insulin secretion above 200 mg/dL and causing relative insulin deficiency.","E":"Chronic cortisol excess activates insulin receptor tyrosine kinase in adipose tissue, redirecting intracellular signaling away from GLUT4 translocation and toward triglyceride synthesis and storage."},"explanation":"Glucocorticoids (cortisol) cause insulin resistance through multiple mechanisms: increased gluconeogenesis in the liver, decreased glucose uptake in muscle (downregulation of GLUT4), increased lipolysis releasing FFAs that impair insulin signaling, and direct serine phosphorylation of IRS-1. This insulin resistance initially triggers compensatory insulin hypersecretion. With prolonged cortisol excess, the beta cells face sustained demand and accumulate lipotoxicity and glucotoxicity, eventually developing functional exhaustion (decreased GSIS), reduced beta cell mass, and overt diabetes mellitus. This explains the progression from hyperinsulinemia to frank diabetes in patients with Cushing syndrome or on chronic corticosteroids. Growth hormone and cortisol acutely stimulate insulin secretion but cause resistance chronically. Cortisol does not occupy K-ATP channels, induce glucokinase inhibitors, act through somatostatin on insulin receptors, or activate the insulin receptor kinase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Steroid-induced DM is the most common form of secondary diabetes. Mechanism: insulin resistance (peripheral) + eventual beta cell exhaustion. Hyperglycemia is predominantly postprandial in early stages. Treatment often requires insulin when glucose control is inadequate on oral agents alone. COMLEX: Patients on chronic corticosteroids, including during a taper, require glucose monitoring; steroid-induced DM can be reversible after steroid dose reduction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — Negative and positive feedback regulation of hormone secretion with clinical examples","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Cortisol does not occupy K-ATP","B":"Cortisol does not occupy K-ATP","C":"Cortisol does not occupy K-ATP","E":"Cortisol does not occupy K-ATP"}},{"id":"sdl27_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify factors that increase and decrease insulin secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with a 10-year history of type 2 diabetes mellitus and BMI of 44 undergoes Roux-en-Y gastric bypass surgery. Within 3 days — before any significant weight loss — his fasting glucose normalizes to 96 mg/dL and he is able to discontinue his sulfonylurea and metformin. An oral glucose tolerance test shows an exaggerated early insulin spike at 30 minutes. Which mechanism most directly explains this rapid glycemic improvement?","choices":{"B":"Rapid delivery of nutrients to the distal intestine greatly increases postprandial GLP-1 secretion from L-cells, potentiating glucose-stimulated insulin secretion and suppressing glucagon secretion.","A":"Removal of the gastric fundus eliminates ghrelin-producing cells, reducing appetite and caloric intake so profoundly that blood glucose normalizes within days through caloric restriction alone.","C":"Surgical rerouting bypasses the duodenum and jejunum where GIP-secreting K-cells reside, eliminating the primary incretin signal and forcing the pancreas to upregulate intrinsic glucose-sensing to compensate.","D":"Reduced stomach volume decreases hydrochloric acid production, raising gastric pH and slowing glucose absorption into the bloodstream so that peak postprandial glucose is reduced below the threshold for insulin resistance.","E":"Roux-en-Y anastomosis eliminates bile salt recirculation to the proximal small intestine, activating TGR5 bile acid receptors in the colon that directly stimulate pancreatic beta cell proliferation within 72 hours."},"explanation":"The rapid glycemic remission after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass (before weight loss occurs) is largely explained by the hindgut hypothesis: bypassing the proximal small intestine delivers undigested nutrients to the distal ileum faster and in greater quantities than normal. The distal ileum contains L-cells that secrete GLP-1 in response to nutrient contact. The resulting surge in postprandial GLP-1 amplifies beta cell sensitivity to glucose (via cAMP/PKA), suppresses glucagon (improving hepatic glucose output), and promotes satiety. The early exaggerated insulin spike (hyperinsulinemic response to oral glucose) seen post-bypass is largely GLP-1-mediated. This incretin mechanism accounts for diabetes remission well before weight loss contributes. Marks' Basic Medical Biochemistry links the resolution of diabetes shortly after gastric bypass, before significant weight loss, to a rapid and sustained rise in GLP-1 and amylin, although Guyton notes that the full set of contributing factors is still unclear. Note: eliminating GIP (foregut) may also play a role per the foregut hypothesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bariatric surgery achieves DM remission via two main mechanisms: (1) hindgut — increased GLP-1 from rapid nutrient delivery to L-cells; (2) reduced caloric intake/weight loss improving insulin sensitivity. The exaggerated insulin spike post-bypass can cause postprandial hypoglycemia in some patients. GLP-1 receptor agonists (semaglutide, liraglutide) mimic this mechanism pharmacologically and cause comparable glycemic benefits with weight loss. COMLEX: Bariatric surgery is the most effective long-term intervention for obesity-related type 2 DM; osteopathic principles of holistic treatment support surgical consultation for severe obesity with DM.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — Negative and positive feedback regulation of hormone secretion with clinical examples","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B"}],"28":[{"id":"sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain insulin's effects on carbohydrate metabolism in liver, muscle, and adipose tissue.","batch":1,"stem":"During much of the day, between meals, resting skeletal muscle obtains most of its energy from fatty acids rather than glucose. Which best explains this fuel preference?","choices":{"C":"Resting muscle membrane is barely permeable to glucose without insulin, and between meals too little insulin is secreted to change this","A":"Resting muscle lacks hexokinase between meals, so glucose that enters cannot be phosphorylated and diffuses back out into the extracellular fluid","B":"Glucagon acts on muscle glucagon receptors between meals, which blocks GLUT4 and switches resting muscle metabolism to fatty acid oxidation","D":"Muscle glycogen is released into the blood as free glucose between meals, leaving none for the muscle's own needs","E":"Fatty acids enter muscle through insulin-stimulated transporters, and between meals insulin is high enough to favor fatty acids over glucose"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 79) explains that during much of the day muscle depends mainly on fatty acids for energy because the normal resting muscle membrane is only slightly permeable to glucose except when stimulated by insulin, and between meals the amount of insulin secreted is too small to promote significant glucose entry. Muscle uses large amounts of glucose under two conditions: moderate or heavy exercise, when contraction moves GLUT4 to the membrane without large amounts of insulin, and the few hours after a meal, when high insulin makes muscle use glucose preferentially. Insulin can raise glucose transport into resting muscle at least 15-fold. The explanation is membrane permeability, not a lack of hexokinase. Glucagon acts mainly on the liver. Muscle stores glycogen for its own later use rather than releasing glucose to the blood. Insulin promotes glucose use and spares fat, so it does not favor fatty acids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLUT4 in muscle and fat is insulin-responsive; resting muscle burns fatty acids between meals and switches to glucose after a meal or during exercise, when contraction moves GLUT4 to the membrane (Guyton Ch 79). Muscle glycogen, up to 2 to 3 percent, is used by the muscle itself. COMLEX: Exercise raises muscle glucose uptake with little insulin, one reason physical activity lowers glucose in diabetes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — insulin receptor tyrosine kinase activates PI3K-Akt to translocate GLUT4 in muscle and adipose tissue","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl28_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain insulin's effects on carbohydrate metabolism in liver, muscle, and adipose tissue.","batch":1,"stem":"Upon entry of glucose into hepatocytes after a meal, which enzyme phosphorylates glucose to glucose-6-phosphate to trap it intracellularly for glycogen synthesis, and what kinetic property makes this enzyme an effective hepatic glucose sensor?","choices":{"A":"Glucokinase (hexokinase IV) phosphorylates hepatic glucose to glucose-6-phosphate; its high Km means its activity rises proportionally with postprandial glucose, and it is not inhibited by its product, making it an ideal sensor for hepatic glucose capture.","B":"Hexokinase I phosphorylates hepatic glucose to glucose-6-phosphate; because it has a very low Km for glucose, its activity is nearly saturated at fasting glucose levels and it continues to trap glucose even during prolonged hypoglycemia, providing a constitutive glucose sink.","C":"Phosphofructokinase-1 phosphorylates hepatic glucose to glucose-6-phosphate as the first committed step of glycolysis; insulin activates this enzyme by increasing fructose-2,6-bisphosphate levels, diverting glucose toward glycolytic energy production rather than glycogen storage.","D":"Glycogen synthase phosphorylates glucose to glucose-6-phosphate at the hepatocyte plasma membrane immediately after GLUT2-mediated entry, directly channeling incoming glucose into glycogen polymer chains without an intermediate soluble pool.","E":"Glucose-6-phosphatase phosphorylates glucose to glucose-6-phosphate in the endoplasmic reticulum lumen of hepatocytes, and insulin increases its activity to retain glucose within the ER for subsequent glycogen synthesis in the cytoplasm."},"explanation":"In the liver, glucokinase (hexokinase IV) is the glucose-trapping enzyme activated by insulin. Its key kinetic properties: (1) High Km (about 5–6 mM in Marks Ch 9; ~10 mM in Marks Ch 44) — not saturated at fasting glucose, so activity increases post-meal as portal glucose rises; (2) Sigmoidal kinetics — cooperative behavior at physiological glucose range; (3) Not product-inhibited by glucose-6-phosphate — unlike hexokinase I-III. Insulin induces glucokinase gene transcription and promotes its translocation from nucleus to cytoplasm. This traps incoming portal glucose as G-6-P, which then feeds glycogen synthesis (via glycogen synthase, also activated by insulin) or glycolysis. Hexokinase I has a low Km (approximately 0.05 mM, Marks Ch 9) and is saturated at fasting glucose — not a glucose sensor. Phosphofructokinase-1 converts fructose-6-phosphate to fructose-1,6-bisphosphate, not glucose to glucose-6-phosphate; in the liver glucokinase phosphorylates glucose. Glycogen synthase adds glucosyl residues from UDP-glucose to existing glycogen chains and does not phosphorylate glucose; glucokinase does. Glucose-6-phosphatase splits the phosphate off glucose so free glucose can leave the liver, the opposite reaction, and insulin inhibits rather than activates it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucokinase mutations cause disease: activating mutations cause congenital hyperinsulinism (too much trapping = too much ATP = too much insulin secretion); inactivating mutations cause MODY type 2 (mild persistent hyperglycemia from impaired sensing). Glucokinase is expressed in hepatocytes AND beta cells — it serves as the glucose sensor in both. Insulin inactivates glycogen phosphorylase while activating glycogen synthase — a coordinated switch from glycogenolysis to glycogen synthesis. COMLEX: In the fed state, insulin promotes 4 hepatic actions: glycogen synthesis, glycolysis, fatty acid synthesis, protein synthesis — inhibits gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — Insulin receptor tyrosine kinase activates PI3K-Akt to translocate GLUT4 in muscle and adipose tissue","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Hexokinase I has a low Km (app","C":"Phosphofructokinase-1 converts fructose-6-phosphate to fruct","D":"Glycogen synthase adds glucosyl residues from UDP-glucose to","E":"Glucose-6-phosphatase splits the phosphate off glucose so fr"}},{"id":"sdl28_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain insulin's effects on carbohydrate metabolism in liver, muscle, and adipose tissue.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the effect of insulin on hepatic gluconeogenesis, and which enzyme is a key target of insulin-mediated suppression in this pathway?","choices":{"E":"Insulin suppresses hepatic gluconeogenesis by reducing the expression and activity of gluconeogenic enzymes, including PEPCK and fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase, decreasing glucose production from non-carbohydrate precursors.","A":"Insulin activates hepatic PEPCK by phosphorylating it through the cAMP-PKA signaling axis, increasing conversion of oxaloacetate to phosphoenolpyruvate and accelerating gluconeogenesis to replenish glycogen stores after a meal.","B":"Insulin stimulates fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase by removing allosteric inhibition by AMP and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate, thereby increasing gluconeogenic flux in parallel with glycogen synthesis during the postprandial state.","C":"Insulin promotes hepatic gluconeogenesis indirectly by stimulating proteolysis in skeletal muscle, providing circulating alanine and glutamine as gluconeogenic substrates for the liver to maintain blood glucose during fasting.","D":"Insulin has no direct effect on gluconeogenic enzyme expression; it regulates blood glucose exclusively through GLUT4 translocation in peripheral tissues, and gluconeogenesis is controlled solely by glucagon and cortisol."},"explanation":"Insulin is the primary inhibitor of hepatic gluconeogenesis. Mechanistically, insulin activates Akt (via PI3K), which phosphorylates and activates PDE3B to reduce cAMP, and also phosphorylates the FoxO1 transcription factor, excluding it from the nucleus. FoxO1 normally drives transcription of gluconeogenic enzymes (PEPCK, G6Pase). Insulin also reduces PEPCK and fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase mRNA levels. Clinically, this is why elevated insulin (fed state) reduces hepatic glucose output, and why insulin resistance in type 2 DM allows excessive gluconeogenesis that contributes to fasting hyperglycemia. Metformin works partly by reducing hepatic gluconeogenesis via AMPK activation. Insulin decreases the quantities and activities of hepatic gluconeogenic enzymes; cAMP-protein kinase signaling is the glucagon pathway that activates conversion of pyruvate to phosphoenolpyruvate. Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase is inhibited by AMP and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate, and insulin decreases gluconeogenic enzyme activity, so it does not stimulate this enzyme after a meal. Insulin decreases release of amino acids from muscle, limiting gluconeogenic precursors, rather than stimulating muscle proteolysis to feed gluconeogenesis. Insulin acts directly on the liver, decreasing the quantities and activities of gluconeogenic enzymes, so its glucose-lowering effect is not limited to peripheral GLUT4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In type 2 DM, the liver fails to suppress gluconeogenesis in response to insulin — this is a major driver of fasting hyperglycemia. Metformin is first-line partly because it reduces hepatic glucose production. Key gluconeogenic enzymes: PEPCK (rate-limiting), fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase, pyruvate carboxylase, glucose-6-phosphatase. These are upregulated by glucagon (cAMP-PKA-CREB) and cortisol, and downregulated by insulin (Akt-FoxO1). COMLEX: Excessive hepatic gluconeogenesis in poorly controlled diabetes causes persistent fasting hyperglycemia and contributes to the dawn phenomenon (early morning glucose rise).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — Insulin receptor tyrosine kinase activates PI3K-Akt to translocate GLUT4 in muscle and adipose tissue","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Insulin decreases the quantities and activities of hepatic g","B":"Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase is inhibited by AMP and fructose","C":"Insulin decreases release of amino acids from muscle, limiti","D":"Insulin acts directly on the liver, decreasing the quantitie"}},{"id":"sdl28_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain insulin's effects on fat metabolism.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme does insulin inhibit in adipose tissue to prevent lipolysis, and what is the physiological consequence of this inhibition during the fed state?","choices":{"B":"Insulin inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase (HSL) in adipocytes by activating PDE3B (reducing cAMP) and activating PP1 to dephosphorylate and inactivate HSL, preventing the hydrolysis of stored triglycerides and promoting net triglyceride accumulation.","A":"Insulin inhibits lipoprotein lipase (LPL) at the luminal surface of adipose capillaries, preventing hydrolysis of circulating VLDL and chylomicron triglycerides into free fatty acids, thereby reducing the substrate available for adipocyte triglyceride storage.","C":"Insulin inhibits pancreatic lipase in the duodenal lumen, reducing dietary fat absorption and thereby decreasing the flux of chylomicron-derived fatty acids available for adipose triglyceride storage in the postprandial period.","D":"Insulin inhibits acetyl-CoA carboxylase in adipocytes by activating AMPK, preventing malonyl-CoA formation from acetyl-CoA and blocking de novo fatty acid synthesis that would otherwise provide substrate for triglyceride esterification.","E":"Insulin inhibits carnitine palmitoyltransferase I in adipocyte mitochondria by raising malonyl-CoA levels through acetyl-CoA carboxylase activation, preventing entry of fatty acids into the mitochondrial matrix and blocking their beta-oxidation."},"explanation":"Hormone-sensitive lipase (HSL) is the rate-limiting enzyme for adipose triglyceride hydrolysis. In the fasted state, catecholamines raise cAMP via beta-adrenergic Gs-coupled receptors, activating PKA, which phosphorylates and activates HSL. Insulin opposes this by (1) activating PDE3B to degrade cAMP, reducing PKA activity, and (2) activating protein phosphatase 1 (PP1) to dephosphorylate and inactivate HSL. Perilipin, the lipid droplet coat protein, is also dephosphorylated, limiting lipase access to the droplet. Result: triglycerides remain stored. Lipoprotein lipase (LPL) is actually INCREASED by insulin — it cleaves circulating VLDL triglycerides to fatty acids for adipocyte uptake. This is the opposite of what the LPL-inhibition option states. Pancreatic lipase digests dietary triglycerides in the intestinal lumen; the lipase insulin inhibits to stop release of stored fat is hormone-sensitive lipase in fat cells. Insulin activates acetyl-CoA carboxylase to promote fatty acid synthesis, rather than inhibiting it, and the enzyme it inhibits to stop lipolysis is hormone-sensitive lipase. Malonyl-CoA inhibition of carnitine acyltransferase I limits fatty acid oxidation, not lipolysis; the enzyme insulin inhibits to stop adipose lipolysis is hormone-sensitive lipase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Insulin stimulates LPL (increases TG uptake from blood) while inhibiting HSL (prevents TG release from adipose) — net effect is fat storage. In insulin deficiency, HSL runs unchecked: massive lipolysis → elevated FFAs → hepatic ketogenesis → DKA. Classic high-yield: HSL is inhibited by insulin (via reduced cAMP) and activated by glucagon, catecholamines, cortisol, GH. COMLEX: In metabolic syndrome and insulin resistance, elevated basal lipolysis (insufficient HSL inhibition) drives elevated circulating FFAs that worsen hepatic insulin resistance and dyslipidemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — Insulin receptor signaling inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase and promotes GLUT4-mediated glycerol-3-phosphate synthesis in adipocytes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"C":"Pancreatic lipase digests dietary triglycerides in the intes","D":"Insulin activates acetyl-CoA carboxylase to promote fatty ac","E":"Malonyl-CoA inhibition of carnitine acyltransferase I limits"}},{"id":"sdl28_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain insulin's effects on fat metabolism.","batch":1,"stem":"Excess glucose in the liver is converted to fatty acids for packaging and export as VLDL. Which enzyme, upregulated and activated by insulin, catalyzes the committed first step of hepatic de novo lipogenesis, and what is the product of this reaction?","choices":{"D":"Acetyl-CoA carboxylase catalyzes the ATP-dependent carboxylation of acetyl-CoA to malonyl-CoA; insulin activates this enzyme by promoting its dephosphorylation and increasing its expression, supplying malonyl-CoA for chain elongation by fatty acid synthase.","A":"Hormone-sensitive lipase catalyzes the hydrolysis of intracellular triglycerides to diacylglycerol and free fatty acids as the first committed step of lipogenesis; insulin activates this enzyme to provide fatty acid building blocks for de novo synthesis.","B":"HMG-CoA synthase catalyzes the condensation of two acetyl-CoA molecules to form HMG-CoA as the committed first step of hepatic lipogenesis; insulin activates this enzyme, and HMG-CoA is subsequently reduced to mevalonate and then to cholesterol rather than triglycerides.","C":"Fatty acid synthase catalyzes the elongation of malonyl-CoA to form the 16-carbon palmitate using NADPH as the electron donor; although essential for lipogenesis, it operates downstream of the true committed step and requires pre-formed malonyl-CoA as substrate.","E":"Carnitine palmitoyltransferase I catalyzes the transfer of fatty acyl-CoA to carnitine for entry into the mitochondrial matrix; insulin activates this enzyme in hepatocytes to increase beta-oxidation and provide reducing equivalents that drive downstream de novo lipogenesis."},"explanation":"De novo lipogenesis begins when excess glucose-derived acetyl-CoA is converted to malonyl-CoA by acetyl-CoA carboxylase (ACC). This is the committed, regulated first step. Malonyl-CoA then serves as the 2-carbon donor for fatty acid synthase (FAS), which elongates the chain to palmitate (16:0). Insulin activates ACC by (1) dephosphorylation (via PP2A, opposing AMPK-mediated inactivation), (2) increased gene transcription via SREBP-1c. Notably, malonyl-CoA also inhibits carnitine palmitoyltransferase I (CPT-I), preventing newly synthesized fatty acids from being immediately oxidized — a coordinated switch from oxidation to synthesis. In insulin resistance, excess lipogenesis drives NAFLD and VLDL overproduction. Hormone-sensitive lipase breaks down stored triglycerides and is inhibited, not activated, by insulin; the committed lipogenic step is acetyl-CoA carboxylase. HMG-CoA synthase is the rate-determining enzyme of ketogenesis, not the first step of fatty acid synthesis, which is acetyl-CoA carboxylase forming malonyl-CoA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pathway: Glucose → pyruvate → acetyl-CoA → malonyl-CoA (ACC) → palmitate (FAS) → VLDL → peripheral tissues. Insulin activates ACC; glucagon and AMPK inhibit ACC (phosphorylation). Malonyl-CoA inhibits CPT-I, preventing fatty acid oxidation when synthesis is active — elegant reciprocal regulation. Metformin activates AMPK, which inhibits ACC and reduces hepatic lipogenesis. COMLEX: NAFLD is driven by insulin resistance causing increased ACC activity, lipogenesis, and hepatic TG accumulation — directly relevant to metabolic syndrome management in osteopathic practice.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — Insulin receptor signaling inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase and promotes GLUT4-mediated glycerol-3-phosphate synthesis in adipocytes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Hormone-sensitive lipase breaks down stored triglycerides an","B":"HMG-CoA synthase is the rate-determining enzyme of ketogenes","C":"Malonyl-CoA then serves as the","E":"Notably, malonyl-CoA also inhi"}},{"id":"sdl28_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain insulin's effects on fat metabolism.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with new-onset type 1 diabetes presents with nausea, fruity breath, and rapid deep breathing. His arterial pH is 7.11. Which of the following sequences correctly describes the metabolic pathway responsible for the accumulation of ketone bodies in insulin-deficient states?","choices":{"A":"Insulin deficiency removes tonic inhibition of hormone-sensitive lipase, causing massive lipolysis; free fatty acids undergo hepatic beta-oxidation to acetyl-CoA, which forms acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate, causing anion-gap acidosis.","B":"Insulin deficiency accelerates glycogenolysis, releasing glucose-1-phosphate that is converted via glycolytic bypass enzymes to pyruvate and then to ketone bodies through a pyruvate dehydrogenase-independent route in hepatic mitochondria.","C":"Insulin deficiency activates glycogen phosphorylase in adipose tissue, releasing glycerol from stored triglycerides; glycerol is converted to dihydroxyacetone phosphate, which is subsequently metabolized to acetoacetyl-CoA and secreted as ketone bodies.","D":"Insulin deficiency upregulates malonyl-CoA synthesis via acetyl-CoA carboxylase, which paradoxically activates carnitine palmitoyltransferase I to increase fatty acid import into mitochondria, overwhelming the TCA cycle with acetyl-CoA and producing ketone bodies.","E":"Insulin deficiency depletes hepatic oxaloacetate through constitutive gluconeogenesis, which leaves acetyl-CoA from baseline fatty acid oxidation unable to condense with oxaloacetate in the TCA cycle and forces its diversion to ketone body synthesis."},"explanation":"The ketoacidosis sequence: Insulin deficiency → HSL disinhibited → lipolysis → massive FFA release → hepatic beta-oxidation → acetyl-CoA accumulates (TCA cycle cannot keep pace because oxaloacetate is consumed by gluconeogenesis) → acetoacetyl-CoA → acetoacetate + beta-hydroxybutyrate. Both are strong organic acids that consume bicarbonate buffer, causing anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Free fatty acids rise even faster than blood glucose after insulin withdrawal. The brain can partially adapt to use ketones as fuel, but ketones are too acidic in excess. The oxaloacetate-depletion choice is partially correct (OAA consumption by gluconeogenesis contributes), but it omits the initiating event — loss of HSL inhibition and the resulting FFA flood — and mislabels the fatty acid source as baseline oxidation. Insulin deficiency lowers, not raises, malonyl-CoA, and ketones do not come from glycogen or glycerol.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DKA triad — hyperglycemia + anion-gap metabolic acidosis + ketonemia/ketonuria. FFAs are the primary substrate for ketogenesis, not amino acids. Insulin is ketosis-preventive — even small amounts suppress HSL and ketogenesis. DKA occurs in T1DM (absolute insulin deficiency); HHS (hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state) occurs in T2DM where residual insulin prevents significant ketogenesis. Beta-hydroxybutyrate (reduced form) predominates in severe DKA and is NOT detected by nitroprusside-based ketone tests (which only detect acetoacetate). COMLEX: DKA treatment = fluids, insulin, potassium replacement (insulin shifts K+ into cells, causing hypokalemia).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — Insulin receptor signaling inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase and promotes GLUT4-mediated glycerol-3-phosphate synthesis in adipocytes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Insulin deficiency lowers, not","C":"Insulin deficiency lowers, not","D":"Insulin deficiency lowers, not"}},{"id":"sdl28_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain insulin's effects on protein metabolism.","batch":1,"stem":"Insulin promotes net protein synthesis partly by stimulating cellular uptake of specific amino acids. Which of the following groups of amino acids is preferentially transported into cells in response to insulin?","choices":{"C":"Branched-chain amino acids including valine, leucine, and isoleucine, as well as aromatic amino acids including tyrosine and phenylalanine, are among the amino acids preferentially transported into cells in response to insulin signaling.","A":"Negatively charged amino acids including glutamate and aspartate are preferentially transported into skeletal muscle cells by insulin-sensitive carriers, where they serve primarily as substrates for the malate-aspartate shuttle and support oxidative phosphorylation rather than protein synthesis.","B":"Small uncharged amino acids including glycine and serine are the primary substrates transported into skeletal muscle fibers by insulin, where they are incorporated preferentially into collagen and extracellular matrix proteins rather than myofibrillar proteins.","D":"Sulfur-containing amino acids including methionine and cysteine are preferentially transported into hepatocytes in response to insulin to support glutathione synthesis and antioxidant defense, rather than contributing to net protein anabolism.","E":"Positively charged amino acids including arginine and lysine are transported into pancreatic alpha cells in response to insulin as part of a paracrine feedback loop that stimulates glucagon secretion during the postprandial state."},"explanation":"Insulin promotes uptake of specific amino acids, particularly the branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs: valine, leucine, isoleucine) and aromatic amino acids (tyrosine, phenylalanine). These are essential amino acids that must be obtained from the diet and transported into cells. Leucine is particularly important as it also directly activates mTORC1 (a leucine sensor), creating a feed-forward signal for protein synthesis. Importantly, growth hormone promotes uptake of a different set of amino acids (including arginine and some neutral amino acids), which is why insulin and GH act synergistically for growth — each supplies amino acids the other does not. BCAAs are metabolized primarily in muscle (not liver), distinguishing them from most other amino acids. The amino acids insulin most strongly transports into cells are valine, leucine, isoleucine, tyrosine and phenylalanine, not glutamate and aspartate. The amino acids insulin most strongly transports into cells are valine, leucine, isoleucine, tyrosine and phenylalanine, not glycine and serine. The amino acids insulin most strongly transports into cells are valine, leucine, isoleucine, tyrosine and phenylalanine, not methionine and cysteine. Arginine and lysine are the amino acids that most potently stimulate insulin secretion; the ones insulin most strongly transports into cells are valine, leucine, isoleucine, tyrosine and phenylalanine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: BCAAs (Val, Leu, Ile) are the primary amino acids used for muscle protein synthesis and energy during exercise. Leucine is a direct mTORC1 activator — this is why protein supplements enriched with leucine are anabolic. Insulin promotes BCAA uptake; glucagon promotes uptake of alanine and arginine for gluconeogenesis. Classic trap: Hyperaminoacidemia (especially from protein meals) stimulates both insulin AND glucagon — insulin drives amino acid uptake and protein synthesis, while glucagon prevents the hypoglycemia that the insulin rise alone would cause. COMLEX: In renal failure, restricting dietary protein (especially BCAAs) reduces uremic solute production; insulin resistance of uremia impairs normal BCAA utilization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — Growth hormone synergizes with insulin to promote protein synthesis via complementary amino acid uptake profiles in tissues","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"The amino acids insulin most strongly transports into cells are valine, leucine, isoleucine, tyrosine and phenylalanine, not glutamate and a","B":"The amino acids insulin most strongly transports into cells are valine, leucine, isoleucine, tyrosine and phenylalanine, not glycine and ser","D":"The amino acids insulin most strongly transports into cells are valine, leucine, isoleucine, tyrosine and phenylalanine, not methionine and ","E":"Arginine and lysine are the amino acids that most potently s"}},{"id":"sdl28_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain insulin's effects on protein metabolism.","batch":1,"stem":"Through which combination of mechanisms does insulin promote net protein anabolism in skeletal muscle?","choices":{"B":"Insulin stimulates amino acid transport into cells, activates ribosomal mRNA translation via the PI3K-Akt-mTORC1 axis, increases transcription of structural proteins, and inhibits lysosomal proteolysis, coordinately raising synthesis and lowering degradation.","A":"Insulin promotes net protein anabolism by activating the ubiquitin-proteasome pathway via MuRF-1 upregulation, which preferentially degrades oxidized and damaged proteins, freeing amino acid pools for new protein synthesis in the fed state.","C":"Insulin promotes net protein anabolism by enhancing lysosomal cathepsin activity and accelerating autophagosome formation, increasing the rate of protein turnover and providing recycled amino acids that are preferentially reincorporated into new myofibrillar proteins.","D":"Insulin promotes net protein anabolism by activating adenylyl cyclase via a Gs-coupled insulin receptor variant, raising intracellular cAMP levels that phosphorylate and activate translational elongation factors in skeletal muscle ribosomes.","E":"Insulin promotes net protein anabolism solely by lowering blood glucose and reducing non-enzymatic glycosylation of structural proteins, thereby decreasing RAGE-mediated proteolysis; the anabolic effect is entirely indirect and does not involve direct transcriptional or translational activation."},"explanation":"Insulin exerts coordinated anabolic effects on protein metabolism through four mechanisms: (1) Amino acid transport — increases uptake of BCAAs and other essential amino acids into cells; (2) mRNA translation — via PI3K-Akt-mTORC1 signaling, which phosphorylates 4EBP1 (releasing eIF4E for cap-dependent translation initiation) and activates S6K1 (stimulating ribosomal protein synthesis); (3) Transcription — insulin increases transcription of anabolic genes including structural muscle proteins; (4) Anti-proteolytic — insulin inhibits lysosomal cathepsins and suppresses autophagy, reducing protein degradation. The net result in skeletal muscle after a protein-containing meal is positive nitrogen balance. Insulin deficiency leads to net negative nitrogen balance — muscle wasting, weight loss, elevated urine nitrogen. Insulin inhibits protein catabolism, decreasing amino acid release from muscle, rather than activating proteasomal protein degradation. Insulin diminishes the normal degradation of proteins by lysosomes rather than enhancing it. The insulin receptor is an enzyme-linked receptor whose beta subunits autophosphorylate and activate a tyrosine kinase, not a Gs-coupled receptor raising cAMP. Insulin acts directly on protein metabolism, increasing amino acid transport, mRNA translation and gene transcription and inhibiting protein catabolism, not only by lowering glucose.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The mTORC1 pathway is the central integrator of anabolic signals in cells: activated by insulin (via Akt), leucine (direct sensing), and growth factors. Inhibited by AMPK (energy deficit), rapamycin. mTORC1 → 4EBP1 phosphorylation → eIF4E released → cap-dependent translation. mTORC1 → S6K1 → ribosomal biogenesis. In critical illness and starvation, insulin deficiency plus cortisol excess causes catabolism: muscle wasting, gluconeogenesis from amino acids, and negative nitrogen balance. COMLEX: Anabolic steroids and insulin abuse by athletes both work through mTORC1 and protein synthesis pathways; relevant in sports medicine and osteopathic practice.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — Growth hormone synergizes with insulin to promote protein synthesis via complementary amino acid uptake profiles in tissues","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Insulin inhibits protein catabolism, decreasing amino acid r","C":"Insulin diminishes the normal degradation of proteins by lys","D":"The insulin receptor is an enzyme-linked receptor whose beta","E":"Insulin acts directly on protein metabolism, increasing amin"}},{"id":"sdl28_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain insulin's effects on protein metabolism.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with poorly controlled type 1 diabetes develops progressive muscle wasting and a negative nitrogen balance despite adequate caloric intake. Which of the following best explains the biochemical basis for muscle protein loss in insulin-deficient states?","choices":{"D":"Insulin deficiency activates lysosomal proteolysis and removes inhibition of hepatic gluconeogenesis, so amino acids freed from muscle protein are consumed as gluconeogenic substrates, causing protein wasting and negative nitrogen balance.","A":"Hyperglycemia in uncontrolled type 1 diabetes activates the hexosamine pathway in muscle cells, which directly phosphorylates and inactivates Akt, causing ubiquitin ligase MuRF-1 upregulation and proteasomal degradation of myosin heavy chains.","B":"Elevated circulating ketone bodies in poorly controlled type 1 diabetes competitively inhibit leucine-sensitive mTORC1 activation, reducing protein synthesis to near-zero rates and mimicking the fasting state independently of insulin levels.","C":"Insulin deficiency reduces hepatic growth hormone receptor expression, causing secondary reduction in IGF-1 production; without circulating IGF-1, growth plate and muscle cells lack the primary anabolic signal for ribosomal protein synthesis.","E":"Insulin deficiency paradoxically activates mTORC1 in skeletal muscle by removing IRS-1-mediated negative feedback on mTOR, causing hyperactivation of S6K1 that phosphorylates IRS-1 in a way that ultimately inhibits elongation factor eEF2."},"explanation":"In insulin-deficient states, four protein-catabolic processes converge: (1) Lysosomal proteolysis is disinhibited — insulin normally suppresses cathepsin activity and autophagy; (2) mRNA translation is reduced — loss of PI3K-Akt-mTORC1 activation; (3) Amino acid uptake decreases — less insulin-stimulated BCAA transport; (4) Gluconeogenesis runs unchecked — the liver consumes alanine, glutamine, and other amino acids as gluconeogenic substrates. The result is net negative nitrogen balance: more nitrogen is lost in urine (as urea from amino acid catabolism) than is consumed. Clinically this manifests as muscle wasting, polyphagia (the patient is hungry but cannot anabolize nutrients), weight loss, and asthenia. This is why T1DM before insulin discovery was uniformly fatal despite patients eating large amounts. Wasting follows from loss of insulin itself: protein catabolism rises, synthesis stops and released amino acids feed gluconeogenesis, rather than a hyperglycemia-driven hexosamine pathway inactivating Akt. Wasting follows from insulin deficiency itself, as protein storage halts and catabolism rises when insulin is unavailable, not from ketone bodies acting independently of insulin. Protein loss follows directly from absent insulin, which is itself required for protein synthesis, rather than from reduced hepatic growth hormone receptors and IGF-1. Without insulin, mRNA translation stops as the ribosomes stop working, so insulin deficiency reduces rather than paradoxically activates protein synthesis signaling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic T1DM at presentation: polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia PLUS weight loss — the 3 Ps with wasting. Weight loss despite polyphagia = the tissues cannot utilize nutrients without insulin. Urine nitrogen is elevated (amino acid catabolism). Contrast with T2DM presentation: often obese with gradual onset, weight loss less prominent. Nitrogen balance: positive = anabolic (growing, recovering); negative = catabolic (starvation, DM, severe illness, burns). COMLEX: Nitrogen balance = nitrogen intake (dietary protein / 6.25) minus nitrogen output (urine urea nitrogen + insensible losses). Negative nitrogen balance in ICU patients worsens morbidity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — Growth hormone synergizes with insulin to promote protein synthesis via complementary amino acid uptake profiles in tissues","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Wasting follows from loss of insulin itself: protein catabol","B":"Wasting follows from insulin deficiency itself, as protein s","C":"Protein loss follows directly from absent insulin, which is ","E":"Without insulin, mRNA translation stops as the ribosomes sto"}},{"id":"sdl28_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe glucagon secretion, mechanism of action, and metabolic effects.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly identifies the cell of origin, molecular structure, and molecular weight of glucagon?","choices":{"E":"Glucagon is secreted by alpha cells of the islets of Langerhans, is a 29-amino acid polypeptide with a molecular weight of approximately 3485 daltons, and is derived from cleavage of proglucagon.","A":"Glucagon is secreted by beta cells of the islets of Langerhans, is a 51-amino acid two-chain polypeptide linked by two disulfide bonds with a molecular weight of approximately 5808 daltons, and is derived from cleavage of proinsulin.","B":"Glucagon is secreted by delta cells of the islets of Langerhans, is a 14-amino acid cyclic peptide with a disulfide bridge, with a molecular weight of approximately 1640 daltons, and functions to inhibit both insulin and glucagon secretion within the islet.","C":"Glucagon is secreted by acinar cells of the exocrine pancreas, is a 36-amino acid linear peptide with a molecular weight of approximately 4200 daltons, and is co-secreted with digestive enzymes in response to duodenal cholecystokinin.","D":"Glucagon is secreted by F cells (PP cells) of the islets of Langerhans, is a 36-amino acid peptide with a molecular weight of approximately 4240 daltons, and functions to inhibit exocrine pancreatic secretion and gallbladder contraction after meals."},"explanation":"Glucagon is produced by the alpha cells of the islets of Langerhans. It is a single-chain 29-amino acid polypeptide with a molecular weight of 3485 daltons, derived from proglucagon (which also generates GLP-1 and GLP-2 in intestinal L-cells). Alpha cells make up about 25% of islet cells in Guyton Ch 79 (about 20% of islet mass in Katzung Table 41–1). Glucagon is the principal counter-regulatory hormone to insulin. Beta cells make insulin (51 AA, MW 5808). Delta cells make somatostatin (14 AA). F cells (PP cells) make pancreatic polypeptide. All four cell types interact within the islet via paracrine signaling — insulin inhibits glucagon from adjacent alpha cells, somatostatin inhibits both, and so on. Glucagon is made by alpha cells of the islets, is a 29-amino acid peptide of about 3485 daltons, and is not secreted by exocrine acinar cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Islet cell types and their products — Alpha (about 25% in Guyton, 20% in Katzung): glucagon; Beta (about 60% in Guyton, 75% in Katzung): insulin + C-peptide (and amylin); Delta (about 10% in Guyton, 3–5% in Katzung): somatostatin; F/PP cells (rare): pancreatic polypeptide. Proglucagon is differentially processed: in pancreatic alpha cells → glucagon; in intestinal L-cells → GLP-1 + GLP-2 (different cleavage by prohormone convertase 1/3 vs 2). GLP-1 is the target of incretin-based therapies (GLP-1 agonists: semaglutide, liraglutide). COMLEX: Glucagonoma (alpha cell tumor) presents with necrolytic migratory erythema, hyperglycemia, weight loss, and deep vein thrombosis — 4 Ds: Dermatitis, Diabetes, DVT, Depression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — Gs-coupled receptor signaling: glucagon activates adenylyl cyclase to raise cAMP and activate protein kinase A in hepatocytes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"C":"Glucagon is made by alpha cells of the islets, is a 29-amino","D":"F cells (PP cells) make pancre"}},{"id":"sdl28_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe glucagon secretion, mechanism of action, and metabolic effects.","batch":1,"stem":"Glucagon binds its receptor on the hepatocyte surface and initiates a signaling cascade leading to glycogenolysis. Which intracellular pathway is directly activated by glucagon receptor binding, and what is the amplification mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon binds a Gi-coupled receptor, inhibiting adenylyl cyclase and reducing cAMP; the resulting decrease in PKA activity activates protein phosphatase 2A, which dephosphorylates glycogen phosphorylase b to its active a form in an inhibitory release mechanism.","B":"Glucagon binds a Gs-coupled receptor, activating adenylyl cyclase to raise cAMP; PKA phosphorylates phosphorylase b kinase, which converts glycogen phosphorylase b to its active a form, releasing glucose-1-phosphate in a cascade with millionfold amplification.","C":"Glucagon binds a Gq-coupled receptor, activating phospholipase C to generate IP3 and DAG; IP3 releases calcium from the endoplasmic reticulum, activating calmodulin-dependent kinase II, which directly phosphorylates glycogen phosphorylase to initiate glycogenolysis.","D":"Glucagon binds a receptor tyrosine kinase on hepatocytes, autophosphorylating IRS-2 to activate PI3K and Akt; Akt then phosphorylates and inactivates GSK-3, releasing glycogen synthase from inhibition to paradoxically initiate glycogen synthesis.","E":"Glucagon binds a JAK2-associated cytokine receptor on hepatocytes, activating STAT3 to transcribe the glycogen phosphorylase gene; the resulting increase in phosphorylase enzyme mass over 2 to 4 hours accounts for the delayed but sustained glycogenolytic response."},"explanation":"Glucagon acts through a classic Gs-cAMP-PKA cascade: (1) Glucagon binds Gs-coupled receptor → (2) Adenylyl cyclase activated → (3) cAMP rises → (4) PKA activated → (5) Phosphorylase b kinase phosphorylated and activated → (6) Glycogen phosphorylase b converted to active glycogen phosphorylase a → (7) Glycogen → glucose-1-phosphate. The cascade amplifies the signal approximately one millionfold: one glucagon molecule activates multiple adenylyl cyclases, each producing many cAMP molecules, each activating multiple PKA molecules, and so on. PKA simultaneously phosphorylates and inactivates glycogen synthase, reciprocally shutting off glycogen synthesis. This dual regulation (activate phosphorylase, inactivate synthase) maximizes net glycogen breakdown. Glucagon's receptor activates adenylyl cyclase and raises cAMP, which leads to phosphorylase b kinase activation; it does not lower cAMP. Glucagon acts through adenylyl cyclase, cAMP and protein kinase to activate phosphorylase b kinase, not through phospholipase C, IP3 and calcium. Glucagon acts on a G protein-coupled receptor that raises cAMP to drive glycogenolysis; the tyrosine kinase receptor that promotes glycogen storage is insulin's. Glucagon raises blood glucose within minutes through the cAMP cascade, with up to millionfold amplification, not through slow JAK2-STAT3 transcription of phosphorylase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucagon = Gs = cAMP = PKA cascade. Insulin = receptor tyrosine kinase = IRS = PI3K = Akt. These two pathways are mutually antagonistic. PKA also phosphorylates CREB to transcribe gluconeogenic genes (long-term). Classic high-yield: Glucagon activates phosphorylase AND inactivates glycogen synthase simultaneously. Epinephrine does the same in muscle via beta-2/Gs AND alpha-1/Gq (dual signaling). Hepatic glycogen can reach 5% to 6% of liver mass under insulin (Guyton Ch 79) and is mobilized by glucagon. COMLEX: IM or SC glucagon (1 mg) is first-line treatment for severe hypoglycemia when IV access is unavailable; now available as intranasal formulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — Gs-coupled receptor signaling: glucagon activates adenylyl cyclase to raise cAMP and activate protein kinase A in hepatocytes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Glucagon's receptor activates adenylyl cyclase and raises cA","C":"Glucagon acts through adenylyl cyclase, cAMP and protein kin","D":"Glucagon acts on a G protein-coupled receptor that raises cA","E":"Glucagon raises blood glucose within minutes through the cAM"}},{"id":"sdl28_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe glucagon secretion, mechanism of action, and metabolic effects.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following stimulates glucagon secretion from pancreatic alpha cells, and what is the physiological purpose of glucagon release in this context?","choices":{"B":"Amino acids from a protein-rich meal, particularly alanine and arginine, directly stimulate glucagon secretion; this glucagon drives hepatic gluconeogenesis from the same amino acids, preventing hypoglycemia from protein-stimulated insulin.","A":"Hyperglycemia following a high-carbohydrate meal stimulates alpha-cell glucagon secretion via glucose-induced membrane depolarization, and this postprandial glucagon amplifies beta-cell insulin secretion through paracrine cAMP signaling within the islet.","C":"Somatostatin released from delta cells in response to mixed nutrient absorption directly stimulates glucagon secretion by binding a Gs-coupled receptor on alpha cells, creating a positive paracrine feedback loop within the islets of Langerhans.","D":"Elevated portal insulin levels after a meal directly stimulate alpha-cell glucagon secretion via insulin receptor activation on alpha cells, ensuring that increased insulin availability is matched by proportional glucagon to maintain glycemic homeostasis.","E":"GLP-1 released from intestinal L-cells in response to carbohydrate and fat absorption directly stimulates glucagon secretion from alpha cells through the same Gs-cAMP pathway used by glucose to amplify beta-cell insulin secretion."},"explanation":"A protein-rich meal (no carbohydrate) poses a metabolic challenge: amino acids like arginine and alanine stimulate insulin secretion, which would lower blood glucose. However, the same amino acids also stimulate glucagon secretion from alpha cells. The glucagon drives hepatic gluconeogenesis using the dietary amino acids as substrate, maintaining euglycemia. This is an elegant example of coordinated islet regulation: both insulin and glucagon rise after a protein meal — insulin promotes protein anabolism, glucagon prevents hypoglycemia. Somatostatin INHIBITS both insulin and glucagon. High glucose inhibits glucagon (inversely related to blood glucose). GLP-1 stimulates insulin but SUPPRESSES glucagon. Insulin does not stimulate glucagon secretion; high glucose and insulin inhibit glucagon, whereas protein meal amino acids such as alanine and arginine stimulate it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucagon stimulators — hypoglycemia, amino acids (arginine, alanine), exercise (4-5x increase), catecholamines (beta-2 adrenergic), cortisol, CCK. Glucagon inhibitors — hyperglycemia, insulin (paracrine), somatostatin, GLP-1/GIP (incretin effect). Classic exam pearl: After a pure protein meal — BOTH insulin and glucagon rise. After a carbohydrate meal — insulin rises, glucagon falls. After exercise — glucagon rises 4-5 fold to maintain glucose for working muscle. COMLEX: Glucagon is the most important counter-regulatory hormone in the short term; cortisol and GH are more important in prolonged fasting/stress.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — Gs-coupled receptor signaling: glucagon activates adenylyl cyclase to raise cAMP and activate protein kinase A in hepatocytes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"High glucose inhibits glucagon","C":"Somatostatin INHIBITS both ins","D":"Insulin does not stimulate glucagon secretion; high glucose ","E":"GLP-1 stimulates insulin but S"}},{"id":"sdl28_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain insulin's effects on carbohydrate metabolism in liver, muscle, and adipose tissue.","batch":2,"stem":"A 21-year-old man with type 1 diabetes mellitus managed with an insulin pump begins a 60-minute aerobic cycling session. His endocrinologist advised him to reduce his basal infusion rate, but despite this reduction his blood glucose continues to fall throughout the workout. His residual plasma insulin level is near zero. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of ongoing glucose uptake in his skeletal muscle during sustained aerobic exercise in the near absence of insulin?","choices":{"D":"Muscle contraction activates AMP kinase and calcium-calmodulin-dependent kinase II, which independently trigger GLUT4 vesicle translocation to the sarcolemma through an insulin-independent pathway, allowing glucose uptake in proportion to exercise intensity.","A":"Circulating epinephrine released in response to exercise activates beta-2 adrenergic receptors on skeletal muscle fibers, which directly phosphorylate AS160 via a Gs-cAMP-PKA pathway to translocate GLUT4 to the sarcolemma independently of insulin receptor activation.","B":"Hypoglycemia during exercise upregulates GLUT1 expression in skeletal muscle through a hypoxia-inducible factor 1-alpha-mediated transcriptional program, providing an alternative low-affinity glucose transporter that compensates for absent GLUT4 translocation.","C":"Exercise-induced local tissue acidosis from lactate production lowers intracellular pH in muscle fibers, which activates a pH-sensitive glucose channel that admits glucose independently of transporter-mediated facilitated diffusion or insulin signaling.","E":"Glucagon released in response to exercise-induced hypoglycemia binds glucagon receptors on skeletal muscle, activating cAMP-PKA signaling to phosphorylate a muscle-specific isoform of GLUT2 that translocates to the sarcolemma during aerobic exercise."},"explanation":"Skeletal muscle has two distinct pathways for GLUT4 translocation: (1) Insulin-dependent — PI3K-Akt-AS160 pathway; (2) Contraction-dependent — activated by exercise through AMPK (senses elevated AMP:ATP ratio during energy consumption) and CaMKII (senses calcium transients from muscle contraction). Both pathways converge on the same intracellular GLUT4 vesicle pool and use the same final effectors (Rab GTPases, VAMP2 SNARE proteins). This explains why exercise lowers blood glucose even in T1DM with minimal insulin — and why patients must anticipate hypoglycemia during exercise. Importantly, these pathways are additive: exercise + insulin causes more GLUT4 translocation than either alone. This is the mechanistic basis for exercise as a therapeutic tool in diabetes management. Contraction itself moves GLUT4 to the muscle membrane during exercise; epinephrine instead raises blood glucose through liver glycogenolysis and raises fatty acids through lipolysis. Exercising muscle takes up glucose because contraction translocates GLUT4 to the cell membrane without needing much insulin, not because GLUT1 is upregulated. Glucose enters exercising muscle by facilitated diffusion through GLUT4 moved to the membrane by contraction, not through a pH-sensitive channel. Exercising muscle uses GLUT4 translocated by contraction; GLUT2 is the transporter of beta cells, liver, kidney and gut, and glucagon's glycogenolytic target is the liver.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two independent GLUT4 translocation pathways in muscle: (1) Insulin → PI3K-Akt-AS160-Rab10-GLUT4; (2) Exercise/contraction → AMPK and CaMKII-AS160-GLUT4. High yield: In T1DM, exercise can cause hypoglycemia despite minimal insulin because contraction-mediated GLUT4 translocation continues. In T2DM, exercise improves insulin sensitivity by increasing GLUT4 expression and restoring downstream signaling. COMLEX: Exercise prescription is a cornerstone of T2DM management — regular aerobic exercise increases GLUT4 expression (training effect).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Insulin receptor tyrosine kinase activates PI3K-Akt to translocate GLUT4 in muscle and adipose tissue","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Contraction itself moves GLUT4 to the muscle membrane during","B":"Exercising muscle takes up glucose because contraction trans","C":"Glucose enters exercising muscle by facilitated diffusion th","E":"Exercising muscle uses GLUT4 translocated by contraction; GL"}},{"id":"sdl28_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain insulin's effects on carbohydrate metabolism in liver, muscle, and adipose tissue.","batch":2,"stem":"A 51-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes mellitus has been initiating basal insulin therapy. Her endocrinologist explains that insulin has coordinated effects on the liver that are essential for controlling postprandial blood glucose. After a carbohydrate-rich meal, which of the following best describes the full set of coordinated insulin actions in the liver that together promote net hepatic glucose uptake and glycogen storage?","choices":{"C":"Insulin activates mTORC1 in hepatocytes, which phosphorylates and activates GSK-3 to promote glycogen synthase activity; simultaneously, mTORC1 activates PEPCK to redirect oxaloacetate toward glycogen synthesis rather than gluconeogenesis.","A":"Insulin activates both glycogen phosphorylase and glycogen synthase simultaneously, increasing glycogen turnover while net storing glucose; the simultaneous activation creates a futile cycling that allows the liver to regulate glycogen content precisely without causing large glucose fluxes.","B":"Insulin inhibits GLUT2 at the hepatocyte basolateral membrane during the immediate postprandial period, forcing glucose to remain in the portal circulation until hepatic metabolism can be coordinated, preventing glucose flooding into the liver before glycogen synthase is fully activated.","D":"Insulin inactivates glycogen phosphorylase (stopping glycogenolysis), activates glucokinase (trapping portal glucose as glucose-6-phosphate), and activates glycogen synthase, while suppressing gluconeogenic enzymes, producing net hepatic glucose uptake and storage.","E":"Insulin promotes hepatic glycogen storage by activating hepatic glycogen phosphorylase to liberate glucose-1-phosphate from existing glycogen, which is then re-polymerized in a more compact form by glycogen synthase to increase the hepatic glycogen concentration."},"explanation":"Insulin orchestrates multiple coordinated hepatic actions: (1) Glucokinase induction — insulin promotes glucokinase gene transcription and cytoplasmic translocation, trapping portal glucose as G-6-P (prevents it from leaving the liver); (2) Glycogen phosphorylase inactivation — insulin-activated PP1 dephosphorylates and inactivates phosphorylase a → b (stops glycogenolysis); (3) Glycogen synthase activation — PP1 also dephosphorylates and activates glycogen synthase (promotes storage); (4) Gluconeogenesis suppression — via FoxO1 exclusion from nucleus, reducing PEPCK and G6Pase. The liver can store up to 5-6% of its weight as glycogen under maximal insulin stimulation. These actions are all simultaneously activated, not sequential. Insulin inactivates glycogen phosphorylase while activating glycogen synthase; it does not activate both in a futile cycle. Insulin enhances liver uptake of glucose by increasing glucokinase activity, trapping it as glucose phosphate, rather than blocking glucose entry into hepatocytes. Insulin inactivates phosphorylase, increases glycogen synthase activity and decreases gluconeogenic enzyme activity, so it does not activate PEPCK. Insulin inactivates, not activates, glycogen phosphorylase; storing glucose requires stopping glycogenolysis while activating glycogen synthase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Insulin effects on liver — activate: glucokinase, glycogen synthase, glycolysis (PFK-1), fatty acid synthesis (ACC, FAS), protein synthesis; inhibit: glycogen phosphorylase, gluconeogenesis (PEPCK, G6Pase), fatty acid oxidation. Classic pattern: fed state liver = glycogen synthesis + lipogenesis + gluconeogenesis OFF. Fasted liver = glycogenolysis + gluconeogenesis + ketogenesis ON. COMLEX: Hepatic insulin resistance in T2DM is characterized by paradox: insulin fails to suppress gluconeogenesis (liver remains gluconeogenic) but continues to activate lipogenesis (liver continues making fat) — causing fasting hyperglycemia AND fatty liver simultaneously.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Insulin receptor tyrosine kinase activates PI3K-Akt to translocate GLUT4 in muscle and adipose tissue","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Insulin inactivates glycogen phosphorylase while activating ","B":"Insulin enhances liver uptake of glucose by increasing gluco","C":"Insulin inactivates phosphorylase, increases glycogen syntha","E":"Insulin inactivates, not activates, glycogen phosphorylase; "}},{"id":"sdl28_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain insulin's effects on carbohydrate metabolism in liver, muscle, and adipose tissue.","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old man with type 2 diabetes managed with a sulfonylurea is found unresponsive by his home health aide. Point-of-care blood glucose is 22 mg/dL. After IV dextrose administration, his mental status improves within minutes. Unlike skeletal muscle and adipose tissue, his neurons were acutely and severely impaired by the hypoglycemia because of their absolute dependence on glucose. Which of the following best explains why brain neurons cannot substitute an alternative fuel source or increase glucose uptake independently during severe hypoglycemia?","choices":{"A":"Neurons and astrocytes express GLUT1 and GLUT3, constitutively active insulin-independent transporters that cannot increase their surface expression in response to hypoglycemia, so glucose flux through them falls in proportion to blood glucose, making the brain directly dependent on the plasma glucose.","B":"The blood-brain barrier actively concentrates glucose against a concentration gradient using sodium-coupled SGLT transporters, but during severe hypoglycemia the low plasma glucose concentration overwhelms the active transport capacity, causing transporter saturation and neuronal glucose starvation.","C":"Neurons express GLUT4 at high constitutive surface density because cerebral GLUT4 lacks the dileucine motif required for endosomal sequestration; this prevents insulin-mediated GLUT4 upregulation and explains why neuronal glucose uptake cannot be augmented above its already-maximal basal rate during hypoglycemia.","D":"Neurons can partially compensate for hypoglycemia by upregulating beta-oxidation of fatty acids via peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor delta activation, but this response requires 4 to 6 hours of gene transcription and cannot prevent the acute neuronal dysfunction seen during abrupt hypoglycemia.","E":"Insulin receptors in the hypothalamic arcuate nucleus detect hypoglycemia and trigger a compensatory neuroendocrine reflex that increases splanchnic nerve-mediated hepatic glucose output specifically toward cerebral circulation, but this reflex is insufficient during sulfonylurea-induced hypoglycemia because insulin secretion continues."},"explanation":"The brain relies almost exclusively on glucose as its energy substrate under normal conditions. Neurons express GLUT3 (high affinity, high capacity, constitutive surface expression) and astrocytes express GLUT1. Neither is regulated by insulin. These transporters are insulin-independent, which means the brain gets glucose without insulin — but it also means they cannot be upregulated in response to falling blood glucose. Glucose flux into neurons falls linearly with plasma glucose concentration. When glucose falls to 20 to 50 mg/100 mL, clonic seizures and loss of consciousness are likely (Guyton Ch 79). Fatty acids cannot cross the blood-brain barrier efficiently in the short term. Ketone bodies can cross and serve as alternative fuel (brain can adapt during prolonged starvation), but this adaptation takes days — not minutes. Brain glucose uptake runs through insulin-independent GLUT1 and GLUT3 transporters, not sodium-coupled SGLT transport against a gradient; SGLTs are in kidney and small intestine. Neurons use insulin-independent GLUT1 and GLUT3; GLUT4 is the insulin-dependent transporter of adipose tissue and striated muscle. Low glucose acts directly on the hypothalamus to trigger sympathetic, epinephrine-driven liver glucose release; neurons are vulnerable because they depend on blood glucose, not because an insulin-receptor reflex fails.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Brain glucose uptake = GLUT1 (astrocytes, endothelium) + GLUT3 (neurons) — both insulin-independent, both constitutively at cell surface. Guyton Ch 79 thresholds: 50 to 70 mg/100 mL = CNS excitability, nervousness, trembling, sweating; 20 to 50 mg/100 mL = clonic seizures and loss of consciousness; lower still = coma. Counter-regulatory hierarchy for hypoglycemia: glucagon (first), epinephrine (second), cortisol + GH (third, delayed). COMLEX: Sulfonylurea-induced hypoglycemia is prolonged because the drug continues to act after glucose is corrected; may require prolonged IV dextrose infusion and octreotide to suppress ongoing insulin secretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Insulin receptor tyrosine kinase activates PI3K-Akt to translocate GLUT4 in muscle and adipose tissue","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Brain glucose uptake runs through insulin-independent GLUT1 ","C":"Neurons use insulin-independent GLUT1 and GLUT3; GLUT4 is th","D":"Fatty acids cannot cross the b","E":"Low glucose acts directly on the hypothalamus to trigger sym"}},{"id":"sdl28_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain insulin's effects on fat metabolism.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with type 1 diabetes and poor glycemic control for 15 years has fasting plasma that appears lipemic. Total cholesterol and triglycerides are both markedly elevated, and carotid ultrasound shows early atherosclerotic plaque. Which mechanism best links his insulin deficiency to these lipid changes?","choices":{"E":"Excess free fatty acids reaching the liver are converted to phospholipids, cholesterol, and triglycerides that are discharged in lipoproteins","A":"Insulin deficiency increases hepatic LDL receptor expression, which pulls cholesterol into the liver and raises plasma triglycerides as a result","B":"Insulin deficiency activates lipoprotein lipase in adipose capillaries, which releases lipoprotein cholesterol from adipocytes into the plasma","C":"Hyperglycemia drives glucose into hepatic fatty acid synthesis through insulin-independent pathways, which raises lipoprotein output from the liver","D":"Ketone bodies are condensed into cholesterol in peripheral tissues, which then return it to the plasma bound to high-density lipoprotein"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 79) explains that the excess plasma fatty acids of insulin deficiency promote liver conversion of some fatty acids into phospholipids and cholesterol, which, along with excess triglycerides formed at the same time, are discharged into the blood in lipoproteins. Plasma lipoproteins occasionally increase as much as 3-fold, giving total plasma lipids of several percent rather than the normal 0.6 percent, and the high cholesterol promotes atherosclerosis in severe diabetes. Insulin normally activates lipoprotein lipase in adipose capillaries, so its absence reduces rather than increases that enzyme's activity. Increased LDL receptor expression would lower plasma cholesterol. Insulin, not hyperglycemia, promotes hepatic conversion of excess glucose into fatty acids. Ketone bodies are used for energy by peripheral tissues; they are not condensed into cholesterol there.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Insulin lack → hormone-sensitive lipase activation → free fatty acid flood → hepatic overproduction of cholesterol, phospholipids, and triglycerides in lipoproteins, plus ketogenesis (Guyton Ch 79). COMLEX: Myocardial infarction from coronary atherosclerosis is the most common cause of death in diabetes (Robbins).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Insulin receptor signaling inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase and promotes GLUT4-mediated glycerol phosphate formation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl28_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain insulin's effects on fat metabolism.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher studying adipocyte biology adds a physiological concentration of insulin to isolated primary human adipocytes in culture. Radiolabeling confirms that within 30 minutes, both intracellular glycerol-3-phosphate synthesis from glucose increases and incorporation of fatty acids from albumin-bound palmitate in the culture medium increases, with net triglyceride accumulation in lipid droplets. Which of the following best explains the coordinated mechanism by which insulin promotes triglyceride storage in adipocytes?","choices":{"B":"Insulin stimulates GLUT4 translocation to increase intracellular glucose availability for glycerol-3-phosphate synthesis (the triglyceride backbone), while simultaneously upregulating lipoprotein lipase activity to hydrolyze VLDL and chylomicron triglycerides at the capillary endothelium, providing fatty acids for adipocyte uptake and esterification.","A":"Insulin directly activates hormone-sensitive lipase in adipocytes via the PI3K-Akt-PDE3B pathway, generating free fatty acids from stored triglycerides that are immediately re-esterified with glucose-derived glycerol-3-phosphate to form new triglycerides in a remodeling cycle.","C":"Insulin activates adipocyte-specific acetyl-CoA carboxylase to convert glucose-derived acetyl-CoA to malonyl-CoA, which is elongated by fatty acid synthase to palmitate; this palmitate is then esterified with oxaloacetate-derived glycerol to form stored triglycerides.","D":"Insulin increases microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP) activity in adipocytes, enhancing the assembly and intracellular trafficking of VLDL-like particles that are retained within the adipocyte lipid droplet rather than secreted into circulation.","E":"Insulin activates adipose triglyceride lipase (ATGL) on lipid droplet surfaces via the mTORC2-SGK1 signaling pathway, liberating diacylglycerol intermediates that are rapidly re-acylated using fatty acids from the circulation to enlarge the triglyceride pool."},"explanation":"Insulin promotes adipocyte triglyceride storage through two coordinated mechanisms: (1) Glucose → glycerol-3-phosphate: GLUT4 translocation increases glucose uptake → glucose is converted to glycerol-3-phosphate (via DHAP → glycerol-3-phosphate in the cytoplasm). Glycerol-3-phosphate is the essential backbone for triglyceride synthesis — adipocytes cannot use free glycerol directly (they lack glycerol kinase). (2) Fatty acid supply — insulin dramatically increases lipoprotein lipase (LPL) activity in adipose capillaries; LPL hydrolyzes circulating VLDL and chylomicron triglycerides → free fatty acids → taken up by adipocytes → esterified with glycerol-3-phosphate → triglycerides stored in lipid droplets. Net effect: both the backbone and the fatty acid chains are provided by insulin action. Insulin inhibits, not activates, hormone-sensitive lipase, so release of fatty acids from stored triglyceride falls rather than being recycled. Glucose entering fat cells mainly forms alpha-glycerol phosphate, which supplies the glycerol backbone, while most fatty acids are made in the liver; adipocytes make only minute amounts. Adipocytes store fat by taking up fatty acids freed by insulin-activated lipoprotein lipase and combining them with glucose-derived alpha-glycerol phosphate, not by assembling VLDL-like particles. Insulin inhibits hydrolysis of triglycerides already stored in fat cells rather than activating a lipase; storage relies on lipoprotein lipase-freed fatty acids and alpha-glycerol phosphate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Adipose tissue TG synthesis requires: (1) fatty acids (from LPL-cleaved VLDL/chylomicrons or small de novo synthesis) + (2) glycerol-3-phosphate (from glucose — adipocytes lack glycerol kinase). Insulin is the master switch: activates LPL (increases FA supply) + stimulates GLUT4 (provides glycerol-3-P backbone) + inhibits HSL (prevents breakdown). In obesity with insulin resistance: LPL in adipose is dysregulated, HSL is hyperactive basally → excess FFA release → ectopic fat deposition in liver, muscle → worsened insulin resistance (lipotoxicity). COMLEX: LPL is regulated reciprocally in adipose vs muscle: insulin increases adipose LPL (storage) and decreases muscle LPL; fasting does the opposite (muscle LPL increases for energy, adipose LPL decreases).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Insulin receptor signaling inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase and promotes GLUT4-mediated glycerol-3-phosphate synthesis in adipocytes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Insulin inhibits, not activates, hormone-sensitive lipase, s","C":"Glucose entering fat cells mainly forms alpha-glycerol phosp","D":"Adipocytes store fat by taking up fatty acids freed by insul","E":"Insulin inhibits hydrolysis of triglycerides already stored "}},{"id":"sdl28_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain insulin's effects on fat metabolism.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man undergoes a total pancreatectomy for pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma involving the entire gland. No exogenous insulin is administered in the immediate postoperative period while the surgical team assesses his metabolic status. Which of the following laboratory abnormalities is expected to be the earliest and most prominent manifestation of total insulin deficiency in this patient?","choices":{"D":"Elevation of plasma free fatty acids appears first, even before significant hyperglycemia, because loss of insulin removes tonic inhibition of hormone-sensitive lipase in adipose tissue, so immediate lipolysis raises free fatty acids faster than lost glucose uptake raises plasma glucose.","A":"Severe hyperglycemia exceeding 500 mg/dL appears within the first hour because the loss of insulin-stimulated GLUT4 translocation across all peripheral tissues simultaneously and completely eliminates glucose uptake, causing immediate glucose accumulation in the bloodstream faster than the kidney can clear it.","B":"Metabolic ketoacidosis with a pH below 7.20 and anion gap above 30 mEq/L appears within the first 2 to 3 hours because hepatic ketone body production from baseline fatty acid oxidation is constitutive and fully inhibited by even trace amounts of insulin that now are entirely absent.","C":"Hyperaminoacidemia with branched-chain amino acid concentrations exceeding three times the normal range appears first because insulin is the sole inhibitor of lysosomal cathepsins in all peripheral tissues, and its complete absence triggers immediate, widespread, and simultaneous proteolysis within minutes.","E":"Hypertriglyceridemia from chylomicron and VLDL accumulation appears first because insulin is required for the transcription of lipoprotein lipase in all tissues, and its sudden absence halts all circulating triglyceride hydrolysis, causing rapid buildup of triglyceride-rich particles."},"explanation":"This clinical pearl comes directly from Guyton and Hall Chapter 79: after total pancreatectomy (or insulin withdrawal in T1DM), free fatty acid concentrations in plasma rise FASTER than blood glucose. The mechanism: insulin continuously suppresses HSL at low tonic levels, and this suppression is completely removed by pancreatectomy. HSL is immediately active, liberating FFAs from adipose stores into the circulation. In contrast, blood glucose rises because of (1) reduced peripheral uptake (GLUT4) and (2) increased hepatic output (glycogenolysis + gluconeogenesis) — but these processes take somewhat longer to produce measurable hyperglycemia. Ketoacidosis typically develops over hours to days, not minutes. Full DKA requires sustained FFA flux and hepatic ketogenesis to overwhelm buffering capacity. After pancreatectomy plasma free fatty acids rise almost immediately, faster even than glucose, as hormone-sensitive lipase is activated; hyperaminoacidemia is not the earliest change. After pancreatectomy plasma free fatty acids rise almost immediately, faster even than glucose, as hormone-sensitive lipase is activated; triglyceride-rich particle buildup is not the first change.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic teaching from Guyton — in absolute insulin deficiency, the sequence of metabolic derangements begins with: (1) elevated FFAs (immediate, within minutes-hours) → (2) hyperglycemia (within hours) → (3) ketonemia (within hours to 1-2 days) → (4) metabolic acidosis (once buffering capacity is exceeded). In clinical practice, this explains why some newly diagnosed T1DM patients present with FFAs and early ketosis before they have extreme hyperglycemia. COMLEX: Brittle diabetes (unstable T1DM with frequent DKA episodes) reflects the absence of the normal glucoregulatory buffer provided by residual beta cell function and adipose fat regulation — any insulin omission causes rapid FFA release and DKA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Insulin receptor signaling inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase and promotes GLUT4-mediated glycerol-3-phosphate synthesis in adipocytes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"In contrast, blood glucose ris","B":"Ketoacidosis typically develop","C":"After pancreatectomy plasma free fatty acids rise almost immediately, faster even than glucose, as hormone-sensitive lipase is activated; hy","E":"After pancreatectomy plasma free fatty acids rise almost immediately, faster even than glucose, as hormone-sensitive lipase is activated; tr"}},{"id":"sdl28_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain insulin's effects on protein metabolism.","batch":2,"stem":"A 15-year-old boy with newly diagnosed type 1 diabetes mellitus presents with a 4-month history of progressive weight loss, generalized weakness, and muscle wasting. Despite polyphagia and a reported dietary intake exceeding 4,000 kcal per day, he has lost 14 kilograms. His HbA1c is 14.3%. Physical examination reveals diffuse muscle atrophy. Which of the following best explains the primary biochemical mechanism responsible for his skeletal muscle protein loss?","choices":{"C":"Insulin deficiency removes inhibition of lysosomal proteolysis and eliminates mTORC1-driven translational activation, while uninhibited gluconeogenesis consumes the amino acids released from muscle protein catabolism as substrates, producing negative nitrogen balance and wasting.","A":"Hyperglycemia-induced activation of the receptor for advanced glycation end products on myocytes drives NF-kB-mediated upregulation of ubiquitin ligases MuRF-1 and MAFbx, causing selective proteasomal degradation of myosin heavy chain and titin independent of insulin signaling.","B":"Elevated glucagon directly stimulates protein catabolism in skeletal muscle by binding glucagon receptors on myocytes and activating cAMP-PKA, which phosphorylates and activates lysosomal proteases, the same pathway by which glucagon activates glycogen phosphorylase in hepatocytes.","D":"Loss of insulin reduces hepatic IGF-1 production because insulin receptor signaling on hepatocytes is required for growth hormone receptor expression, and the resulting IGF-1 deficiency eliminates the primary anabolic stimulus for myofibrillar protein synthesis in skeletal muscle.","E":"Insulin deficiency paradoxically increases PI3K-Akt-mTORC1 signaling in skeletal muscle through derepression of an inhibitory IRS-1 feedback mechanism, and this hyperactivated mTOR causes S6K1-mediated phosphorylation of eIF4B that paradoxically inhibits translational elongation."},"explanation":"Insulin exerts four coordinated pro-anabolic, anti-catabolic effects on skeletal muscle: (1) mRNA translation — activated via PI3K-Akt-mTORC1 → 4EBP1 phosphorylation → eIF4E release → translation initiation; (2) DNA transcription — insulin activates anabolic gene expression; (3) Anti-proteolytic — insulin suppresses lysosomal cathepsins and autophagic flux; (4) Amino acid uptake — insulin drives BCAA transport into cells. In T1DM without insulin, ALL four are reversed: lysosomal proteolysis increases, translation stops, amino acid uptake decreases, and unrestrained gluconeogenesis consumes the released amino acids. The combination of catabolic excess and anabolic deficit produces severe muscle wasting — the classic wasted, hungry T1DM patient of the pre-insulin era. Insulin deficiency itself halts protein synthesis and increases catabolism, dumping amino acids for gluconeogenesis, rather than wasting through RAGE-driven ubiquitin ligases independent of insulin. Glucagon's described actions are on the liver, glycogenolysis and amino acid uptake for gluconeogenesis; muscle protein loss comes from absent insulin, which normally limits lysosomal protein degradation. Protein loss follows directly from absent insulin, which is itself required for protein synthesis, rather than from reduced hepatic growth hormone receptors and IGF-1.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The protein-wasting state of T1DM is clinically indistinguishable from other catabolic states (sepsis, burns, prolonged starvation, cortisol excess). Key marker: negative nitrogen balance (urine urea nitrogen exceeds dietary nitrogen intake). In pre-insulin era, Joslin described T1DM patients as starving to death despite eating. Insulin is the hormone of plenty — its absence is a state of cellular starvation even when calories are abundant. COMLEX: Cachexia in cancer patients shares the same mechanism — elevated cytokines (TNF-alpha, IL-6) reduce insulin sensitivity and increase proteolysis, mimicking insulin-deficient catabolism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — Growth hormone synergizes with insulin to promote protein synthesis via complementary amino acid uptake profiles in tissues","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Insulin deficiency itself halts protein synthesis and increa","B":"Glucagon's described actions are on the liver, glycogenolysi","D":"Protein loss follows directly from absent insulin, which is "}},{"id":"sdl28_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain insulin's effects on protein metabolism.","batch":2,"stem":"An 11-year-old girl with isolated growth hormone deficiency is evaluated by a pediatric endocrinologist for short stature and growth failure. Her plasma insulin levels and glucose tolerance are normal. Her endocrinologist explains that growth hormone and insulin act synergistically — meaning together they promote more growth than either can achieve alone. Which of the following best describes the physiological basis for this synergy between insulin and growth hormone in promoting protein synthesis and growth?","choices":{"E":"Growth hormone and insulin each promote cellular uptake of a different selection of amino acids, all of which are required for growth; Guyton offers this as perhaps a small part of why both hormones must be present together for the dramatic growth that neither achieves alone.","A":"Insulin substitutes for growth hormone in stimulating hepatic IGF-1 production when growth hormone is absent, and this insulin-derived IGF-1 then acts on growth plate chondrocytes through a shared insulin receptor isoform to drive cartilage synthesis and linear bone growth.","B":"Growth hormone and insulin activate the same receptor tyrosine kinase present on skeletal muscle and growth plate cells; their synergy results from cooperative IRS-1 phosphorylation that additively activates the PI3K-Akt-mTORC1 axis beyond what either hormone achieves through the receptor alone.","C":"Growth hormone upregulates insulin receptor expression on osteoblasts and growth plate chondrocytes by activating the JAK2-STAT5b pathway, amplifying insulin-mediated GLUT4 translocation and glucose uptake that provides the metabolic fuel needed for chondrocyte proliferation.","D":"Insulin promotes the transcription of growth hormone receptor mRNA in muscle and liver through an Akt-FoxO1 pathway, increasing tissue sensitivity to circulating growth hormone and creating a positive feed-forward loop for protein anabolism during the postprandial state."},"explanation":"Guyton (course reading) says a hypophysectomized, pancreatectomized rat hardly grows, that either growth hormone or insulin alone causes almost no growth, and that the combination causes dramatic growth, so the two hormones act synergistically, each performing a specific function separate from the other. Guyton adds that perhaps a small part of the need for both hormones is that each promotes cellular uptake of a different selection of amino acids, all of which are required for growth. Guyton does not say which amino acids each hormone favors, so the specific amino acid lists are not part of this answer. The other choices invent shared receptors, insulin-driven IGF-1 production, or receptor up-regulation steps that the course text does not describe.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GH-insulin synergy for growth: (1) GH stimulates IGF-1 from liver → IGF-1 acts on growth plate; (2) GH directly stimulates amino acid uptake in tissues (arginine, neutral AAs); (3) Insulin stimulates BCAA uptake and mTORC1; (4) Together they supply the full essential amino acid complement. Laron syndrome = GH receptor defect → elevated GH, low IGF-1, dwarfism despite normal insulin. Treatment with recombinant IGF-1. Acromegaly (GH excess): increased protein synthesis, insulin resistance (GH is counter-regulatory). COMLEX: In critical illness, GH resistance develops — despite elevated GH, IGF-1 is low and protein catabolism dominates; refeeding with adequate protein + insulin management is key.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — Growth hormone synergizes with insulin to promote protein synthesis via complementary amino acid uptake profiles in tissues","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl28_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain insulin's effects on protein metabolism.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher infuses a physiological dose of insulin into the portal vein of a fasted dog. Skeletal muscle biopsies taken 45 minutes later show increased incorporation of radiolabeled leucine into myofibrillar proteins and significantly decreased release of free amino acids from muscle into venous effluent. Which of the following best describes the molecular mechanisms responsible for both the increased protein synthesis and the decreased amino acid efflux observed in this experiment?","choices":{"B":"Insulin activates hepatic gluconeogenesis by stimulating PEPCK transcription via the Akt-mTOR pathway, which reduces circulating amino acid levels by consuming them as gluconeogenic substrates; this fall in plasma amino acids signals skeletal muscle to reduce its amino acid efflux and redirect its own amino acid pool toward local protein synthesis.","A":"Insulin activates the PI3K-Akt-mTORC1 axis, which phosphorylates 4EBP1 to release eIF4E for cap-dependent translation and activates S6 kinase 1 to promote ribosomal biogenesis, while suppressing lysosomal cathepsin activity and autophagosome formation, producing net protein accretion.","C":"Insulin reduces sympathetic nerve activity via hypothalamic insulin receptor activation, which decreases norepinephrine-mediated activation of the ubiquitin-proteasome pathway in skeletal muscle, thereby reducing protein degradation and the resulting free amino acid efflux into venous blood.","D":"Insulin activates eIF2-alpha phosphorylation via the PERK kinase arm of the integrated stress response, which selectively upregulates translation of mRNAs with upstream open reading frames encoding myofibrillar structural proteins at the expense of global protein synthesis.","E":"Insulin activates AMPK in skeletal muscle by reducing the intracellular ATP-to-ADP ratio through increased glucose transport via GLUT4, and this AMPK activation directly stimulates mTORC1 complex assembly to coordinate ribosomal translation with cellular energy status."},"explanation":"Insulin promotes protein synthesis via the PI3K-Akt-mTORC1 pathway: Insulin → IRS-1 → PI3K → PIP3 → PDK1 → Akt (PKB) phosphorylation → mTORC1 activation. mTORC1 then: (1) phosphorylates 4EBP1 → releases eIF4E → cap-dependent translation initiation; (2) activates S6K1 → ribosomal protein S6 phosphorylation → ribosomal biogenesis. Simultaneously, Akt directly suppresses autophagy by phosphorylating and inhibiting ULK1 and Beclin-1, and insulin reduces lysosomal cathepsin activity. The combined result: more protein is synthesized, less is degraded, and amino acids are retained within the cell rather than released. AMPK is actually INHIBITED when energy (glucose via GLUT4) rises — the opposite of the AMPK option. Insulin depresses, not activates, hepatic gluconeogenesis, which conserves amino acids in protein stores; retention in muscle reflects increased translation and inhibited protein catabolism. Insulin acts on the muscle cells themselves, turning on mRNA translation and diminishing lysosomal protein degradation, rather than working by lowering sympathetic activity. Phosphorylation of eIF2-alpha by stress-activated kinases inactivates it and blocks translation initiation, whereas insulin turns on ribosomal translation of mRNA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PI3K-Akt-mTORC1 = anabolic master regulator. Activated by: insulin, IGF-1, leucine (direct sensor), amino acid sufficiency. Inhibited by: AMPK (energy deficit), rapamycin (pharmacologic), TSC1/2 mutations (cause tuberous sclerosis with mTOR hyperactivation). Anabolic steroids amplify mTORC1 signaling downstream of androgen receptor. 4EBP1 phosphorylation = key switch for translation initiation; S6K1 phosphorylation = ribosomal biogenesis. High-yield rapamycin targets: (1) transplant immunosuppression, (2) PIK3CA-mutated cancers, (3) tuberous sclerosis complex tumors. COMLEX: mTOR signaling in muscle is the mechanistic target for resistance training adaptations — mechanical stress activates mTORC1 independently of insulin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — Growth hormone synergizes with insulin to promote protein synthesis via complementary amino acid uptake profiles in tissues","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Insulin depresses, not activates, hepatic gluconeogenesis, w","C":"Insulin acts on the muscle cells themselves, turning on mRNA","D":"Phosphorylation of eIF2-alpha by stress-activated kinases in","E":"AMPK is actually INHIBITED whe"}},{"id":"sdl28_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe glucagon secretion, mechanism of action, and metabolic effects.","batch":2,"stem":"In an experiment, glucagon is infused into a fasting volunteer for 6 hours. Blood glucose rises within minutes. Serial liver biopsies show that hepatic glycogen is nearly exhausted by about 4 hours, yet blood glucose stays elevated until the infusion ends. Which best explains the continued hyperglycemia after glycogen is depleted?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon raises hepatic amino acid uptake and activates gluconeogenic enzymes, including the pyruvate to phosphoenolpyruvate step","B":"Glucagon releases glucose from skeletal muscle glycogen, which continues after liver glycogen is gone because muscle stores are much larger","C":"Glucagon blocks glucose uptake by skeletal muscle and fat through GLUT4, so glucose stays in the blood after hepatic glucose output stops","D":"Glucagon speeds insulin breakdown by hepatic insulinase, so glucose cannot be cleared after glycogen is gone","E":"Glucagon converts hepatic fatty acids into glucose through beta-oxidation, which keeps blood glucose up once glycogen stores are used up"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 79) explains that the most dramatic effect of glucagon is liver glycogenolysis within minutes, through a cAMP-driven amplifying cascade, and that infusion for about 4 hours can deplete all liver glycogen. Even after liver glycogen is exhausted, continued infusion still causes hyperglycemia because glucagon increases amino acid uptake by liver cells and their conversion to glucose by gluconeogenesis, activating the enzymes for amino acid transport and gluconeogenesis, especially the system that converts pyruvate to phosphoenolpyruvate, a rate-limiting step. Muscle glycogen is used by the muscle itself and is not released as blood glucose. Glucagon's glucose effect is hepatic output, not blockade of GLUT4. Insulinase breakdown is not part of glucagon's action. Fatty acid oxidation yields acetyl-CoA, which cannot be converted to glucose.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucagon: Gs → cAMP → protein kinase → phosphorylase kinase → phosphorylase a → glycogenolysis within minutes, then gluconeogenesis from amino acids once glycogen is gone (Guyton Ch 79). COMLEX: At high concentrations glucagon also activates adipose lipase and inhibits hepatic triglyceride storage, making more fatty acids available for energy (Guyton Ch 79).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — Gs-coupled receptor signaling: glucagon activates adenylyl cyclase to raise cAMP and activate protein kinase A in hepatocytes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A"},{"id":"sdl28_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe glucagon secretion, mechanism of action, and metabolic effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old marathon runner has blood drawn at the 35-km mark of a race. Plasma glucagon is about 4-fold higher than his resting value, and blood glucose is normal. Which best explains the glucagon rise and its function?","choices":{"D":"Glucagon rises because somatostatin secretion stops during exercise, and glucagon then stimulates insulin release to fuel the working muscle","A":"Glucagon rises because exercise raises blood glucose, and glucagon secretion increases whenever blood glucose climbs above the fasting level","B":"Glucagon rises to drive glucose into contracting muscle through muscle glucagon receptors, taking over insulin's role during exercise","C":"Glucagon rises in exhaustive exercise, perhaps from amino acids and beta-adrenergic islet stimulation, and it prevents a fall in blood glucose","E":"Glucagon rises to turn lactate from the legs into muscle glycogen, which stores energy for the final kilometers of the race"},"explanation":"Guyton (Ch 79) states that during exhaustive exercise blood glucagon often increases 4- to 5-fold, and that a beneficial effect of the glucagon is to prevent a decrease in blood glucose. Increased circulating amino acids may be one factor that raises glucagon during exercise, and beta-adrenergic stimulation of the islets may also play a role. Blood glucose is by far the most potent controller of glucagon secretion, but in the opposite direction from insulin: hyperglycemia decreases glucagon, so a rise in glucose would not explain the increase. Contracting muscle takes up glucose through contraction-driven GLUT4 translocation, not through glucagon receptors. Somatostatin inhibits glucagon and insulin, but the source does not attribute the exercise rise to somatostatin stopping, and glucagon's role here is hepatic glucose output. Glucagon acts on liver glycogen, not on rebuilding muscle glycogen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucagon stimulators: hypoglycemia (the most potent controller), amino acids such as alanine and arginine, exhaustive exercise (4- to 5-fold); inhibitors: hyperglycemia and somatostatin (Guyton Ch 79). COMLEX: During exercise, muscle glucose uptake rises through contraction-driven GLUT4 translocation while glucagon keeps hepatic glucose output up.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — muscle contraction moves GLUT4 to the membrane during exercise","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Blood glucose is by far the mo","D":"Somatostatin inhibits glucagon","E":"Glucagon acts on liver glycoge"}},{"id":"sdl28_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe glucagon secretion, mechanism of action, and metabolic effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman is referred to endocrinology after a 7-month history of a migratory blistering rash, 16-kg weight loss, and fasting blood glucose levels consistently between 180 and 280 mg/dL. CT scan reveals a 3.2 cm pancreatic tail mass with multiple hepatic lesions. Laboratory evaluation confirms a plasma glucagon level of 1,380 pg/mL (markedly elevated). In addition to its primary hepatic glycogenolytic and gluconeogenic effects, which of the following best describes a direct extrahepatic pharmacological effect of glucagon at the markedly elevated concentrations present in this patient?","choices":{"C":"At pharmacological concentrations, glucagon exerts a positive inotropic and chronotropic effect on the heart via cAMP-mediated activation of cardiac contractile machinery, and also increases renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate while inhibiting gastric acid secretion and triglyceride storage in the liver.","A":"At pharmacological concentrations, glucagon suppresses cardiac contractility by activating phosphodiesterase in myocardial cells, lowering cAMP and reducing intracellular calcium transients; this negative inotropic effect is the mechanism by which IV glucagon reverses the bradycardia and hypotension of beta-blocker overdose.","B":"At pharmacological concentrations, glucagon stimulates gastric acid secretion by directly activating proton-pump exocytosis in parietal cells via a cAMP-PKA pathway, and also promotes triglyceride storage in hepatocytes by activating hepatic lipoprotein lipase and reducing VLDL secretion.","D":"At pharmacological concentrations, glucagon acts on brown adipose tissue to stimulate uncoupling protein-1 expression via the cAMP-PKA pathway, increasing thermogenesis and contributing to the severe involuntary weight gain that is the hallmark presentation of glucagonoma syndrome.","E":"At pharmacological concentrations, glucagon promotes intestinal absorption of glucose by activating SGLT1 and GLUT2 expression in enterocytes via transcriptional mechanisms, amplifying postprandial hyperglycemia beyond what hepatic glycogenolysis alone produces in glucagonoma patients."},"explanation":"At pharmacological concentrations, glucagon has multiple extrahepatic effects beyond hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis: (1) Cardiovascular — positive inotrope and chronotrope via cAMP in cardiomyocytes; this is the basis for using IV glucagon in beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker overdose; (2) Renal — increases renal blood flow and GFR; (3) GI — INHIBITS gastric acid secretion (anti-secretory) and inhibits GI motility; (4) Hepatic fat — INHIBITS triglyceride storage in liver (not promotes it). The glucagonoma syndrome features the 4 Ds: Dermatitis (necrolytic migratory erythema from amino acid deficiency), Diabetes (from hyperglycemia), Deep vein thrombosis, and Depression (some include Diarrhea). Weight LOSS is characteristic, not weight gain — hyperglucagonemia causes massive catabolism. At high concentrations glucagon enhances the strength of the heart, a positive rather than negative inotropic effect. At high concentrations glucagon inhibits gastric acid secretion and inhibits triglyceride storage in the liver, the opposite of both claims. Glucagon's high-concentration effects are on heart strength, kidney blood flow, bile secretion and gastric acid inhibition; increased intestinal glucose absorption is not among them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glucagonoma syndrome = necrolytic migratory erythema + hyperglycemia + weight loss + DVT + anemia + glossitis. The rash is pathognomonic — it results from amino acid deficiency (glucagon drives gluconeogenesis, consuming all amino acids). Glucagon pharmacological uses: (1) Severe hypoglycemia (1 mg IM/SQ); (2) Beta-blocker overdose (increases cAMP independently of beta receptors); (3) Calcium channel blocker overdose; (4) Bowel relaxation during endoscopy/radiology (inhibits motility). COMLEX: Octreotide (somatostatin analog) is used to treat glucagonoma (inhibits glucagon secretion) and also treats insulinoma, carcinoid, and VIPoma — broad islet cell tumor management.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — Gs-coupled receptor signaling: glucagon activates adenylyl cyclase to raise cAMP and activate protein kinase A in hepatocytes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"At high concentrations glucagon enhances the strength of the","B":"At high concentrations glucagon inhibits gastric acid secret","D":"Weight LOSS is characteristic,","E":"Glucagon's high-concentration effects are on heart strength,"}}],"29":[{"id":"sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define Prediabetes, Diabetes Mellitus and its classification.","batch":1,"stem":"Type 1 diabetes mellitus accounts for approximately what percentage of all diabetes cases, and which immunological mechanism most directly causes the absolute insulin deficiency seen in this condition?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 30–40% of cases; peripheral insulin receptor downregulation by excess circulating free fatty acids leads to relative insulin resistance and compensatory hyperinsulinemia","B":"Approximately 5–10% of cases; destruction of pancreatic beta cells by autoreactive CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes and Th1 helper cells leads to absolute insulin deficiency","C":"Approximately 50–60% of cases; amyloid polypeptide deposition within islet beta cells causes progressive beta-cell dysfunction and reduced insulin secretory capacity","D":"Approximately 20–25% of cases; glucagon-secreting alpha cells undergo hyperplasia due to loss of paracrine insulin inhibition, leading to net hepatic glucose output exceeding pancreatic insulin production","E":"Approximately 1–2% of cases; a single gain-of-function mutation in the glucokinase gene causes constitutive activation of glucose sensing in beta cells, leading to unregulated insulin secretion"},"explanation":"Type 1 DM accounts for 5–10% of all diabetes cases and results from autoimmune destruction of beta cells. Autoreactive CD8+ CTLs and Th1 cells mediate this destruction in genetically predisposed individuals (HLA-DR3/DR4). The result is absolute insulin deficiency, making patients dependent on exogenous insulin and prone to DKA. The other choices describe T2D insulin resistance, T2D beta-cell amyloid deposition, glucagon physiology in T1D (a consequence, not cause), and MODY (a monogenic diabetes subtype), respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T1D = autoimmune, ~10% of DM, HLA-DR3/DR4 association, islet cell antibodies (anti-GAD65, anti-IA2, anti-insulin). Classic presentation: young, lean, DKA-prone, C-peptide low/absent. COMLEX: consider viscerosomatic reflex at T5–T9 (T6–T10 as the alternate range) per the OMM course map (celiac plexus innervates pancreas), vs reflex T5–T9 with sympathetic T5–T11 per OMM atlas Ch 15 and T6–T10 per Foundations Ch 6; Chapman reflex for pancreas on 7th intercostal space anteriorly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Beta-cell GLUT2 and glucokinase link glucose metabolism to insulin secretion; loss of this function defines the spectrum from prediabetes to overt diabetes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"The other choices describe T2D","C":"The other choices describe T2D","D":"The other choices describe T2D","E":"The other choices describe T2D"}},{"id":"sdl29_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define Prediabetes, Diabetes Mellitus and its classification.","batch":1,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman is found to have a fasting plasma glucose of 118 mg/dL on routine screening. She is asymptomatic, has no family history of type 1 or type 2 diabetes, and her BMI is 22 kg/m². Further testing reveals a glucokinase gene mutation. Which of the following best characterizes this patient's condition?","choices":{"B":"Maturity-onset diabetes of the young type 2 (MODY2), caused by glucokinase haploinsufficiency that raises the glucose threshold for insulin secretion, resulting in mild stable hyperglycemia without progressive beta-cell failure","A":"Prediabetes due to early peripheral insulin resistance, likely to progress to type 2 diabetes mellitus within 5–10 years if lifestyle modifications are not implemented promptly by the patient","C":"Type 1 diabetes mellitus in a honeymoon phase, where residual beta-cell function temporarily maintains near-normal glucose levels before autoimmune destruction is completed over months","D":"Secondary diabetes mellitus caused by glucokinase-mediated ATP overproduction in beta cells, leading to constitutive insulin secretion, recurrent hypoglycemia, and paradoxical fasting hyperglycemia","E":"Gestational diabetes mellitus equivalent, triggered by hormonal shifts that unmask a latent glucokinase mutation, expected to resolve completely after the precipitating hormonal changes normalize"},"explanation":"MODY2 is caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in glucokinase (GCK), the glucose sensor of beta cells. Glucokinase haploinsufficiency raises the threshold at which glucose triggers insulin secretion, causing mild, stable fasting hyperglycemia. Unlike T2D, it does not progress and rarely causes complications. Unlike T1D, there are no autoantibodies and no risk of DKA. It is autosomal dominant and often discovered incidentally in young, non-obese individuals. Glucokinase MODY comes from a loss-of-function mutation that alters the glucose set point for insulin secretion, causing hyperglycemia, not constitutive insulin secretion with hypoglycemia. A germline glucokinase mutation causes MODY with set-point hyperglycemia, not gestational diabetes, which arises for the first time in pregnancy and typically resolves after delivery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MODY = monogenic, autosomal dominant, non-obese, young onset. MODY1=HNF-4α, MODY2=glucokinase (mildest, stable), MODY3=HNF-1α (most common MODY, responds to sulfonylureas). COMLEX: MODY3 sulfonylurea sensitivity is a classic test point; glucokinase MODY typically needs no pharmacologic treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Beta-cell GLUT2 and glucokinase link glucose metabolism to insulin secretion; loss of this function defines the spectrum from prediabetes to overt diabetes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Unlike T2D, it does not progre","C":"Unlike T1D, there are no autoa","D":"Glucokinase MODY comes from a loss-of-function mutation that","E":"A germline glucokinase mutation causes MODY with set-point h"}},{"id":"sdl29_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define Prediabetes, Diabetes Mellitus and its classification.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following combinations of laboratory values correctly defines the prediabetes range across all three accepted screening parameters?","choices":{"C":"Fasting plasma glucose 100–125 mg/dL, 2-hour OGTT glucose 140–199 mg/dL after 75 g oral load, and HbA1c 5.7–6.4%","A":"Fasting plasma glucose 90–109 mg/dL, 2-hour OGTT glucose 120–159 mg/dL after 75 g oral load, and HbA1c 5.4–6.0%, representing early impairment before insulin resistance is detectable by clinical examination","B":"Fasting plasma glucose 110–135 mg/dL, 2-hour OGTT glucose 150–209 mg/dL after 75 g oral load, and HbA1c 6.0–6.9%, representing the overlap zone between prediabetes and overt diabetes mellitus in most patients","D":"Fasting plasma glucose 126–145 mg/dL, 2-hour OGTT glucose 200–249 mg/dL after 100 g oral load, and HbA1c 6.5–7.0%, representing the lowest diagnostic threshold for confirmed diabetes mellitus requiring treatment","E":"Fasting plasma glucose 100–119 mg/dL, 2-hour OGTT glucose 140–179 mg/dL after 100 g oral load, and HbA1c 5.5–6.2%, representing impaired fasting glucose only when all three parameters are simultaneously abnormal"},"explanation":"Prediabetes is defined by ADA criteria as: FPG 100–125 mg/dL (impaired fasting glucose), 2-hr OGTT 140–199 mg/dL (impaired glucose tolerance), or HbA1c 5.7–6.4%. The OGTT uses a 75 g glucose load in non-pregnant adults. These thresholds identify individuals at high risk of progressing to T2D and cardiovascular disease, where lifestyle intervention is most effective. Values at or above the upper limit (FPG ≥126, 2-hr OGTT ≥200, HbA1c ≥6.5%) define diabetes mellitus. ADA prediabetes values are fasting glucose 100 to 125, 2-hour OGTT 140 to 199 and HbA1c 5.7 to 6.4%, not the lower ranges here. ADA prediabetes is fasting glucose 100 to 125 mg/dL and HbA1c 5.7 to 6.4%; values of 126 or above and 6.5% or above define diabetes. A fasting glucose of 126 or more, 2-hour glucose of 200 or more and HbA1c of 6.5% or more define diabetes, not prediabetes, and the OGTT uses a 75 g load. ADA prediabetes uses fasting glucose 100 to 125, 2-hour OGTT 140 to 199 after a 75 g load and HbA1c 5.7 to 6.4%, and any one abnormal parameter suffices.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memorize the cutoffs — prediabetes FPG 100–125, DM FPG ≥126; prediabetes OGTT 140–199, DM OGTT ≥200; prediabetes HbA1c 5.7–6.4%, DM HbA1c ≥6.5%. COMLEX: lifestyle modification (diet and regular exercise) reduces progression to T2D in prediabetes — key OMM/lifestyle counseling point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Beta-cell GLUT2 and glucokinase link glucose metabolism to insulin secretion; loss of this function defines the spectrum from prediabetes to overt diabetes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"ADA prediabetes values are fasting glucose 100 to 125, 2-hou","B":"ADA prediabetes is fasting glucose 100 to 125 mg/dL and HbA1","D":"A fasting glucose of 126 or more, 2-hour glucose of 200 or m","E":"ADA prediabetes uses fasting glucose 100 to 125, 2-hour OGTT"}},{"id":"sdl29_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand how a diagnosis of diabetes mellitus is established by specific criteria.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the ADA criteria in the course reading, which single plasma glucose result establishes the diagnosis of diabetes mellitus without a repeat test on a separate day in a patient with classic hyperglycemic symptoms (polyuria, polydipsia, and unexplained weight loss)?","choices":{"B":"Fasting plasma glucose ≥126 mg/dL; once classic hyperglycemic signs are present, this single fasting result no longer has to be repeated and confirmed on a separate day before diagnosis","A":"Random plasma glucose ≥200 mg/dL; in a patient with classic hyperglycemic signs this single result is diagnostic and is the one test that does not have to be repeated on a separate day","C":"Fasting plasma glucose of 100 to 125 mg/dL; when classic hyperglycemic signs accompany it, a single result in this range is enough to establish the diagnosis of diabetes mellitus","D":"Two-hour plasma glucose of 140 to 199 mg/dL after a 75-g oral glucose load; classic hyperglycemic signs make a single result in this range diagnostic of diabetes mellitus","E":"HbA1c of 5.7% to 6.4%; when classic hyperglycemic signs are present, a single result in this range establishes diabetes mellitus without any further confirmatory laboratory testing"},"explanation":"The course reading (Robbins, Endocrine SDL 29/33/38, p62) lists the ADA and WHO criteria as fasting plasma glucose ≥126 mg/dL, random plasma glucose ≥200 mg/dL in a patient with classic hyperglycemic signs, 2-hour glucose ≥200 mg/dL on a 75-g OGTT, or HbA1c ≥6.5%, and states that every test except the random glucose in a patient with classic signs must be repeated and confirmed on a separate day. First Aid 2025 likewise requires hyperglycemic symptoms for the random glucose criterion, and Katzung calls a fasting glucose ≥126 mg/dL or HbA1c ≥6.5% diagnostic if confirmed by repeat testing. A single fasting glucose of 126 mg/dL or more still needs confirmation on a separate day, even when symptoms are present. A fasting glucose of 100 to 125 mg/dL is impaired fasting glucose, a prediabetes range, not diabetes. A 2-hour OGTT value of 140 to 199 mg/dL is impaired glucose tolerance, a prediabetes range; the diabetes cutoff is 200 mg/dL. An HbA1c of 5.7% to 6.4% is the prediabetes range; the diabetes cutoff is 6.5%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diabetes = FPG ≥126 mg/dL, 2-hour OGTT ≥200 mg/dL, or HbA1c ≥6.5%, each confirmed on a separate day, or a random glucose ≥200 mg/dL with classic signs, which needs no repeat; prediabetes = FPG 100–125, 2-hour 140–199, HbA1c 5.7–6.4% (Robbins). COMLEX: polyuria, polydipsia and weight loss with a random glucose ≥200 mg/dL is diagnostic on a single test.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — Insulin-mediated hepatic and peripheral glucose regulation provides the physiological basis for fasting glucose, OGTT, and HbA1c diagnostic thresholds","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"A single fasting glucose of 126","C":"A fasting glucose of 100 to 125","D":"A 2-hour OGTT value of 140","E":"An HbA1c of 5.7% to 6.4%"}},{"id":"sdl29_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand how a diagnosis of diabetes mellitus is established by specific criteria.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best explains the biochemical basis for using hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c) as a long-term marker of glycemic control, and what time period does it reflect?","choices":{"A":"HbA1c reflects non-enzymatic glycosylation of hemoglobin proportional to average plasma glucose over the preceding 2–3 months, corresponding to the 120-day lifespan of red blood cells in circulation","B":"HbA1c reflects enzymatic phosphorylation of hemoglobin by hexokinase in proportion to intracellular glucose-6-phosphate levels, representing average glycemic control over the preceding 4–6 weeks of the patient's life","C":"HbA1c reflects competitive binding of glucose to the oxygen-carrying site on hemoglobin, reducing tissue oxygen delivery in a manner proportional to blood glucose over the preceding 3–4 days of plasma glucose elevation","D":"HbA1c reflects glycogen-bound glucose stored within erythrocytes, released during red blood cell senescence in a pattern that mirrors average glucose concentrations over the preceding 6–12 months of metabolic activity","E":"HbA1c reflects sorbitol accumulation within red blood cells via aldose reductase activity, producing osmotic stress proportional to chronic hyperglycemia over the preceding 1–2 months of elevated blood glucose levels"},"explanation":"HbA1c results from the non-enzymatic, irreversible glycosylation (Maillard reaction) of the N-terminal valine of the beta-globin chain of hemoglobin. Because red blood cells have a lifespan of ~120 days (4 months), HbA1c reflects average plasma glucose over 2–3 months (the most recent weeks contribute more). This makes it a superior long-term glycemic marker compared to single fasting glucose measurements. Diagnosis threshold: ≥6.5%; treatment target in T2D: typically <7%. HbA1c forms by nonenzymatic covalent addition of glucose to hemoglobin over the red cell's 120-day lifespan, not by enzymatic phosphorylation over 4 to 6 weeks. HbA1c is nonenzymatic covalent addition of glucose to hemoglobin reflecting the red cell's 120-day lifespan, not competitive binding at the oxygen site over a few days. HbA1c is glucose covalently attached to hemoglobin, not glycogen stored in red cells, and it reflects average glucose over about the previous 3 months. HbA1c is glucose covalently attached to hemoglobin; sorbitol made by aldose reductase belongs to the polyol pathway of tissue injury, not to HbA1c.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HbA1c is non-enzymatic glycosylation, NOT enzymatic. Falsely low HbA1c: hemolytic anemia, sickle cell disease (shortened RBC lifespan). Falsely elevated: iron deficiency anemia, renal failure. COMLEX: HbA1c ≥6.5% = diagnostic criterion; HbA1c below 7% is the usual treatment target (Robbins; Katzung).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — Insulin-mediated hepatic and peripheral glucose regulation provides the physiological basis for fasting glucose, OGTT, and HbA1c diagnostic thresholds","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"HbA1c forms by nonenzymatic covalent addition of glucose to ","C":"HbA1c is nonenzymatic covalent addition of glucose to hemogl","D":"HbA1c is glucose covalently attached to hemoglobin, not glyc","E":"HbA1c is glucose covalently attached to hemoglobin; sorbitol"}},{"id":"sdl29_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand how a diagnosis of diabetes mellitus is established by specific criteria.","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has an HbA1c of 7.1% on routine screening. He is asymptomatic. Which of the following best describes the correct next step in confirming his diagnosis of diabetes mellitus according to ADA guidelines?","choices":{"D":"Repeat the HbA1c test on a different day using a new sample, or perform a fasting plasma glucose or OGTT on a separate occasion, since a single HbA1c result without symptoms requires confirmation","A":"No further testing is needed because HbA1c ≥6.5% in an asymptomatic patient is categorically diagnostic of diabetes mellitus on a single occasion, requiring immediate initiation of metformin therapy and dietary counseling","B":"Order a serum C-peptide and fasting insulin level to distinguish type 1 from type 2 diabetes before confirmation is pursued, since treatment approach differs fundamentally between the two conditions based on insulin levels","C":"Repeat the HbA1c test in 3 months to establish a trend, since a single elevated result may reflect a transient physiologic stress response and the ADA requires two elevated measurements separated by 90 days for diagnosis","E":"Perform a 2-hour 100 g oral glucose tolerance test, since the 75 g OGTT is reserved for gestational diabetes screening and the 100 g load is the standard confirmatory test in asymptomatic non-pregnant adults"},"explanation":"The ADA requires confirmation of all abnormal screening tests (FPG, HbA1c, 2-hr OGTT) in asymptomatic individuals. This means repeating the same test or performing a different approved test on a different day. The only exception is a random glucose ≥200 mg/dL in the presence of classic hyperglycemic symptoms, which is immediately diagnostic. The 75 g OGTT is standard for non-pregnant adults (the 100 g load is used in GDM screening); repeat testing is not required to be 3 months later — a next-visit repeat is appropriate. Except for a random glucose in a patient with classic symptoms, an abnormal diagnostic test such as HbA1c must be repeated and confirmed on a separate day. Diagnosis rests on glucose or HbA1c criteria confirmed on a separate day; C-peptide and insulin levels are not part of the diagnostic criteria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Key rule — without symptoms, ALL tests (FPG, HbA1c, OGTT) need repeat confirmation. COMLEX: If both tests are above threshold, diagnosis is confirmed. If one is above and one is normal, repeat the abnormal test. GDM screening is usually done at 24–28 weeks of gestation (Katzung Ch 41).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — Insulin-mediated hepatic and peripheral glucose regulation provides the physiological basis for fasting glucose, OGTT, and HbA1c diagnostic thresholds","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Except for a random glucose in a patient with classic sympto","B":"Diagnosis rests on glucose or HbA1c criteria confirmed on a ","C":"The 75 g OGTT is standard for "}},{"id":"sdl29_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus type 1.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the major genetic susceptibility locus for type 1 diabetes mellitus, and what proportion of the total genetic susceptibility does it account for?","choices":{"A":"HLA region on chromosome 6p21, particularly HLA-DR3 and HLA-DR4 haplotypes, which together account for approximately 50% of the genetic susceptibility to type 1 diabetes mellitus","B":"Insulin gene (INS) VNTR on chromosome 11p15, which accounts for approximately 50% of genetic susceptibility by determining the level of thymic insulin expression and thereby regulating central tolerance to insulin antigens","C":"PTPN22 gene on chromosome 1p13, encoding a protein tyrosine phosphatase that inhibits T-cell receptor signaling, accounting for approximately 50% of T1D risk by causing constitutive autoreactive T-cell activation","D":"CTLA4 gene on chromosome 2q33, encoding a co-inhibitory receptor on regulatory T cells, accounting for approximately 50% of T1D risk by reducing Treg suppression of autoreactive effector T lymphocytes","E":"TCF7L2 gene on chromosome 10q25, encoding a transcription factor involved in Wnt signaling and beta-cell proliferation, accounting for approximately 50% of T1D risk through impaired beta-cell regeneration capacity"},"explanation":"The HLA region (chromosome 6p21) is the most important genetic susceptibility locus for T1D, accounting for ~50% of genetic risk. HLA-DR3 and HLA-DR4 haplotypes are most strongly associated (HLA-DR3/DR4 heterozygotes have the highest risk). The INS VNTR, PTPN22, and CTLA4 are additional susceptibility loci but individually account for much less. TCF7L2 is associated with T2D, not T1D. Monozygotic twin concordance in T1D is ~50%, indicating strong but incomplete genetic determination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HLA-DR3/DR4 → highest T1D risk. HLA-DQ also important. MZ concordance T1D ~50% (vs T2D ~90%) — this difference is key for distinguishing the two on exams. Non-HLA risk genes: INS, PTPN22, CTLA4, IL2RA (CD25). COMLEX: Environmental triggers (Coxsackie B4 virus, early cow milk exposure) proposed as initiators in genetically susceptible hosts.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Pancreatic beta-cell GSIS mechanism abolished when beta cells are autoimmunely destroyed in type 1 diabetes, producing absolute insulin deficiency","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"The INS VNTR, PTPN22, and CTLA","C":"The INS VNTR, PTPN22, and CTLA","D":"The INS VNTR, PTPN22, and CTLA","E":"TCF7L2 is associated with T2D,"}},{"id":"sdl29_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus type 1.","batch":1,"stem":"The natural history of type 1 diabetes mellitus is described as proceeding through three stages. Which of the following correctly characterizes Stage 3 of this progression?","choices":{"E":"Stage 3 is defined by the presence of multiple islet autoantibodies, dysglycemia, and symptomatic hyperglycemia with clinical features such as polyuria, polydipsia, and weight loss, corresponding to overt clinical diabetes","A":"Stage 3 is defined by the presence of islet autoantibodies with completely normal blood glucose on fasting and OGTT testing, representing immune activation without detectable beta-cell loss or any measurable glucose abnormality","B":"Stage 3 is defined by destruction of more than 90% of beta-cell mass in the absence of any circulating islet autoantibodies, representing the end-stage of autoimmune islet injury after the immune response has completely resolved","C":"Stage 3 is defined by the first detection of a single islet autoantibody alongside normal fasting glucose, representing immune sensitization before polyclonal autoimmune spread to additional beta-cell antigens has occurred","D":"Stage 3 is defined by complete beta-cell failure with undetectable C-peptide, absolute insulin dependence, and the first episode of diabetic ketoacidosis requiring hospitalization and intravenous insulin infusion therapy"},"explanation":"The three-stage model of T1D (Insel et al., 2015) defines: Stage 1 — multiple islet autoantibodies present with normoglycemia (pre-symptomatic); Stage 2 — multiple autoantibodies with dysglycemia (pre-symptomatic); Stage 3 — autoantibodies + dysglycemia + clinical symptoms (overt T1D). Stage 1 has normal glucose; Stage 2 has dysglycemia without symptoms; Stage 3 is the clinical presentation. Autoantibodies (anti-GAD65, anti-IA-2, anti-insulin, anti-ZnT8) are present from Stage 1 onward. Stage 3 is the appearance of symptomatic disease; islet autoantibodies are found in most patients with type 1 diabetes, so their absence does not define it. A single autoantibody with normal glucose is not Stage 3; Stage 1 is two or more autoantibodies with normoglycemia, and Stage 3 adds clinical symptoms. Stage 3 is the appearance of classic symptoms; residual insulin secretion can persist for 1 to 2 years afterward (the honeymoon period), so complete beta-cell failure is not required.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T1D autoantibodies: GAD65, IA-2/IA-2β (tyrosine phosphatase), insulin autoantibodies, ZnT8. Autoantibodies can precede symptoms by years. Stage 1 = 2+ autoantibodies + normoglycemia. COMLEX: Teplizumab (anti-CD3 mAb) delays progression from Stage 2 to Stage 3 — first FDA-approved T1D prevention drug.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Pancreatic beta-cell GSIS mechanism abolished when beta cells are autoimmunely destroyed in type 1 diabetes, producing absolute insulin deficiency","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Stage 1 has normal glucose; St","B":"Stage 3 is the appearance of symptomatic disease; islet auto","C":"A single autoantibody with normal glucose is not Stage 3; St","D":"Stage 3 is the appearance of classic symptoms; residual insu"}},{"id":"sdl29_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus type 1.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the immunological mechanism that underlies beta-cell destruction in type 1 diabetes mellitus?","choices":{"D":"Failure of central and peripheral self-tolerance activates autoreactive CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes and CD4+ Th1 helper cells that infiltrate the islets (insulitis) and directly destroy beta cells while sparing glucagon-secreting alpha cells","A":"Molecular mimicry between viral antigens and beta-cell surface proteins activates natural killer cells that secrete perforin and granzymes, bypassing T-cell-mediated immunity and selectively eliminating beta cells through innate immune pathways","B":"Type II hypersensitivity reaction mediated by IgG autoantibodies against insulin and GAD65 activates complement via the classical pathway, causing complement-mediated lysis of beta cells in a pattern consistent with antibody-dependent cytotoxicity","C":"T-regulatory cell hyperactivation leads to excessive IL-10 and TGF-β production, which paradoxically suppresses effector T-cell apoptosis and enables unchecked autoreactive lymphocyte expansion within the pancreatic islets","E":"Failure of self-tolerance activates autoreactive B lymphocytes that produce islet cell antibodies, which then cross-link beta-cell surface antigens to induce receptor-mediated endocytosis and intracellular degradation of beta-cell components"},"explanation":"T1D results from failure of self-tolerance, allowing autoreactive T cells to escape deletion in the thymus (central tolerance) and suppression in the periphery. CD8+ CTLs directly kill beta cells via perforin/granzyme; CD4+ Th1 cells amplify the response via IFN-γ and IL-2. This creates insulitis (inflammatory islet infiltrate). Alpha cells are spared because they do not express the target autoantigens. Islet autoantibodies are present but are primarily markers of the autoimmune process rather than the direct effectors of destruction. Beta-cell injury is caused by autoreactive T cells after failure of self-tolerance, Th1 cells releasing cytokines and CD8+ CTLs killing directly, not by NK cells bypassing T-cell immunity. Type 1 diabetes involves defective regulatory T-cell function or effector resistance to suppression, not regulatory T-cell hyperactivation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T1D = T-cell mediated (Type IV hypersensitivity), NOT antibody-mediated directly. Autoantibodies are markers, not effectors of destruction. Insulitis = lymphocytic infiltrate (CD4+ and CD8+ T cells). COMLEX: Regulatory T cell (Treg) deficiency is a key component — CTLA4 and IL2RA gene variants reduce Treg function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Pancreatic beta-cell GSIS mechanism abolished when beta cells are autoimmunely destroyed in type 1 diabetes, producing absolute insulin deficiency","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Beta-cell injury is caused by autoreactive T cells after fai","B":"Islet autoantibodies are prese","C":"Type 1 diabetes involves defective regulatory T-cell functio","E":"Islet autoantibodies are prese"}},{"id":"sdl29_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus type 2.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the two fundamental pathophysiological defects that together produce the hyperglycemia of type 2 diabetes mellitus?","choices":{"C":"Peripheral insulin resistance in skeletal muscle, liver, and adipose tissue combined with progressive beta-cell dysfunction that renders the pancreas unable to secrete sufficient insulin to compensate for the increased demand","A":"Absolute destruction of pancreatic beta cells by autoimmune mechanisms combined with compensatory alpha-cell hyperplasia that produces excess glucagon-mediated hepatic glucose output in the setting of complete insulinopenia","B":"Accelerated renal glucose reabsorption in the proximal tubule combined with defective hepatic glycogen synthesis, producing postprandial hyperglycemia that overwhelms peripheral glucose uptake despite preserved insulin secretion capacity","D":"Impaired glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) secretion from L-cells in the gut combined with excess gastric inhibitory polypeptide (GIP) production from K-cells, independently causing hyperglycemia without any primary defect in insulin action","E":"Central leptin resistance in the hypothalamus combined with peripheral adiponectin excess, which inhibits AMPK signaling in skeletal muscle and redirects fuel utilization away from glucose oxidation toward free fatty acid beta-oxidation"},"explanation":"T2D results from two interrelated defects: (1) insulin resistance — target tissues (muscle, liver, fat) respond inadequately to insulin, particularly impaired GLUT4 translocation in skeletal muscle and failure to suppress hepatic gluconeogenesis; and (2) beta-cell dysfunction — initially compensatory hyperinsulinemia develops, but over time lipotoxicity, glucotoxicity, amyloid deposition, and incretin defects cause progressive beta-cell failure. Neither defect alone is sufficient; both must coexist for overt T2D to develop. Autoimmune destruction of beta cells with complete insulinopenia describes type 1 diabetes, not the insulin resistance plus beta-cell dysfunction of type 2. Type 2 diabetes combines peripheral insulin resistance with an inadequate beta-cell secretory response, not accelerated renal glucose reabsorption with preserved insulin secretion. A reduced incretin effect contributes to beta-cell dysfunction in type 2 diabetes, but the disease also requires insulin resistance; it is not incretin change without any defect in insulin action. Adiponectin increases insulin sensitivity and is reduced in obesity, so adiponectin excess is not a type 2 defect; the pair is insulin resistance with beta-cell dysfunction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T2D = insulin resistance + beta-cell failure. Skeletal muscle = primary site of postprandial glucose disposal. Liver = primary source of fasting hyperglycemia (excess gluconeogenesis). COMLEX: \"Twin\" defect — insulin resistance AND impaired secretion; treating only one is insufficient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — GLUT4 translocation in skeletal muscle and hepatic gluconeogenesis suppression by insulin; failure of these processes defines peripheral insulin resistance in type 2 diabetes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Autoimmune destruction of beta cells with complete insulinop","B":"Type 2 diabetes combines peripheral insulin resistance with ","D":"A reduced incretin effect contributes to beta-cell dysfuncti","E":"Adiponectin increases insulin sensitivity and is reduced in "}},{"id":"sdl29_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus type 2.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the mechanisms by which pancreatic beta-cell dysfunction progresses in type 2 diabetes mellitus, beyond the initial phase of compensatory hyperinsulinemia?","choices":{"E":"Beta-cell failure in T2D involves glucotoxicity from sustained hyperglycemia, lipotoxicity from excess free fatty acid exposure, islet amyloid polypeptide (IAPP/amylin) deposition within islets, and a blunted incretin response, all of which cumulatively reduce functional beta-cell mass","A":"Beta-cell failure in T2D is caused solely by autoimmune insulitis identical to T1D, but proceeding at a slower pace over decades rather than years, ultimately resulting in the same absolute insulin deficiency seen in end-stage T1D","B":"Beta-cell failure in T2D occurs exclusively through mitochondrial uncoupling induced by excess ketone body production, which bypasses the normal glucose-sensing mechanism and decouples ATP synthesis from insulin secretion triggers","C":"Beta-cell failure in T2D results primarily from pancreatic duct obstruction by visceral fat accumulation in the head of the pancreas, compressing beta cells and impairing their access to arterial nutrients and oxygen supply","D":"Beta-cell failure in T2D is driven by excess glucagon secretion from alpha cells that directly inhibits beta-cell function through paracrine signaling, independent of glucose levels or free fatty acid concentrations in the portal circulation"},"explanation":"In T2D, beta-cell failure is multifactorial: glucotoxicity (chronic hyperglycemia generates ROS, ER stress, and UPR activation in beta cells), lipotoxicity (excess FFAs cause ceramide accumulation and apoptosis), IAPP/amylin deposition (forms fibrils in islets causing local toxicity), and blunted incretin response (reduced GLP-1 sensitivity and GIP responsiveness reduce glucose-stimulated insulin secretion). Together these mechanisms reduce functional beta-cell mass and secretory capacity over time. This is fundamentally different from the autoimmune destruction in T1D. Robbins lists lipotoxicity, glucotoxicity, an abnormal incretin effect and islet amyloid as causes of beta-cell dysfunction, not ketone-driven mitochondrial uncoupling; ketoacidosis is uncommon in type 2 diabetes. Beta-cell dysfunction in type 2 diabetes is attributed to lipotoxicity, glucotoxicity, an abnormal incretin effect and islet amyloid, not duct obstruction by visceral fat. Beta-cell dysfunction in type 2 diabetes is attributed to lipotoxicity, glucotoxicity, an abnormal incretin effect and islet amyloid, not paracrine inhibition by glucagon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IAPP (islet amyloid polypeptide = amylin) deposits are the morphological hallmark of T2D islets. GLP-1 response is blunted in T2D — basis for GLP-1 agonist therapy (semaglutide, liraglutide). Lipotoxicity also impairs GLUT2 expression. COMLEX: Pramlintide = synthetic amylin analog; exenatide/semaglutide = GLP-1 agonists that restore lost incretin response.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — GLUT4 translocation in skeletal muscle and hepatic gluconeogenesis suppression by insulin; failure of these processes defines peripheral insulin resistance in type 2 diabetes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"This is fundamentally differen","B":"Robbins lists lipotoxicity, glucotoxicity, an abnormal incre","C":"Beta-cell dysfunction in type 2 diabetes is attributed to lipotoxicity, glucotoxicity, an abnormal incretin effect and islet amyloid, not du","D":"Beta-cell dysfunction in type 2 diabetes is attributed to lipotoxicity, glucotoxicity, an abnormal incretin effect and islet amyloid, not pa"}},{"id":"sdl29_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus type 2.","batch":1,"stem":"In type 2 diabetes, insulin resistance in different tissues produces different parts of the metabolic abnormality. Which tissue defect contributes most to the elevated fasting blood glucose?","choices":{"D":"Failure of insulin to inhibit hepatic gluconeogenesis, because weak signaling lets phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase ramp up","A":"Failure of insulin to drive glucose uptake into skeletal muscle after meals, because GLUT4 translocation to the muscle cell surface is reduced","B":"Failure of insulin to inhibit hormone-sensitive lipase in adipose tissue, because stored triglyceride is broken down into free fatty acids","C":"Failure of insulin to stimulate glucose uptake into neurons, because brain GLUT4 is lost and the brain releases its glycogen into the blood","E":"Failure of insulin to suppress intestinal glucose absorption, because enterocyte SGLT1 rises with hyperinsulinemia"},"explanation":"Robbins states that the liver, skeletal muscle, and adipose tissue are the major tissues where insulin resistance gives rise to abnormal glucose tolerance, and assigns each a different result. Failure to inhibit endogenous glucose production (gluconeogenesis) in the liver contributes to high fasting blood glucose; insulin normally inhibits gluconeogenesis by blocking phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase, and attenuated insulin signaling lets this enzyme ramp up. Failure of glucose uptake and glycogen synthesis in skeletal muscle after a meal contributes to high postprandial glucose, not mainly fasting glucose. Failure to inhibit hormone-sensitive lipase in adipose tissue raises circulating free fatty acids. Most brain neurons do not depend on insulin for glucose uptake, so brain resistance is not a cause. Insulin does not regulate intestinal glucose absorption in this way.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Insulin resistance by tissue (Robbins): liver = fasting hyperglycemia (unchecked gluconeogenesis via phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase); muscle = postprandial hyperglycemia (less GLUT4 at the surface); adipose = high free fatty acids (unchecked hormone-sensitive lipase). COMLEX: Insulin resistance predates hyperglycemia and is first offset by beta-cell hypersecretion and hyperinsulinemia (Robbins).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — GLUT4 translocation in skeletal muscle and hepatic gluconeogenesis suppression by insulin","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"C":"Most brain neurons do not depe"}},{"id":"sdl29_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the role that obesity plays in insulin resistance.","batch":1,"stem":"Which adipose tissue distribution pattern is most strongly associated with insulin resistance, and why does this distribution confer greater metabolic risk than peripheral adiposity?","choices":{"A":"Pericardial adiposity is the most metabolically harmful distribution because cardiac muscle is the largest glucose-consuming organ in the body, and local lipid release from pericardial fat directly impairs myocardial insulin receptor function","B":"Subcutaneous gluteal-femoral adiposity is more strongly associated with insulin resistance because this depot is more directly innervated by sympathetic nerves that stimulate lipolysis and release fatty acids into the systemic circulation continuously","C":"Intramuscular adiposity (myosteatosis) is the primary determinant of insulin resistance because intramyocellular lipid droplets directly compete with glucose for mitochondrial oxidation, forcing skeletal muscle to rely exclusively on fat as its fuel source","D":"Visceral (intra-abdominal) adiposity is more strongly associated with insulin resistance because visceral fat is more metabolically active, releases free fatty acids and adipokines directly into the portal circulation, and contains more inflammatory macrophages","E":"Hepatic steatosis (NAFLD) is the primary cause of systemic insulin resistance because excess triglyceride accumulation in hepatocytes reduces the expression of insulin receptor substrate-2, eliminating hepatic insulin sensitivity before peripheral resistance develops"},"explanation":"Visceral adiposity confers greater metabolic risk than subcutaneous fat for several reasons: (1) visceral fat drains directly into the portal vein, delivering a high concentration of FFAs and inflammatory cytokines (TNF-α, IL-6) to the liver first; (2) visceral adipocytes are more lipolytically active and less sensitive to insulin's anti-lipolytic effect; (3) visceral fat contains more M1 macrophages (pro-inflammatory) relative to M2 macrophages; (4) visceral fat produces less adiponectin (insulin-sensitizing) relative to subcutaneous fat. Central abdominal (visceral) fat, which is more lipolytic than peripheral fat, is the distribution most linked to insulin resistance, not pericardial fat. Central abdominal fat is the more lipolytic depot and is more strongly linked to insulin resistance, whereas predominantly subcutaneous adiposity may be relatively protective. Robbins names central visceral adiposity, which is more lipolytic than peripheral fat, as the distribution most linked to insulin resistance, not intramuscular fat. Hepatic steatosis results from high circulating free fatty acids in the insulin-resistant state; it is a consequence, not the primary cause, of the visceral-fat-driven resistance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Visceral fat (omental, mesenteric) > subcutaneous fat for metabolic risk. Portal FFA flux → hepatic insulin resistance → increased hepatic glucose output. Central obesity is a component of metabolic syndrome. COMLEX: Body composition measurement (DEXA, waist-hip ratio) = osteopathic preventive medicine; visceral adiposity reversible with lifestyle modification before pharmacotherapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — Hormone-sensitive lipase inhibition by insulin; in obesity excess free fatty acids from uninhibited lipolysis drive the inflammatory cascade underlying insulin resistance","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Central abdominal (visceral) fat, which is more lipolytic th","B":"Central abdominal fat is the more lipolytic depot and is mor","C":"Robbins names central visceral adiposity, which is more lipo","E":"Hepatic steatosis results from high circulating free fatty a"}},{"id":"sdl29_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the role that obesity plays in insulin resistance.","batch":1,"stem":"Excess circulating free fatty acids in obese individuals contribute to insulin resistance in skeletal muscle through which of the following intracellular signaling mechanisms?","choices":{"B":"Free fatty acids are converted to diacylglycerol (DAG) and ceramides within myocytes, which activate protein kinase C-θ (PKC-θ) and serine kinases that phosphorylate IRS-1 on inhibitory serine residues, blocking downstream insulin signaling","A":"Free fatty acids bind directly to the insulin receptor extracellular domain and competitively displace insulin from its binding site, preventing receptor autophosphorylation and initiating a conformational change that locks the receptor in an inactive state","C":"Free fatty acids are incorporated into the inner mitochondrial membrane, uncoupling oxidative phosphorylation from ATP synthesis by allowing proton leak, which reduces the ATP/ADP ratio and prevents the ATP-sensitive insulin vesicle fusion required for GLUT4 trafficking","D":"Free fatty acids activate PPAR-γ in skeletal muscle, driving transcriptional upregulation of fatty acid oxidation genes that suppress glucose oxidation enzymes via competitive product inhibition of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex","E":"Free fatty acids inhibit acetyl-CoA carboxylase, reducing malonyl-CoA levels and allowing carnitine palmitoyltransferase-1 to import excess fatty acids into mitochondria, generating excess NADH that inhibits the TCA cycle and elevates glucose-6-phosphate"},"explanation":"Excess FFAs undergo intracellular metabolism to DAG and ceramides, which are bioactive lipid intermediates. DAG activates PKC-θ (the predominant PKC isoform in skeletal muscle), which phosphorylates IRS-1 on serine residues. This serine phosphorylation converts IRS-1 from a signal amplifier to an inhibitor of insulin receptor kinase activity, blocking PI3K→Akt→GLUT4 translocation. Ceramides independently activate PP2A (dephosphorylates Akt) and activate JNK. Together these mechanisms create lipid-induced insulin resistance. Excess fatty acids act inside cells, where diacylglycerol and ceramides accumulate and attenuate insulin receptor signaling; they do not displace insulin from its receptor. Excess fatty acids impair insulin signaling through accumulated diacylglycerol and ceramides, not through mitochondrial uncoupling that blocks an ATP-dependent vesicle fusion step. PPAR-gamma activation, the action of pioglitazone, increases insulin sensitivity rather than causing resistance; fatty acids impair signaling through accumulated diacylglycerol and ceramides. Excess fatty acids overwhelm oxidation pathways so diacylglycerol and ceramides accumulate and attenuate insulin signaling, rather than causing resistance through increased mitochondrial fatty acid import.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FFA → DAG/ceramide → PKC-θ/JNK → serine phosphorylation of IRS-1 → blocked insulin signaling. Randle cycle (glucose-fatty acid cycle) is a separate but related concept — fatty acid oxidation raises acetyl-CoA/CoA and NADH/NAD+ ratios, allosterically inhibiting PDH and reducing glucose oxidation. COMLEX: Exercise reduces intramyocellular DAG and ceramide content — mechanistic basis for exercise as T2D prevention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — Hormone-sensitive lipase inhibition by insulin; in obesity excess free fatty acids from uninhibited lipolysis drive the inflammatory cascade underlying insulin resistance","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Excess fatty acids act inside cells, where diacylglycerol an","C":"Excess fatty acids impair insulin signaling through accumula","D":"PPAR-gamma activation, the action of pioglitazone, increases","E":"Excess fatty acids overwhelm oxidation pathways so diacylgly"}},{"id":"sdl29_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the role that obesity plays in insulin resistance.","batch":1,"stem":"Which adipokine has its circulating levels inversely correlated with visceral adiposity, and what is its normal metabolic function that is lost when its levels are reduced in obesity?","choices":{"E":"Adiponectin levels are inversely correlated with visceral adiposity; at normal concentrations adiponectin activates AMPK and PPAR-α in muscle and liver, enhancing fatty acid oxidation, increasing insulin sensitivity, and suppressing hepatic gluconeogenesis","A":"Leptin levels are inversely correlated with visceral adiposity; at normal concentrations leptin suppresses hypothalamic NPY/AgRP neurons to reduce appetite and activates peripheral GLUT4 translocation to stimulate insulin-independent glucose uptake","B":"Resistin levels are inversely correlated with visceral adiposity; at normal concentrations resistin activates IRS-2 phosphorylation in hepatocytes, promoting glycogen synthesis and suppressing gluconeogenesis in a manner synergistic with insulin receptor signaling","C":"Visfatin levels are inversely correlated with visceral adiposity; at normal concentrations visfatin binds the insulin receptor at a site distinct from insulin and activates downstream PI3K-Akt signaling to stimulate glucose uptake in insulin-resistant adipocytes","D":"TNF-α levels are inversely correlated with visceral adiposity; at normal concentrations TNF-α activates adipose tissue lipolysis in a controlled manner that prevents lipid overflow into non-adipose tissues while maintaining adequate fuel availability during fasting"},"explanation":"Adiponectin is an insulin-sensitizing adipokine secreted predominantly by subcutaneous (not visceral) adipocytes. Its levels are paradoxically low in obese individuals despite excess adipose tissue. Adiponectin activates AMPK (the cellular energy sensor) and PPAR-α, which together promote fatty acid oxidation in muscle, reduce hepatic gluconeogenesis, and enhance insulin sensitivity. Hypoadiponectinemia in obesity contributes significantly to insulin resistance. Leptin levels increase with adiposity (not decrease); resistin and TNF-α increase with obesity; adiponectin is uniquely inversely correlated. Visfatin is produced by visceral fat and its level correlates with visceral fat mass, so it is not the adipokine inversely related to visceral adiposity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Adiponectin = insulin-sensitizing, anti-inflammatory, anti-atherogenic. Low in obesity, T2D, metabolic syndrome. Activates AdipoR1 (muscle) and AdipoR2 (liver) → AMPK activation. Contrast: leptin ↑ with obesity (but leptin resistance develops); TNF-α ↑ with obesity; adiponectin ↓ with obesity. COMLEX: Thiazolidinediones (rosiglitazone, pioglitazone) increase adiponectin levels — one mechanism of insulin sensitization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — Hormone-sensitive lipase inhibition by insulin; in obesity excess free fatty acids from uninhibited lipolysis drive the inflammatory cascade underlying insulin resistance","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Leptin levels increase with ad","B":"Leptin levels increase with ad","C":"Visfatin is produced by visceral fat and its level correlate","D":"Leptin levels increase with ad"}},{"id":"sdl29_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus complications.","batch":1,"stem":"Advanced glycation end-products (AGEs) formed during chronic hyperglycemia mediate vascular complications of diabetes through which of the following mechanisms?","choices":{"B":"AGEs bind RAGE receptors on endothelial cells, macrophages, and vascular smooth muscle cells, activating NF-κB signaling that upregulates TGF-β, VEGF, and ROS production, promoting matrix expansion, angiogenesis, and procoagulant activity","A":"AGEs directly inhibit nitric oxide synthase in endothelial cells, reducing vasodilation through a mechanism independent of receptor activation, primarily affecting large arteries by preventing prostaglandin-mediated smooth muscle relaxation","C":"AGEs activate the polyol pathway in endothelial cells, converting glucose to sorbitol via aldose reductase and then to fructose via sorbitol dehydrogenase, depleting NADPH and reducing glutathione regeneration in vascular walls","D":"AGEs cross-link type IV collagen in the glomerular basement membrane through non-enzymatic reactions that are independent of receptor signaling, directly reducing filtration selectivity without requiring activation of growth factor pathways","E":"AGEs are internalized by proximal tubular cells and activate the hexosamine pathway, causing O-GlcNAc modification of transcription factors that upregulates aldose reductase expression and amplifies osmotic injury in the diabetic kidney"},"explanation":"AGEs are formed when glucose reacts non-enzymatically with amino groups on proteins and lipids (Maillard reaction/Amadori products → AGEs). When AGEs bind RAGE (receptor for AGEs), they activate NF-κB, producing: TGF-β (promotes collagen deposition and basement membrane thickening), VEGF (pathological angiogenesis in retinopathy), ROS (endothelial injury), and procoagulant factors (tissue factor, PAI-1). AGEs also directly cross-link collagen (RAGE-independent), but the most clinically significant effects are receptor-mediated. This mechanism underlies diabetic nephropathy, retinopathy, and accelerated atherosclerosis. AGEs act mainly by binding RAGE on endothelium, inflammatory cells and smooth muscle, releasing TGF-beta and VEGF and generating ROS, not by receptor-independent nitric oxide synthase inhibition. The polyol pathway, aldose reductase converting glucose to sorbitol with NADPH depletion, is a separate mechanism of hyperglycemic injury and not how AGEs act. AGEs act by binding RAGE; hexosamine pathway flux and the polyol pathway are separate mechanisms of hyperglycemic injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Four pathways of hyperglycemic damage: (1) AGE-RAGE → NF-κB → TGF-β/VEGF/ROS; (2) Polyol pathway → sorbitol/depletes NADPH; (3) PKC activation → VEGF/TGF-β; (4) Hexosamine pathway → O-GlcNAc modification. All four converge on mitochondrial ROS overproduction. COMLEX: AGE-RAGE system is the target of drugs in development; current best prevention = strict glycemic control.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — Chronic hyperglycemia from failed insulin action drives non-enzymatic glycosylation forming AGEs that mediate the vascular and renal complications of diabetes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"AGEs act mainly by binding RAGE on endothelium, inflammatory","C":"The polyol pathway, aldose reductase converting glucose to s","D":"AGEs also directly cross-link ","E":"AGEs act by binding RAGE; hexosamine pathway flux and the po"}},{"id":"sdl29_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus complications.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following glomerular histopathological findings is most characteristic of diabetic nephropathy and distinguishes it from other causes of nephrotic syndrome?","choices":{"D":"Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules — nodular deposits of laminated matrix material within the mesangium of glomeruli, representing nodular glomerulosclerosis, are highly characteristic of diabetic nephropathy","A":"Diffuse mesangial hypercellularity with IgA immune complex deposition in the mesangium, identified by immunofluorescence microscopy, representing immune-mediated glomerulonephritis triggered by recurrent infections in immunosuppressed diabetic patients","B":"Linear IgG deposits along the glomerular basement membrane detected by immunofluorescence microscopy, indicating anti-GBM antibody-mediated disease (Goodpasture syndrome) that is disproportionately prevalent in patients with longstanding diabetes mellitus","C":"Segmental hyalinosis of the glomerular tuft with collapse of the glomerular capillary loops, representing focal segmental glomerulosclerosis caused by hyperfiltration-mediated mechanical injury to the most vulnerable glomeruli in diabetic kidneys","E":"Spike-and-dome deposits of IgG and C3 along the subepithelial aspect of the glomerular basement membrane detected by electron microscopy, indicating membranous nephropathy accelerated by complement dysregulation in hyperglycemic states"},"explanation":"Kimmelstiel-Wilson (KW) nodules are the most characteristic lesion of diabetic nephropathy. They are ovoid, acellular, laminated deposits of extracellular matrix material (predominantly type IV collagen) within the glomerular mesangium, located at the periphery of the lobule. They represent the nodular form of glomerulosclerosis (vs. diffuse mesangial sclerosis, which is more common but less specific). On light microscopy: PAS-positive laminated nodules. Electron microscopy: basement membrane thickening and mesangial expansion. Neither IgA, anti-GBM, FSGS, nor membranous nephropathy is specific to diabetes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: KW nodules = most characteristic lesion of DM nephropathy. Morphology spectrum: BM thickening (earliest) → diffuse mesangial sclerosis (most common) → nodular glomerulosclerosis (Kimmelstiel-Wilson, highly characteristic). Also: both afferent AND efferent arteriolar hyalinosis (efferent involvement is unique to DM). COMLEX: Diabetic nephropathy is the #1 cause of ESRD in the US; microalbuminuria (30–300 mg/day) is the earliest clinical marker.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — Chronic hyperglycemia from failed insulin action drives non-enzymatic glycosylation forming AGEs that mediate the vascular and renal complications of diabetes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Neither IgA, anti-GBM, FSGS, n","B":"Neither IgA, anti-GBM, FSGS, n","C":"Neither IgA, anti-GBM, FSGS, n","E":"Neither IgA, anti-GBM, FSGS, n"}},{"id":"sdl29_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus complications.","batch":1,"stem":"Through which pathological mechanism does the polyol pathway contribute to cataract formation and peripheral neuropathy in patients with chronically elevated blood glucose levels?","choices":{"C":"Aldose reductase converts excess glucose to sorbitol using NADPH; sorbitol accumulates in cells lacking sorbitol dehydrogenase (lens, Schwann cells), causing osmotic swelling, while NADPH depletion impairs glutathione regeneration and redox defense","A":"Aldose reductase phosphorylates glucose to glucose-6-phosphate within the lens and Schwann cells, trapping it intracellularly and preventing its exit, causing progressive intracellular glucose accumulation that directly denatures crystallin proteins","B":"Aldose reductase catalyzes the conversion of fructose-6-phosphate to glucosamine-6-phosphate, activating the hexosamine pathway and causing O-GlcNAc modification of cytoskeletal proteins in lens epithelial cells and axonal transport proteins in neurons","D":"Aldose reductase activates protein kinase C-β by increasing diacylglycerol production from sorbitol, which then phosphorylates connexin-43 gap junction proteins in the lens and voltage-gated sodium channels in peripheral nerve axons","E":"Aldose reductase converts sorbitol directly to advanced glycation end-products without requiring sorbitol dehydrogenase or fructose as an intermediate, cross-linking lens crystallins and peripheral myelin basic protein through non-enzymatic mechanisms"},"explanation":"The polyol pathway involves two enzymes: (1) aldose reductase (NADPH-dependent) converts glucose → sorbitol; (2) sorbitol dehydrogenase (NAD+-dependent) converts sorbitol → fructose. In tissues with high aldose reductase but low sorbitol dehydrogenase (lens epithelium, Schwann cells, retinal pericytes, kidney mesangial cells), sorbitol accumulates. Consequences: osmotic cell swelling, NADPH depletion (reducing glutathione regeneration → oxidative stress), and NAD+ depletion (impairing NO synthesis). Cataracts, peripheral neuropathy, and retinopathy are all partly mediated by this pathway. Aldose reductase converts glucose to sorbitol using NADPH; it does not phosphorylate glucose to glucose-6-phosphate, which is hexokinase's role. Aldose reductase converts glucose to sorbitol; the hexosamine pathway is a separate mechanism of hyperglycemic injury. Aldose reductase converts glucose to sorbitol, not to diacylglycerol; hyperglycemia drives PKC through de novo DAG synthesis from glycolytic intermediates, a separate mechanism. Sorbitol is converted on to fructose by sorbitol dehydrogenase, whereas AGEs form when glucose-derived metabolites such as glyoxal and methylglyoxal react with proteins, not directly from sorbitol.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Polyol pathway = aldose reductase (glucose→sorbitol) + sorbitol dehydrogenase (sorbitol→fructose). Sorbitol accumulates in: lens (cataracts), retinal pericytes, Schwann cells (neuropathy), mesangial cells (nephropathy). NADPH depletion → reduced glutathione → oxidative stress. Aldose reductase inhibitors studied in clinical trials but not FDA-approved. COMLEX: Diabetes = leading cause of adult blindness, ESRD, nontraumatic amputations in the US.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — Chronic hyperglycemia from failed insulin action drives non-enzymatic glycosylation forming AGEs that mediate the vascular and renal complications of diabetes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Aldose reductase converts glucose to sorbitol using NADPH; i","B":"Aldose reductase converts glucose to sorbitol; the hexosamin","D":"Aldose reductase converts glucose to sorbitol, not to diacyl","E":"Sorbitol is converted on to fructose by sorbitol dehydrogena"}},{"id":"sdl29_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define Prediabetes, Diabetes Mellitus and its classification.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman is referred for evaluation of asymptomatic hyperglycemia discovered during a pre-employment physical examination. Fasting plasma glucose is 112 mg/dL confirmed on repeat. BMI is 21 kg/m². She has no polyuria, polydipsia, or weight loss. Her mother and maternal grandfather were both diagnosed with \"mild diabetes\" in their early 20s that required no medication. Hemoglobin A1c is 6.1%. Anti-GAD65 and anti-IA-2 antibodies are negative. Genetic testing reveals a heterozygous loss-of-function mutation in the GCK gene. Which of the following is the most appropriate management recommendation for this patient?","choices":{"E":"Lifestyle counseling and reassurance without pharmacotherapy; GCK-MODY (MODY2) causes stable, mild hyperglycemia that rarely progresses or causes microvascular complications, and pharmacologic treatment does not meaningfully improve outcomes","A":"Initiate metformin 500 mg twice daily with uptitration as tolerated, since the documented fasting hyperglycemia and HbA1c of 6.1% meet the prediabetes threshold and current guidelines recommend metformin in young patients with prediabetes at high progression risk","B":"Refer for endocrinology evaluation to initiate basal insulin therapy, since the autoantibody-negative status in a young lean patient raises concern for latent autoimmune diabetes in adults that has progressed beyond the window of GLP-1 agonist effectiveness","C":"Initiate sulfonylurea therapy with close blood glucose monitoring, since GCK-MODY patients respond well to sulfonylureas by bypassing the glucokinase sensing defect and directly stimulating insulin secretion through KATP channel closure in beta cells","D":"Schedule repeat HbA1c testing in 3 months and order a 2-hour 75 g OGTT, since confirmation of the genetic diagnosis requires demonstration of glucose values in the diabetes range and HbA1c ≥6.5% on a second occasion before genetic results influence management"},"explanation":"MODY2 (GCK-MODY) is caused by glucokinase haploinsufficiency, which raises the glucose set-point for insulin secretion. This produces mild, stable fasting hyperglycemia and rarely causes microvascular complications or progresses to overt T2D. Unlike MODY3 (HNF-1α), MODY2 does not respond meaningfully to sulfonylureas or other agents because the defect is in glucose sensing, not insulin secretory machinery. Treatment guidelines recommend lifestyle counseling and reassurance — pharmacotherapy is not indicated outside of pregnancy. The 2025 ADA Standards of Care describe GCK-MODY as stable, nonprogressive fasting hyperglycemia that typically does not require treatment, with rare microvascular complications. Her hyperglycemia comes from a glucokinase mutation that alters the insulin-secretion set point and does not produce typical diabetic complications, rather than from insulin-resistant prediabetes. LADA is distinguished from type 2 diabetes by one or more diabetes autoantibodies, so negative anti-GAD65 and anti-IA-2 with a GCK mutation argue against it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MODY2 = GCK mutation → stable mild hyperglycemia, no treatment needed (except in pregnancy). MODY3 = HNF-1α → progressive, responds to sulfonylureas. MODY1 = HNF-4α → similar to MODY3. Key discriminator: MODY2 has flat glucose curve on OGTT; other MODYs have progressive rise. COMLEX: Genetic testing for MODY indicated when: young-onset DM, no autoantibodies, family history of DM in 2+ generations, not obese.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — Diabetes Mellitus Classification: MODY subtypes and their distinct pathophysiological mechanisms","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Her hyperglycemia comes from a glucokinase mutation that alt","B":"LADA is distinguished from type 2 diabetes by one or more di","C":"Unlike MODY3 (HNF-1α), MODY2 d"}},{"id":"sdl29_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define Prediabetes, Diabetes Mellitus and its classification.","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old boy presents to the emergency department with a 3-day history of polyuria, polydipsia, and vomiting. He has lost 8 pounds over the past 3 weeks. His BMI is 19 kg/m². Blood glucose is 487 mg/dL, serum pH is 7.22, bicarbonate is 12 mEq/L, and urine ketones are large. Anion gap is 24. Anti-GAD65 antibodies are positive. A 47-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with fatigue and blurry vision. BMI is 34 kg/m². Blood glucose is 298 mg/dL, HbA1c is 9.4%, and urine ketones are trace. pH is 7.38. Anti-GAD65 antibodies are negative. Which of the following best distinguishes the pathophysiology of these two patients' conditions?","choices":{"C":"The adolescent has type 1 DM with absolute insulin deficiency from autoimmune beta-cell destruction, making him DKA-prone because absent insulin allows unopposed glucagon-driven ketogenesis; the adult has type 2 DM with relative insulin deficiency and residual insulin that suppresses maximal ketogenesis","A":"The adolescent has type 2 DM with more severe insulin resistance due to growth hormone excess in puberty, which transiently produces ketosis-prone diabetes until linear growth is complete and GH levels normalize in early adulthood","B":"The adolescent has type 1 DM presenting with HHS rather than DKA based on his profound hyperglycemia and osmotic symptoms, while the adult has type 1 DM in the honeymoon phase with preserved beta-cell function preventing ketoacidosis","D":"Both patients have type 2 DM, but the adolescent has ketosis-prone type 2 DM (Flatbush diabetes) common in African Americans, which presents with DKA at onset but does not require long-term insulin therapy after metabolic stabilization","E":"The adolescent has MODY3 presenting acutely because HNF-1α mutations cause severe insulin secretory failure indistinguishable from T1D, while the adult has T2D with metabolic syndrome and is at higher risk for HHS than for DKA in future crises"},"explanation":"T1D is characterized by absolute insulin deficiency from autoimmune beta-cell destruction (positive anti-GAD65, lean, young, DKA-prone). Even trace insulin in T2D (relative deficiency) suppresses hepatic ketogenesis by inhibiting hormone-sensitive lipase in adipose tissue — preventing the massive FFA mobilization that fuels DKA. T2D patients present with HHS (hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state) rather than DKA because residual insulin, though insufficient for glucose control, is enough to prevent ketoacidosis. C-peptide is low/absent in T1D and normal/elevated early in T2D. Ketoacidosis with positive anti-GAD65 reflects autoimmune type 1 diabetes with absolute insulin deficiency, not pubertal growth hormone insulin resistance. Positive anti-GAD65 marks autoimmune type 1 diabetes, whereas type 2 has no islet autoantibodies and more often presents with the hyperosmolar state than ketoacidosis. Positive anti-GAD65 marks autoimmune type 1 diabetes; MODY is a monogenic beta-cell defect resembling type 2 diabetes, not an autoimmune process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T1D → absolute insulin deficiency → DKA. T2D → relative insulin deficiency → HHS. Key distinction: In HHS, glucose often >600 mg/dL, osmolality >320 mOsm/kg, minimal ketones, pH normal. In DKA: glucose >250 and usually <600 (Bootcamp), anion gap acidosis, ketonemia. COMLEX: DKA treatment = IV fluids + insulin drip + K+ replacement (K+ drops with insulin); HHS = aggressive rehydration is primary.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — Diabetes Mellitus Classification: Type 1 vs Type 2 distinctions in insulin deficiency and ketogenesis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Ketoacidosis with positive anti-GAD65 reflects autoimmune ty","D":"Positive anti-GAD65 marks autoimmune type 1 diabetes, wherea","E":"Positive anti-GAD65 marks autoimmune type 1 diabetes; MODY i"}},{"id":"sdl29_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define Prediabetes, Diabetes Mellitus and its classification.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman at 26 weeks of gestation is referred for evaluation after a 1-hour 50 g glucose challenge test result of 158 mg/dL. She has no personal history of diabetes. Pre-pregnancy BMI was 27 kg/m². She undergoes a 3-hour 100 g oral glucose tolerance test (Carpenter-Coustan criteria). Results: fasting glucose 96 mg/dL, 1-hour 192 mg/dL, 2-hour 164 mg/dL, 3-hour 148 mg/dL. Which of the following best explains why gestational diabetes mellitus increases the risk of macrosomia in the fetus?","choices":{"B":"Maternal hyperglycemia allows excess glucose to cross the placenta, stimulating fetal pancreatic beta cells to secrete increased insulin (fetal hyperinsulinemia), which acts as a growth factor promoting adipogenesis and organomegaly in fetal tissues","A":"Maternal hyperglycemia activates placental GLUT1 transporters that competitively exclude amino acids from fetal uptake, paradoxically reducing fetal protein synthesis while excess glucose is preferentially stored as structural polysaccharides in fetal bone","C":"Maternal insulin crosses the placenta and directly stimulates fetal adipocyte GLUT4 receptors, causing fetal fat deposition independent of fetal insulin secretion, with the degree of macrosomia proportional to maternal insulin dose and duration","D":"Maternal hyperglycemia activates fetal hepatic glucokinase, causing excess fetal glycogen synthesis that accumulates in the fetal liver and skeletal muscle, increasing overall body weight without activating fetal insulin secretion pathways","E":"Fetal hyperglycemia activates placental growth hormone secretion, which stimulates fetal IGF-1 production independently of insulin signaling, producing organomegaly through a growth hormone-dependent pathway that does not involve fetal beta cells"},"explanation":"The Pedersen hypothesis explains GDM macrosomia: maternal hyperglycemia → glucose freely crosses placenta (GLUT1-mediated, not insulin-dependent) → fetal hyperglycemia → fetal beta-cell hyperstimulation → fetal hyperinsulinemia → insulin acts as a fetal growth factor (promotes adipogenesis, protein synthesis, organomegaly). Fetal insulin cannot cross back to the mother. Complications: macrosomia (shoulder dystocia risk), neonatal hypoglycemia at delivery (when maternal glucose source removed but fetal hyperinsulinemia persists), respiratory distress syndrome, polycythemia. Glucose crosses the placenta via GLUT1 and raises fetal insulin, which drives macrosomia; amino acid exclusion and polysaccharide storage in bone are not the mechanism. Unlike glucose, maternal insulin does not cross the placenta; the excess glucose that crosses raises fetal insulin. Macrosomia depends on fetal insulin: excess maternal glucose crosses the placenta and greatly raises fetal insulin, with fetal islet hyperplasia, rather than bypassing fetal insulin secretion. Macrosomia follows excess maternal glucose crossing the placenta and raising fetal insulin, with fetal islet hyperplasia, not a growth hormone pathway independent of fetal beta cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GDM → Pedersen hypothesis → fetal hyperinsulinemia → macrosomia. GDM is diagnosed by a glucose tolerance test during pregnancy (screening usually at 24–28 weeks, Katzung Ch 41). GDM ↑ risk of later T2D in the mother. COMLEX: Neonatal hypoglycemia = most common immediate complication of GDM; check neonatal glucose at delivery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — Diabetes Mellitus Classification: Gestational diabetes and the Pedersen hypothesis for fetal macrosomia","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Glucose crosses the placenta via GLUT1 and raises fetal insu","C":"Unlike glucose, maternal insulin does not cross the placenta","D":"Macrosomia depends on fetal insulin: excess maternal glucose","E":"Macrosomia follows excess maternal glucose crossing the plac"}},{"id":"sdl29_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand how a diagnosis of diabetes mellitus is established by specific criteria.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with well-controlled type 2 diabetes presents for a routine follow-up visit. His internist notes that his HbA1c is 7.2% but his glucose diary (fasting and 2-hour post-meal readings) averages about 125 mg/dL over the past 3 months. The physician explains that the two values appear discordant. The patient is also found to have a hemoglobin of 10.1 g/dL with a low MCV and low serum ferritin on routine labs. Which of the following best explains the discordance between this patient's HbA1c and fasting glucose values?","choices":{"E":"Iron deficiency anemia prolongs red blood cell lifespan and increases the proportion of older, more glycated erythrocytes in circulation, falsely elevating HbA1c relative to the actual average glucose reflected by his glucose diary readings","A":"Metformin therapy in this patient selectively inhibits aldose reductase within erythrocytes, reducing the enzymatic conversion of HbA1 to HbA1c by competing for the same NADPH cofactor and producing a falsely low HbA1c reading relative to true glucose control","B":"The discordance reflects dawn phenomenon, in which cortisol and growth hormone surges at 4–8 AM produce fasting hyperglycemia that disproportionately elevates the fasting plasma glucose without contributing to HbA1c because glycosylation only occurs postprandially","C":"Hemolytic anemia caused by metformin-induced oxidative stress shortens RBC lifespan, resulting in a higher proportion of young erythrocytes with lower glycosylation despite the same average glucose, producing a falsely low HbA1c in relation to glucose control","D":"The patient's CKD causes carbamylation of hemoglobin that artificially cross-reacts with the HbA1c assay, producing a falsely elevated HbA1c value that overestimates the degree of glycemic exposure in the preceding 3 months"},"explanation":"HbA1c reflects cumulative glycation proportional to RBC lifespan. Conditions that shorten RBC lifespan (hemolytic anemia, sickle cell disease, G6PD deficiency) produce falsely LOW HbA1c because there are fewer older (more glycated) cells. Conversely, conditions that prolong RBC lifespan (iron deficiency anemia, vitamin B12/folate deficiency) accumulate older, more glycated cells, producing falsely HIGH HbA1c relative to actual glucose. This patient's HbA1c of 7.2% is artificially elevated by iron deficiency; his true glycemic control may be better than 7.2% suggests. In the ADA Standards of Care eAG table, an HbA1c of 6% matches an average glucose of about 126 mg/dL and 7% about 154 mg/dL, so his 7.2% sits well above his diary average; the ADA lists iron status among factors that alter HbA1c and notes higher values with anemia and reduced red cell turnover. HbA1c forms by nonenzymatic addition of glucose to hemoglobin, so no red cell enzyme such as aldose reductase converts HbA1 to HbA1c. HbA1c reflects the average glucose a red cell is exposed to over its lifespan, so fasting hyperglycemia counts too; glycation is not limited to after meals.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Falsely LOW HbA1c: hemolytic anemia, sickle cell, G6PD deficiency, blood transfusion, splenomegaly. Falsely HIGH HbA1c: iron deficiency, B12/folate deficiency, renal failure, alcoholism. In these settings, fructosamine is preferred for monitoring. COMLEX: In patients with hemoglobinopathies or hemolytic conditions, use fructosamine or continuous glucose monitoring instead.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Diabetes Diagnosis: Conditions that produce falsely elevated or falsely low HbA1c values","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"HbA1c forms by nonenzymatic addition of glucose to hemoglobi","B":"HbA1c reflects the average glucose a red cell is exposed to "}},{"id":"sdl29_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand how a diagnosis of diabetes mellitus is established by specific criteria.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with a BMI of 31 kg/m² and hypertension presents for a routine physical. Fasting plasma glucose is 108 mg/dL. His physician orders a 2-hour 75 g oral glucose tolerance test. The 2-hour post-load glucose result is 188 mg/dL. He has no symptoms of hyperglycemia. Which of the following best characterizes his diagnosis and the appropriate next step?","choices":{"B":"He has impaired glucose tolerance (prediabetes), defined as a 2-hour OGTT glucose of 140–199 mg/dL; no confirmatory testing is needed for prediabetes, and intensive lifestyle modification (diet and regular moderate-intensity exercise) is first-line","A":"He has diabetes mellitus, defined by a 2-hour OGTT glucose ≥180 mg/dL in a symptomatic patient; the diagnosis is confirmed on this single occasion and metformin should be started immediately alongside referral for diabetes self-management education","C":"He has impaired fasting glucose only, defined by a fasting plasma glucose 100–125 mg/dL; the 2-hour OGTT value of 188 mg/dL does not meet the diabetes threshold of ≥200 mg/dL and does not independently contribute to his prediabetes diagnosis","D":"He has both impaired fasting glucose and impaired glucose tolerance but requires a repeat OGTT on a different day to confirm the prediabetes diagnosis before initiating lifestyle counseling, per ADA guidelines for asymptomatic individuals","E":"He has normal glucose tolerance because the 2-hour OGTT threshold for diabetes requires both the fasting value ≥126 mg/dL and the 2-hour value ≥200 mg/dL to be simultaneously abnormal, and his fasting glucose of 108 mg/dL is below the diabetes threshold"},"explanation":"This patient meets criteria for both impaired fasting glucose (IFG: FPG 100–125 mg/dL) and impaired glucose tolerance (IGT: 2-hr OGTT 140–199 mg/dL). Having both IFG + IGT confers higher risk of T2D progression than either alone. Prediabetes does not require confirmatory repeat testing (unlike the diabetes diagnosis criteria). The 2-hr OGTT threshold for diabetes is ≥200 mg/dL (his is 188 — below the diabetes cutoff). Lifestyle intervention reduces progression, and metformin can be added in high-risk individuals.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Prediabetes thresholds — IFG: FPG 100–125; IGT: 2-hr OGTT 140–199; HbA1c 5.7–6.4%. Diabetes: FPG ≥126, 2-hr OGTT ≥200, HbA1c ≥6.5%. Coexistence of IFG+IGT = highest prediabetes risk category. COMLEX: lifestyle counseling is first-line for prediabetes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Diabetes Diagnosis: Prediabetes criteria and risk stratification using IFG plus IGT","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"The 2-hr OGTT threshold for di","D":"Prediabetes does not require c"}},{"id":"sdl29_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand how a diagnosis of diabetes mellitus is established by specific criteria.","batch":2,"stem":"A 51-year-old woman with a family history of type 2 diabetes undergoes routine screening. Her results are: fasting plasma glucose 131 mg/dL and HbA1c 6.3%. She denies polyuria, polydipsia, or unintentional weight loss. Per ADA guidelines, which of the following best describes how to interpret these discordant results and how to proceed?","choices":{"A":"The FPG meets the diabetes threshold (≥126 mg/dL) while the HbA1c falls in the prediabetes range (5.7–6.4%); when two different tests are discordant, the ADA recommends repeating the test that is above the diagnostic threshold to confirm the diagnosis of diabetes","B":"Both results must simultaneously fall in the diabetes range on a single occasion before the diagnosis can be established; since only one of the two tests meets the diabetes threshold and the patient is asymptomatic, the diagnosis cannot be made until both converge","C":"The discordant results indicate the patient has prediabetes, since the HbA1c represents a more accurate 3-month average and should take precedence over the fasting glucose when the two measurements disagree in asymptomatic individuals","D":"A third diagnostic test (2-hour 75 g OGTT) is mandatory before the diagnosis of diabetes can be confirmed when FPG and HbA1c give discordant results, since the OGTT is the gold standard diagnostic test for diabetes in asymptomatic patients","E":"The diagnosis of diabetes is confirmed based on the single FPG result of 131 mg/dL without any further testing required, because fasting plasma glucose has higher specificity than HbA1c and a single above-threshold fasting value is immediately diagnostic"},"explanation":"When two different tests are performed and results are discordant (one meets diabetes criteria, one does not), the ADA recommends repeating the test that was above the diagnostic threshold on a separate day to confirm the diagnosis. If the repeat confirms the result above threshold, diabetes is diagnosed. The FPG of 131 mg/dL meets the ≥126 threshold; the HbA1c of 6.3% is in the prediabetes range. In this scenario, the FPG should be repeated. If two tests are both above threshold (concordant), no repeat is needed. With discordant assays, the result with the greatest abnormality is taken as the readout, so both tests need not reach the diabetes range. With discordant assays, the more abnormal result, here the fasting glucose of 131 mg/dL, is the readout, so HbA1c does not take precedence. A third test is not mandatory; with discordant assays the more abnormal result is the readout, confirmed by repeat testing on a separate day. In an asymptomatic patient, a single above-threshold result needs confirmation by repeat testing, so the diagnosis is not made on one FPG value.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Discordant test algorithm — repeat the test that was above threshold. Concordant tests above threshold = confirmed diagnosis. Single FPG without symptoms requires repeat (with symptoms + random glucose ≥200 = immediate diagnosis). COMLEX: Practical point — in clinical practice, FPG and HbA1c are commonly both ordered; discordance is common due to HbA1c confounders (anemia, hemoglobinopathy).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — Diabetes Diagnosis: ADA algorithm for discordant diagnostic test results","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"With discordant assays, the result with the greatest abnorma","C":"With discordant assays, the more abnormal result, here the f","D":"A third test is not mandatory; with discordant assays the mo","E":"In an asymptomatic patient, a single above-threshold result "}},{"id":"sdl29_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus type 1.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man presents to his primary care physician with a 6-month history of gradually increasing thirst, urinary frequency, and 12-lb unintentional weight loss. He has no prior medical history. BMI is 23 kg/m². Fasting plasma glucose is 268 mg/dL. HbA1c is 10.2%. He is started on metformin but returns 3 months later with worsening hyperglycemia (FPG 312 mg/dL) and new anti-GAD65 antibodies detected on laboratory workup. C-peptide level is 0.4 ng/mL (low). Which of the following best characterizes this patient's condition?","choices":{"E":"Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults (LADA), an autoimmune diabetes presenting in adulthood with an initial oral agent response followed by progressive beta-cell destruction, confirmed by positive anti-GAD65 antibodies and a low C-peptide showing declining insulin secretion","A":"Type 2 diabetes mellitus with secondary oral agent failure due to progressive beta-cell exhaustion from glucotoxicity; the positive anti-GAD65 is a non-specific finding in chronic hyperglycemia and does not indicate an autoimmune process in this clinical setting","B":"Type 1 diabetes mellitus presenting in its classic form with acute-onset absolute insulin deficiency; the 6-month prodrome and initial metformin response indicate this presentation is atypically slow only because the patient has HLA-DQ6 protective alleles","C":"MODY3 (HNF-1α mutation) presenting in adulthood with a characteristic progression through impaired glucose tolerance before overt diabetes develops; autoantibody positivity is a coincidental finding since HNF-1α mutations increase susceptibility to anti-GAD antibody production","D":"Ketosis-prone type 2 diabetes (Flatbush diabetes) presenting with near-absolute insulin deficiency at onset; positive anti-GAD65 in this context represents cross-reactivity with heat shock protein 65 expressed on stressed beta cells rather than true autoimmunity"},"explanation":"LADA (also called Type 1.5 or slowly progressive T1D) presents in adulthood, is lean, has positive islet autoantibodies (anti-GAD65 is most sensitive), and has slow autoimmune beta-cell destruction. Initially it may respond to oral agents, mimicking T2D, but progresses to insulin dependence within months to years. Low C-peptide confirms declining beta-cell reserve. Up to 10–15% of \"type 2\" patients may actually have LADA (Katzung Ch 41). Anti-GAD65 is the most persistent and sensitive autoantibody in LADA (vs. IA-2 and insulin autoantibodies, which may disappear earlier). Anti-GAD65 is a diabetes autoantibody that distinguishes LADA, a slowly progressive autoimmune diabetes of adults, from type 2 diabetes, which has no islet autoantibodies. LADA is a slowly progressive autoimmune diabetes of adults that may not need insulin at diagnosis, which explains the gradual course and initial oral-agent response. MODY is a monogenic beta-cell defect, whereas diabetes autoantibodies such as anti-GAD65 distinguish autoimmune LADA. Anti-GAD65 is a diabetes autoantibody marking autoimmune LADA, whereas type 2 diabetes has no islet autoantibodies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LADA = adult-onset autoimmune DM, slow progression. Anti-GAD65 = most sensitive marker. C-peptide differentiates T1D/LADA (low) from T2D (normal/high). Screen for LADA in: lean adults with \"T2D\" who fail oral agents rapidly. COMLEX: LADA patients often misclassified as T2D and undertreated; early insulin therapy may preserve remaining beta-cell function in some cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Type 1 DM Pathophysiology: LADA as a slowly progressive adult form of autoimmune diabetes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Anti-GAD65 is a diabetes autoantibody that distinguishes LAD","B":"LADA is a slowly progressive autoimmune diabetes of adults t","C":"MODY is a monogenic beta-cell defect, whereas diabetes autoa","D":"Anti-GAD65 is a diabetes autoantibody marking autoimmune LAD"}},{"id":"sdl29_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus type 1.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl is brought to her pediatrician for evaluation. Her 12-year-old brother was diagnosed with type 1 diabetes mellitus 2 years ago. On routine screening performed because of her family history, her anti-GAD65 and anti-IA-2 antibodies are both positive. Fasting plasma glucose is 98 mg/dL. A 2-hour OGTT shows a 2-hour glucose of 152 mg/dL. She has no symptoms. Which of the following best characterizes her current stage and risk?","choices":{"C":"She is in Stage 2 of type 1 diabetes, defined by the presence of two or more islet autoantibodies and dysglycemia (impaired glucose tolerance) without clinical symptoms; she has an ~75% probability of developing symptomatic T1D (Stage 3) within 5 years","A":"She is in Stage 1 of type 1 diabetes, defined by the presence of two or more islet autoantibodies with normoglycemia; the OGTT value of 152 mg/dL is within the normal range for a pediatric population and does not constitute dysglycemia in children under 12","B":"She has type 2 diabetes based on her 2-hour OGTT of 152 mg/dL, which meets impaired glucose tolerance criteria; autoantibody positivity in pediatric patients reflects maternal transfer of antibodies and does not predict autoimmune diabetes development","D":"She is in Stage 3 of type 1 diabetes because the presence of multiple autoantibodies combined with any degree of glucose abnormality constitutes clinical diabetes regardless of symptom status in pediatric patients with an affected first-degree relative","E":"She does not have pre-clinical T1D because the staging system applies only to individuals with HLA-DR3/DR4 haplotypes confirmed by genotyping; without HLA confirmation, autoantibody positivity alone represents a high false-positive rate in pediatric family screening"},"explanation":"Using the ADA/JDRF T1D staging: Stage 1 = ≥2 autoantibodies + normoglycemia; Stage 2 = ≥2 autoantibodies + dysglycemia (IFG or IGT) without symptoms; Stage 3 = autoantibodies + dysglycemia + clinical symptoms. This girl has 2 positive antibodies and 2-hr OGTT of 152 mg/dL (IGT range: 140–199 mg/dL), placing her in Stage 2. Stage 2 T1D carries about a 75% 5-year risk of progressing to Stage 3 (Robbins Ch 24). Teplizumab is now FDA-approved to delay Stage 3 onset in Stage 2 patients. Stage 1 has normoglycemia, but this girl has an impaired-tolerance 2-hour glucose of 152 mg/dL (140 to 199), which is dysglycemia and places her in Stage 2. Two or more islet autoantibodies define presymptomatic type 1 diabetes, and with dysglycemia she is in Stage 2, not type 2 diabetes. Stage 3 requires clinical symptoms; she has two antibodies and dysglycemia but no symptoms, so she is in Stage 2. Type 1 diabetes staging uses islet autoantibodies and glycemia; HLA genotyping is not part of the stage definitions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T1D staging — Stage 1: autoantibodies + normoglycemia; Stage 2: autoantibodies + dysglycemia; Stage 3: clinical DM. Multiple autoantibodies = much higher risk than a single antibody (5-year risk <50% in stage 1 vs 75% in stage 2, Robbins Ch 24). COMLEX: Teplizumab (anti-CD3) delays T1D onset by median 2 years in Stage 2 — preventive immunotherapy. TrialNet for family member screening.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Type 1 DM Pathophysiology: Preclinical staging of T1D and risk stratification by autoantibody status","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Stage 1 has normoglycemia, but this girl has an impaired-tol","B":"Two or more islet autoantibodies define presymptomatic type ","D":"Stage 3 requires clinical symptoms; she has two antibodies a","E":"Type 1 diabetes staging uses islet autoantibodies and glycem"}},{"id":"sdl29_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus type 1.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy with known type 1 diabetes mellitus (diagnosed age 11) presents to the ED with nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain for 12 hours. He ran out of his insulin 2 days ago. Temperature is 37.6°C, HR 118 bpm, BP 98/64 mmHg, RR 28 breaths/min with deep, labored breathing. Blood glucose is 418 mg/dL. ABG: pH 7.19, pCO2 18 mmHg, HCO3 7 mEq/L. Urinalysis shows 4+ glucose and 3+ ketones. Serum β-hydroxybutyrate is elevated. Which of the following best explains why absolute insulin deficiency leads to ketoacidosis in this patient?","choices":{"B":"Absent insulin allows unopposed glucagon to activate hormone-sensitive lipase in adipocytes, releasing massive amounts of free fatty acids; hepatic beta-oxidation of these FFAs generates excess acetyl-CoA that overwhelms the TCA cycle and is channeled into ketone body synthesis","A":"Absent insulin prevents GLUT4 translocation in the liver, causing hepatic glucose deficit that activates de novo lipogenesis from amino acids; the resulting accumulation of malonyl-CoA inhibits CPT-1 and shunts acetyl-CoA into cytoplasmic ketogenesis outside the mitochondrial matrix","C":"Absent insulin causes pancreatic alpha-cell apoptosis, reducing glucagon levels and paradoxically activating gluconeogenesis through a glucagon-independent pathway that generates excess pyruvate, which is then decarboxylated to acetyl-CoA and converted to acetoacetate","D":"Absent insulin reduces renal tubular glucose reabsorption via SGLT2 downregulation, causing glucosuria and an osmotic diuresis that depletes urinary bicarbonate, resulting in a hyperchloremic non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis rather than a true ketoacidosis","E":"Absent insulin activates the hexosamine pathway in hepatocytes, increasing UDP-GlcNAc levels and causing O-GlcNAc modification of HMG-CoA reductase, constitutively activating cholesterol synthesis at the expense of ketone body formation in the mitochondria"},"explanation":"In DKA, absent insulin → unopposed glucagon → activation of hormone-sensitive lipase (HSL) in adipocytes → massive FFA release → hepatic beta-oxidation → excess acetyl-CoA → acetyl-CoA exceeds TCA cycle capacity (oxaloacetate is depleted by gluconeogenesis) → HMG-CoA pathway activated → acetoacetate and β-hydroxybutyrate (ketone bodies). The metabolic acidosis is an anion gap acidosis. Glucagon also activates CPT-1 (by reducing malonyl-CoA via inhibiting acetyl-CoA carboxylase), enabling FFA entry into mitochondria. Kussmaul respirations (deep, labored) are the respiratory compensation: hyperventilation lowers pCO2 (here 18 mmHg), although the pH remains acidemic. In DKA, insulin deficiency stimulates hormone-sensitive lipase and the released fatty acids are oxidized to ketones in hepatic mitochondria; GLUT4 is the muscle and fat transporter, not the liver's. Type 1 diabetes destroys beta cells, and DKA involves glucagon excess, not reduced glucagon from alpha-cell loss. The acidosis comes from ketone bodies, acetoacetic and beta-hydroxybutyric acid made by hepatic fatty acid oxidation, not from bicarbonate loss in glucosuria. Ketogenesis follows hormone-sensitive lipase release of fatty acids and their oxidation in hepatic mitochondria to ketone bodies, not a hexosamine effect on HMG-CoA reductase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DKA mechanism — no insulin + excess glucagon → HSL activation → FFA mobilization → hepatic ketogenesis. Kussmaul breathing = compensatory. Lab findings: anion gap metabolic acidosis, ↑β-hydroxybutyrate (predominant ketone), ↑glucose, ↑K+ initially (but total body K+ depleted). Treatment: isotonic saline → insulin drip → K+ replacement. COMLEX: Replete K+ with insulin therapy, because insulin shifts K+ into cells and causes hypokalemia if K+ is not replaced (Bootcamp).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Type 1 DM Pathophysiology: Insulin deficiency and glucagon excess driving ketoacidosis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"In DKA, insulin deficiency stimulates hormone-sensitive lipa","C":"Type 1 diabetes destroys beta cells, and DKA involves glucag","D":"The acidosis comes from ketone bodies, acetoacetic and beta-","E":"Ketogenesis follows hormone-sensitive lipase release of fatt"}},{"id":"sdl29_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus type 2.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with a BMI of 33 kg/m² and strong family history of type 2 diabetes has serial laboratory testing over 8 years. At age 41: FPG 88 mg/dL, fasting insulin 18 μIU/mL (elevated). At age 45: FPG 112 mg/dL, HbA1c 6.0%, fasting insulin 32 μIU/mL (markedly elevated). At age 49: FPG 143 mg/dL, HbA1c 7.1%, fasting insulin 12 μIU/mL (declining). Which of the following best explains the progression of his fasting insulin values from age 41 to 49?","choices":{"A":"His pancreatic beta cells initially compensated for peripheral insulin resistance by hypersecretion (elevated fasting insulin), but progressive beta-cell failure from glucotoxicity, lipotoxicity, and amyloid deposition eventually reduced secretory capacity, causing overt diabetes as insulin levels fell below the threshold needed to maintain euglycemia","B":"His increasing visceral adiposity progressively upregulated hepatic insulin clearance via receptor-mediated endocytosis, reducing circulating fasting insulin concentrations despite unchanged total insulin production by beta cells over the observation period","C":"His peripheral insulin resistance resolved between ages 45 and 49 because visceral fat remodeling increased adiponectin secretion, allowing the same amount of secreted insulin to have greater peripheral effect and reducing the stimulus for beta-cell hypersecretion","D":"His declining fasting insulin at age 49 reflects successful lifestyle modification that improved insulin sensitivity, reducing the physiological demand on beta cells and allowing them to reduce production to baseline without any underlying beta-cell loss occurring","E":"His hyperinsulinemia at ages 41–45 represents insulin resistance at the hepatic level only, with portal hyperinsulinism driving upregulation of peripheral insulin receptors over time, ultimately restoring euglycemia without requiring any change in beta-cell mass"},"explanation":"This case illustrates the natural history of T2D: (1) Early insulin resistance → compensatory beta-cell hyperinsulinism (fasting insulin 18 → 32 μIU/mL) maintains near-normal glucose; (2) As resistance worsens, beta cells can no longer compensate → IFG/prediabetes stage; (3) Progressive beta-cell failure (glucotoxicity, lipotoxicity, IAPP deposition) reduces insulin secretory capacity → overt T2D as insulin levels fall (12 μIU/mL) despite worsening hyperglycemia. This pattern of hyperinsulinemia followed by relative hypoinsulinemia is pathognomonic for T2D progression. Falling insulin while glucose rises reflects beta cells exhausting their capacity after early compensatory hyperinsulinemia, not greater hepatic insulin clearance with unchanged production. Rising glucose and HbA1c show insulin resistance persisting with failing beta cells, not resolving, and adiponectin is reduced rather than increased in obesity. Falling insulin with rising glucose and HbA1c marks relative insulin deficiency from beta-cell failure, not improved insulin sensitivity. The rising glucose and HbA1c show euglycemia was not restored; early hyperinsulinemia was beta-cell compensation for insulin resistance that later failed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: T2D natural history: Insulin resistance → compensatory hyperinsulinemia → maintained euglycemia → beta-cell fatigue → hyperinsulinemia peaks → beta-cell failure → relative insulinopenia → overt T2D. Beta-cell function is already declining by the time T2D is diagnosed. COMLEX: Early T2D treatment targets insulin resistance (metformin); later stages require insulin secretagogues or exogenous insulin as beta-cell mass declines.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Type 2 DM Pathophysiology: Natural history of beta-cell compensation and failure in T2D progression","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Falling insulin while glucose rises reflects beta cells exha","C":"Rising glucose and HbA1c show insulin resistance persisting ","D":"Falling insulin with rising glucose and HbA1c marks relative","E":"The rising glucose and HbA1c show euglycemia was not restore"}},{"id":"sdl29_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus type 2.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher is studying which organ is responsible for the majority of insulin-stimulated glucose disposal in the postprandial state in healthy humans. He infuses a tracer and measures tissue-specific glucose uptake after a standard mixed meal. Which of the following organs accounts for the largest fraction of postprandial glucose uptake in an insulin-dependent manner, and why is this tissue particularly relevant in type 2 diabetes?","choices":{"D":"The liver accounts for the largest share of postprandial glucose uptake through GLUT2-mediated, insulin-independent glucose transport, making hepatic glucokinase deficiency the primary driver of postprandial hyperglycemia in type 2 diabetes","A":"Skeletal muscle accounts for the largest share of insulin-stimulated postprandial glucose uptake via GLUT4 translocation; insulin resistance in skeletal muscle is therefore the primary driver of postprandial hyperglycemia in type 2 diabetes mellitus","B":"Adipose tissue accounts for the largest share of insulin-stimulated postprandial glucose uptake because GLUT4 expression is highest in adipocytes and the total metabolic activity of the expanded adipose depot in obese patients creates the largest sink for dietary glucose","C":"The brain accounts for the largest share of postprandial glucose disposal via insulin-independent GLUT1 and GLUT3 transporters, and cerebral insulin resistance is the primary cause of the hyperglycemia and neurological symptoms seen in type 2 diabetes","E":"The kidney accounts for the largest share of postprandial glucose disposal via SGLT2-mediated active reabsorption in the proximal tubule, making impaired renal glucose recapture the primary mechanism of postprandial hyperglycemia in insulin-resistant patients"},"explanation":"Skeletal muscle is the primary organ for insulin-stimulated glucose disposal, accounting for most postprandial glucose uptake. This occurs via GLUT4, which is recruited from intracellular vesicles to the plasma membrane in response to insulin signaling (Akt-mediated AS160 phosphorylation). Because skeletal muscle is the major glucose sink, insulin resistance in skeletal muscle directly causes postprandial hyperglycemia. The liver is important for fasting glucose regulation (gluconeogenesis) but uses GLUT2 (insulin-independent) and takes up a smaller share of postprandial glucose. The brain uses GLUT1/GLUT3 (insulin-independent). Skeletal muscle, not adipose tissue, is the major insulin-responsive site of postprandial glucose use; adipocytes are a lesser target. The kidney reabsorbs filtered glucose through SGLT2 rather than disposing of it; skeletal muscle is the major insulin-responsive site of postprandial glucose use.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Postprandial glucose disposal: mostly skeletal muscle (GLUT4, insulin-dependent), with a smaller share in liver (GLUT2, insulin-independent); brain uses GLUT1/3 (insulin-independent). GLUT4 = rate-limiting step for postprandial glucose clearance. Exercise increases GLUT4 translocation via AMPK pathway, independent of insulin. COMLEX: Regular physical activity directly targets GLUT4 translocation in skeletal muscle — mechanistically understood now.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Type 2 DM Pathophysiology: Skeletal muscle GLUT4 as the primary site of postprandial insulin-dependent glucose disposal","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Skeletal muscle, not adipose tissue, is the major insulin-re","E":"The kidney reabsorbs filtered glucose through SGLT2 rather t"}},{"id":"sdl29_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus type 2.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with type 2 diabetes for 18 years is referred for nephrology evaluation. During workup, a renal biopsy is obtained. Histopathology shows: light microscopy with PAS-positive nodular mesangial deposits at the periphery of glomerular lobules (Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules) and diffuse mesangial matrix expansion. On a separate specimen from a patient with long-standing type 2 diabetes, islet pathology shows: amorphous, eosinophilic material surrounding beta cells that stains with Congo red and demonstrates apple-green birefringence under polarized light. Which of the following identifies the protein responsible for the islet amyloid deposits in type 2 diabetes?","choices":{"C":"Beta-2 microglobulin, the light chain of MHC class I molecules shed by immune cells during the chronic islet inflammatory response in type 2 diabetes, accumulates in the mesangium and islets when renal clearance is impaired by diabetic nephropathy","A":"Serum amyloid A (SAA), an acute-phase reactant produced by the liver in response to chronic inflammatory cytokines released by visceral adipose tissue macrophages, deposits systemically in all insulin-resistant tissues including the pancreatic islets during T2D progression","B":"Transthyretin (TTR), a carrier protein for thyroxine and retinol produced by the liver and choroid plexus, misfolds and deposits as amyloid in the pancreatic islets of elderly type 2 diabetic patients in a manner identical to cardiac and peripheral nerve TTR amyloidosis","D":"Islet amyloid polypeptide (IAPP), also called amylin, is a 37-amino-acid peptide co-secreted with insulin by beta cells that forms insoluble amyloid fibrils within the islets in type 2 diabetes, contributing to beta-cell toxicity and progressive secretory failure","E":"Proinsulin-derived C-peptide, the connecting peptide cleaved from proinsulin during insulin processing in beta-cell secretory granules, forms amyloid aggregates within the endoplasmic reticulum when beta cells are chronically hyperstimulated in insulin-resistant states"},"explanation":"Islet amyloid polypeptide (IAPP/amylin) is co-secreted with insulin from beta-cell granules. In T2D, chronic beta-cell hyperstimulation leads to IAPP overproduction and aggregation into insoluble amyloid fibrils (beta-sheet secondary structure). These deposits are characteristic of T2D islets and are not a feature of T1D. Congo red staining with apple-green birefringence is the pathological hallmark of amyloid. IAPP fibrils are directly toxic to beta cells (membrane disruption, apoptosis), contributing to progressive beta-cell failure in T2D. Serum amyloid A is the AA amyloid of chronic inflammation; type 2 diabetic islet amyloid is made from IAPP co-secreted with insulin. Transthyretin forms amyloid in systemic and cardiac amyloidosis; islet amyloid in type 2 diabetes is IAPP. Beta-2 microglobulin is the amyloid of dialysis-related disease; the islet amyloid of type 2 diabetes is IAPP (amylin). The islet amyloid of type 2 diabetes is islet amyloid polypeptide (amylin), co-secreted with insulin, not C-peptide.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Islet amyloid = IAPP (amylin) = pathological hallmark of T2D islets. Congo red → apple-green birefringence under polarized light = amyloid. T2D islet findings: amyloid deposition + decreased beta-cell mass. T1D islet findings: insulitis (lymphocytic infiltrate). Amylin (IAPP, 37 amino acids per Katzung Ch 41) is co-secreted with insulin. COMLEX: Pramlintide (synthetic amylin analog) used as adjunct to insulin therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — Type 2 DM Pathophysiology: Islet amyloid polypeptide deposition and its role in beta-cell failure","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Serum amyloid A is the AA amyloid of chronic inflammation; t","B":"Transthyretin forms amyloid in systemic and cardiac amyloido","C":"Beta-2 microglobulin is the amyloid of dialysis-related dise","E":"The islet amyloid of type 2 diabetes is islet amyloid polype"}},{"id":"sdl29_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the role that obesity plays in insulin resistance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man presents for an annual physical. He has no medical history and takes no medications. Examination: BMI 34 kg/m², waist circumference 42 inches, BP 144/92 mmHg. Labs: fasting glucose 118 mg/dL, triglycerides 220 mg/dL, HDL cholesterol 36 mg/dL, LDL cholesterol 132 mg/dL. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiological link between his visceral obesity and the abnormal laboratory findings shown?","choices":{"D":"Visceral adiposity drives insulin resistance through portal FFA delivery and inflammatory cytokine secretion; insulin resistance causes impaired hepatic VLDL clearance and increased VLDL production (hypertriglyceridemia), reduced HDL (from CETP-mediated exchange), hepatic glucose overproduction (IFG), and sodium retention (hypertension)","A":"Visceral adiposity reduces adiponectin and increases leptin, directly inhibiting renal potassium excretion and causing hyperkalemia-driven aldosterone excess that elevates blood pressure through a mineralocorticoid-independent mechanism in the juxtaglomerular apparatus","B":"Visceral adiposity causes hypothalamic leptin resistance that increases ACTH secretion from the anterior pituitary, producing a secondary hypercortisolism with the identical metabolic phenotype as exogenous Cushing syndrome including hypertriglyceridemia and IFG","C":"Visceral adiposity directly compresses the inferior vena cava, reducing venous return and activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system through a hemodynamic mechanism that secondarily impairs hepatic lipid clearance via reduced portal blood flow","E":"Visceral adiposity increases circulating resistin, which directly activates GLP-1 receptors on pancreatic alpha cells to increase glucagon secretion, producing all elements of metabolic syndrome through glucagon-driven hepatic glucose and VLDL overproduction"},"explanation":"Metabolic syndrome (central obesity, raised blood pressure, raised triglycerides, low HDL and raised fasting glucose) is closely tied to excess visceral adipose tissue. Guyton (course reading) says all features of the metabolic syndrome are closely related to abdominal fat and that insulin resistance clearly causes the raised glucose, but its role in the other components is uncertain. The commonly taught mechanisms below are only partly detailed in the course texts: (1) portal FFA delivery → hepatic insulin resistance → increased VLDL production + impaired VLDL clearance → hypertriglyceridemia; (2) triglyceride-rich VLDL exchanges TG for cholesterol esters in HDL via CETP → HDL becomes triglyceride-enriched and cleared faster → low HDL; (3) insulin resistance → impaired hepatic glucose suppression → IFG; (4) insulin resistance → compensatory hyperinsulinemia → renal Na+ retention → hypertension. Katzung (dyslipidemia chapter) notes that insulin resistance occurs in most patients with the metabolic syndrome and that HDL-C is usually low because cholesteryl esters are transferred to triglyceride-rich lipoproteins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metabolic syndrome (NCEP ATP-III) = any 3 of: central obesity, ↑TG, ↓HDL, ↑BP, ↑FPG. Risk factor for CVD and T2D. Pathological core = insulin resistance. NCEP ATP-III criteria commonly tested. COMLEX: Metabolic syndrome diagnosis allows osteopathic preventive medicine interventions — lifestyle (diet, exercise) before pharmacotherapy; visceral fat reduction normalizes all components.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 5 — Obesity and Insulin Resistance: Metabolic syndrome as the clinical manifestation of visceral adiposity-driven insulin resistance","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl29_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the role that obesity plays in insulin resistance.","batch":2,"stem":"A research group studies the mechanism by which saturated fatty acid excess causes insulin resistance in macrophages and adipocytes. They demonstrate that saturated FFAs are recognized by Toll-like receptor 4 (TLR4) on adipose tissue macrophages, activating a downstream signaling cascade. Which of the following cytokines is the primary effector produced by this TLR4-mediated inflammasome activation that directly promotes insulin resistance in adjacent adipocytes?","choices":{"E":"Interleukin-1β (IL-1β) is the primary inflammasome-derived cytokine; saturated FFAs activate NLRP3 inflammasome assembly in macrophages, which cleaves procaspase-1 to active caspase-1, releasing IL-1β that activates NF-κB in adipocytes and impairs insulin signaling","A":"Interleukin-10 (IL-10) is the primary cytokine produced by M2-polarized macrophages in response to saturated FFA exposure; paradoxically, IL-10 promotes insulin resistance by inhibiting AMPK activation in adipocytes through suppression of SIRT1 deacetylase activity","B":"Interleukin-4 (IL-4) is the primary cytokine released from eosinophils recruited to visceral fat depots by saturated FFA oxidation products; IL-4 drives M2 macrophage polarization that promotes adipogenesis and increases lipid storage capacity in visceral fat depots","C":"Interferon-γ (IFN-γ) is the primary mediator; saturated FFAs activate NLRP3 inflammasome to release IFN-γ from macrophages, which then activates JAK-STAT1 signaling in adipocytes to downregulate GLUT4 expression at the transcriptional level in insulin-resistant states","D":"Transforming growth factor-β (TGF-β) is the primary effector; saturated FFAs trigger TGF-β secretion from adipose tissue macrophages that activates SMAD2/3 signaling in adipocytes, promoting epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition and fibrosis of the adipose tissue stroma"},"explanation":"Saturated FFAs act as danger-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs) recognized by TLR4 on macrophages. This activates the NLRP3 inflammasome — a multi-protein complex that cleaves procaspase-1 → active caspase-1, which processes pro-IL-1β → mature IL-1β. IL-1β then acts on adjacent adipocytes and myocytes, activating IKKβ/NF-κB and JNK pathways that produce serine phosphorylation of IRS-1, impairing insulin signaling. TNF-α (also produced by adipose macrophages) similarly promotes insulin resistance. This inflammatory pathway is distinct from the DAG/ceramide pathway that operates within the insulin-resistant cells themselves. The inflammasome activated by excess fatty acids leads to secretion of IL-1 beta, not IL-10, and IL-1 then promotes insulin resistance. The inflammasome activated by excess fatty acids leads to secretion of IL-1 beta, which promotes insulin resistance, rather than IL-4-driven lipid storage. The inflammasome activated by excess fatty acids leads to secretion of IL-1 beta, not IFN-gamma. The inflammasome activated by excess fatty acids leads to secretion of IL-1 beta, which promotes insulin resistance, rather than TGF-beta-driven adipose fibrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Adipose TLR4 → NLRP3 inflammasome → caspase-1 → IL-1β → NF-κB → IRS-1 serine phosphorylation → insulin resistance. TNF-α is also pro-inflammatory and insulin-resistant (from M1 macrophages in adipose). M1 macrophages (pro-inflammatory, produce TNF-α, IL-1β, IL-6) increase in visceral fat with obesity. COMLEX: Anakinra (IL-1Ra) and canakinumab (anti-IL-1β) studied for T2D and CV risk reduction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 5 — Obesity and Insulin Resistance: NLRP3 inflammasome and IL-1β as inflammatory mediators linking saturated fat excess to insulin resistance","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"The inflammasome activated by excess fatty acids leads to secretion of IL-1 beta, not IL-10, and IL-","B":"The inflammasome activated by excess fatty acids leads to secretion of IL-1 beta, which promotes insulin resistance, rather than IL-4-driven","C":"The inflammasome activated by excess fatty acids leads to secretion of IL-1 beta, not IFN-gamma.","D":"The inflammasome activated by excess fatty acids leads to secretion of IL-1 beta, which promotes insulin resistance, rather than TGF-beta-dr"}},{"id":"sdl29_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the role that obesity plays in insulin resistance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with obesity, a BMI of 36, and type 2 diabetes has mildly elevated ALT. Ultrasound shows a diffusely echogenic liver, and biopsy shows hepatocytes filled with fat without significant inflammation or fibrosis. Which mechanism best links his obesity and insulin resistance to the liver finding?","choices":{"A":"Insulin-resistant adipose tissue releases excess free fatty acids, and high circulating levels deposit as fat within hepatocytes","B":"Hyperglycemia glycates hepatocyte membranes, which traps cholesterol inside the cells and gives a fat-filled look","C":"Visceral fat secretes high levels of adiponectin, which drives fatty acids into hepatocytes and blocks their oxidation in the mitochondria","D":"Islet amyloid deposits release amylin into the portal vein, which directly stimulates triglyceride synthesis and storage in hepatocytes","E":"Autoreactive T cells attack hepatocytes as well as beta cells, and the injured liver cells accumulate fat as they slowly degenerate"},"explanation":"Robbins explains that high circulating levels of free fatty acids may result in the accumulation of excess fat (steatosis) in hepatocytes; this metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) ranges from steatosis without liver injury to steatohepatitis with inflammation and injury, with or without fibrosis, and is common in people with metabolic syndrome and type 2 diabetes. The free fatty acids come from insulin-resistant adipose tissue, where insulin fails to inhibit hormone-sensitive lipase, and central fat is more lipolytic than peripheral fat. Glycation of membranes trapping cholesterol is not the described mechanism. Adiponectin is reduced, not increased, in obesity and increases insulin sensitivity. Islet amyloid is a feature of long-standing type 2 diabetes in the islets, not a driver of hepatic fat. Type 2 diabetes has no autoimmune basis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Obesity drives insulin resistance through free fatty acids (DAG and ceramides), adipokines (low adiponectin), and inflammation (inflammasome IL-1β); the same free fatty acid excess causes hepatic steatosis, MASLD (Robbins). COMLEX: Weight loss and exercise improve insulin sensitivity additively and are first-line in milder type 2 diabetes (Robbins).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — insulin inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase in adipose tissue","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"C":"Adiponectin is reduced, not in","E":"Type 2 diabetes has no autoimm"}},{"id":"sdl29_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with a 22-year history of type 2 diabetes mellitus presents with progressive lower extremity edema and foamy urine. Blood pressure is 158/96 mmHg. Creatinine is 2.4 mg/dL. 24-hour urine protein is 4.8 g/day. A renal biopsy is performed. Light microscopy shows: diffuse increase in mesangial matrix and cellularity; PAS-positive, laminated ovoid nodules located at the periphery of glomerular lobules; and hyaline material in both the afferent and efferent arterioles. Electron microscopy shows diffuse glomerular basement membrane thickening. Which of the following best identifies the combination of histopathological findings that is uniquely specific to diabetic nephropathy compared to other glomerular diseases?","choices":{"D":"Diffuse glomerular basement membrane thickening on electron microscopy is the pathognomonic finding specific to diabetic nephropathy; it is absent in all other forms of glomerulonephritis including membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis and thin basement membrane disease","A":"Diffuse mesangial matrix expansion with basement membrane thickening is pathognomonic for diabetic nephropathy because no other glomerular disease produces simultaneous involvement of both the mesangium and the basement membrane without immune complex deposition","B":"Nephrotic-range proteinuria (>3.5 g/day) combined with hypertension is pathognomonic for diabetic nephropathy because this combination does not occur in IgA nephropathy, membranous nephropathy, or focal segmental glomerulosclerosis in non-diabetic patients","C":"Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules (nodular mesangial glomerulosclerosis) with hyalinosis of both afferent and efferent arterioles; efferent hyalinosis is highly characteristic of diabetes and is not seen in hypertensive nephrosclerosis, which affects only the afferent arteriole","E":"PAS positivity of the mesangial nodules on light microscopy confirms diabetic nephropathy because all other nodular glomerular diseases (amyloidosis, light chain deposition disease) produce PAS-negative mesangial deposits that cannot be confused with diabetic nodules"},"explanation":"In diabetic nephropathy, two findings are uniquely specific: (1) Kimmelstiel-Wilson (KW) nodules — PAS-positive, laminated nodular glomerulosclerosis; and (2) efferent arteriolar hyalinosis. Hypertensive nephrosclerosis causes afferent arteriolar hyalinosis only; the involvement of the efferent arteriole is highly characteristic of diabetes (insulin normally dilates the efferent arteriole, and chronic hyperglycemia/AGE formation deposits hyalin here). Diffuse mesangial expansion and GBM thickening are common to many conditions. The combination of KW nodules + efferent hyalinosis is the unique marker of DM nephropathy. PAS positivity alone does not separate diabetic nodules from light chain deposition disease, whose nodules are also PAS-positive. Nephrotic proteinuria also occurs in FSGS, minimal change and membranous disease, so it is not specific; efferent arteriolar hyalinosis is rarely seen without diabetes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diabetic nephropathy morphology: (1) GBM thickening (earliest, EM); (2) diffuse mesangial sclerosis (most common); (3) Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules (pathognomonic, LM); (4) afferent AND efferent arteriolar hyalinosis (efferent = highly characteristic of DM). Clinical progression: microalbuminuria → macroalbuminuria → nephrotic syndrome → ESRD. COMLEX: ACEi/ARB slow progression in diabetic nephropathy — reduce efferent arteriolar tone and intraglomerular pressure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 6 — DM Complications: Diabetic nephropathy histopathology and the unique significance of efferent arteriolar hyalinosis","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Diffuse mesangial expansion an","B":"Nephrotic proteinuria also occurs in FSGS, minimal change an","D":"Diffuse mesangial expansion an"}},{"id":"sdl29_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with a 15-year history of type 2 diabetes and persistently elevated HbA1c values (averaging 10.2% over 5 years) presents for ophthalmology consultation. Fundoscopic examination shows proliferative diabetic retinopathy with neovascularization. A retinal specialist explains that the neovascularization is driven by a specific growth factor upregulated in hypoxic retinal tissue. Simultaneously, the patient is found to have a serum creatinine of 1.9 mg/dL and 2.1 g/24-hr proteinuria. Which of the following molecular pathways most directly links her chronic hyperglycemia to both the retinal neovascularization and the proteinuria?","choices":{"C":"The AGE-RAGE pathway releases growth factors in the vascular wall, including VEGF, which is implicated in retinal neovascularization, and TGF-β, which drives deposition of excess basement membrane material in glomeruli, linking the retinopathy and the proteinuria","A":"The polyol pathway activates aldose reductase in retinal pericytes and glomerular mesangial cells, depleting NADPH and increasing osmotic stress that causes pericyte apoptosis and mesangial cell lysis, directly disrupting the blood-retinal and glomerular filtration barriers","B":"The hexosamine pathway causes O-GlcNAc modification of VEGF receptor-2 on retinal endothelial cells, constitutively activating neovascularization signaling independent of VEGF ligand concentration and producing glucose-independent retinal vascular proliferation","D":"The protein kinase C-β pathway is activated by diacylglycerol accumulation in retinal pericytes and podocytes, causing phosphorylation of occludin tight junction proteins and increasing paracellular permeability simultaneously in the retinal and glomerular barriers","E":"The renin-angiotensin system is activated by AGE-mediated inhibition of angiotensinogen cleavage in both retinal and renal tissue, increasing local angiotensin II concentrations that promote VEGF-independent neovascularization through Ang-1/Tie-2 receptor signaling"},"explanation":"Robbins (course reading) says the detrimental effects of AGE-RAGE signaling in the vascular compartment include release of TGF-β, which leads to deposition of excess basement membrane material, and VEGF, implicated in diabetic retinopathy, along with reactive oxygen species and procoagulant activity. Robbins also says protein kinase C activation produces VEGF and TGF-β and that its downstream effects overlap with those of AGEs, so PKC is a second route to the same growth factors; the occludin phosphorylation step in that choice is not described. The polyol, hexosamine and renin-angiotensin choices describe steps the course text does not give. Anti-VEGF agents are used for proliferative retinopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AGE-RAGE → NF-κB → VEGF (retinal neovascularization) + TGF-β (mesangial expansion/proteinuria). PKC-β also activates VEGF (another pathway). Proliferative DR treatment: laser photocoagulation or intravitreal anti-VEGF. Microalbuminuria (30–300 mg/day) = earliest clinical sign of nephropathy, precedes overt proteinuria by years. COMLEX: VEGF inhibition (ranibizumab) for wet AMD and proliferative DR — same drug, different indications.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 6 — DM Complications: AGE-RAGE pathway and VEGF as shared mediators of diabetic retinopathy and nephropathy","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl29_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathophysiology of diabetes mellitus complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus for 28 years presents to his primary care physician for annual review. He takes insulin, lisinopril, atorvastatin, and aspirin. He has a history of a myocardial infarction at age 64. He lost vision in his right eye from proliferative retinopathy 3 years ago. He currently has a serum creatinine of 3.8 mg/dL (GFR 18 mL/min) and receives dialysis three times weekly. He also has a below-knee amputation of his right leg from an infected diabetic ulcer. Which of the following best explains why diabetes mellitus is the leading cause of each of this patient's major complications, specifically through mechanisms that differ from the same conditions caused by non-diabetic etiologies?","choices":{"A":"Diabetes produces a unique combination of diffuse non-enzymatic glycosylation (AGEs) causing microvascular basement membrane thickening, VEGF-mediated neovascularization, and polyol pathway-mediated nerve damage — mechanisms not operative in hypertensive or atherosclerotic disease alone — explaining why diabetic nephropathy, retinopathy, and neuropathy are disproportionately prevalent and severe in diabetic versus non-diabetic populations","B":"Diabetes primarily accelerates atherosclerosis through LDL oxidation by excess glucose-derived reactive oxygen species in the arterial wall, and all diabetic complications including nephropathy, retinopathy, and neuropathy are attributable to this single macrovascular mechanism operating across all target organs simultaneously","C":"Diabetes causes all its complications through a single mechanism of hyperinsulinemia-mediated smooth muscle proliferation in arterioles; the insulin excess required to overcome peripheral resistance drives proliferative changes in the retina, kidney, and peripheral nerves identical to those caused by growth hormone excess in acromegaly","D":"Diabetes is the leading cause of these complications purely because of higher prevalence relative to other etiologies; the mechanisms of diabetic nephropathy, retinopathy, and neuropathy are identical to those of hypertensive and ischemic end-organ damage, and diabetes simply increases the frequency of exposure to these standard pathways","E":"Diabetes causes ESRD, blindness, and amputation primarily through impaired immune surveillance from hyperglycemia-induced neutrophil dysfunction, leading to recurrent infections that destroy renal, retinal, and peripheral nerve tissue through inflammatory rather than metabolic mechanisms"},"explanation":"Diabetes is the #1 cause of ESRD, adult-onset blindness, and nontraumatic lower-extremity amputations in the United States. The mechanisms are unique to diabetes and not fully replicated by hypertension or atherosclerosis alone: (1) AGE-RAGE pathway → basement membrane thickening → microangiopathy affecting glomeruli, retinal capillaries, and vasa nervorum; (2) Polyol pathway → sorbitol accumulation → osmotic/oxidative injury in Schwann cells (neuropathy), retinal pericytes (retinopathy), and lens (cataracts); (3) VEGF-driven neovascularization → proliferative retinopathy → vision loss; (4) Both afferent and efferent arteriolar hyalinosis (unique to DM, vs. hypertension which affects only afferent). Macrovascular disease (MI, stroke, PVD) is also accelerated by diabetes but shares mechanisms with non-diabetic atherosclerosis. Robbins separates macrovascular atherosclerosis from microvascular disease of retina, kidney and nerves driven by AGEs, PKC, polyol and hexosamine pathways, so one atherosclerotic mechanism does not explain all. Chronic complications are attributed mainly to persistent hyperglycemia acting through AGEs, PKC activation, polyol and hexosamine pathways, not to hyperinsulinemia-driven smooth muscle proliferation. Diabetic microangiopathy has distinctive lesions, such as nodular glomerulosclerosis and efferent arteriolar hyalinosis rarely seen without diabetes, so its mechanisms are not identical to hypertensive damage. Infections cause about 5% of diabetic deaths; the major complications stem from hyperglycemia-driven micro- and macrovascular damage, not infection-mediated tissue destruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diabetes = leading cause of ESRD (#1), adult blindness (#1), nontraumatic amputations (#1) in the US. MI is the most common cause of death in T2D. Microvascular = retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy (mechanisms unique to DM). Macrovascular = CAD, PVD, stroke (shared with non-DM but accelerated). COMLEX: Comprehensive foot exam (monofilament test for neuropathy, ABI for PVD) is essential annual exam in all diabetic patients; prevention of the first ulceration reduces amputation risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 6 — DM Complications: Unique mechanisms of diabetic microvascular disease explaining why DM leads all causes of ESRD, blindness, and amputation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Robbins separates macrovascular atherosclerosis from microva","C":"Chronic complications are attributed mainly to persistent hy","D":"Diabetic microangiopathy has distinctive lesions, such as no","E":"Infections cause about 5% of diabetic deaths; the major comp"}},{"id":"sdl36_q22","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define Prediabetes, Diabetes Mellitus and its classification.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman (G1P0) at 28 weeks of gestation is diagnosed with gestational diabetes mellitus after an abnormal 50-g screen followed by a 3-hour 100-g OGTT meeting Carpenter-Coustan criteria. Her endocrinologist explains that a placental hormone is the primary driver of her insulin resistance. This hormone is secreted in greater quantities than all other pregnancy hormones combined. Which of the following best identifies this hormone and its mechanism?","choices":{"D":"Human chorionic somatomammotropin, which decreases maternal insulin sensitivity and glucose utilization through a growth hormone-like mechanism, diverting glucose availability to the fetus","A":"Human chorionic gonadotropin, which directly binds insulin receptors on adipocytes and skeletal muscle, producing competitive inhibition of insulin signaling at the postreceptor level","B":"Placental estrogen, which downregulates GLUT-4 transporter expression in peripheral muscle tissue and reduces estrogen receptor-mediated insulin-stimulated glucose uptake in maternal cells","C":"Placental progesterone, which upregulates hepatic gluconeogenesis through glucocorticoid receptor cross-reactivity in pericentral hepatocytes, driving fasting hyperglycemia in pregnant women","E":"Placental relaxin, which activates maternal hepatic glucagon receptors to promote glycogenolysis and elevate fasting maternal blood glucose independently of insulin receptor signaling"},"explanation":"hCS is secreted in quantities several times greater than all other pregnancy hormones combined. Its third and most clinically significant metabolic effect is a decrease in maternal insulin sensitivity and glucose utilization — producing relative insulin resistance. This insulin-resistant state ensures that maternal tissues consume less glucose, making more glucose available for the fetus, which uses it as its primary growth substrate. The placenta also mobilizes maternal free fatty acids as an alternative energy source for the mother. This hCS-driven gestational insulin resistance is the physiologic mechanism underlying gestational diabetes mellitus when maternal beta-cell reserve is insufficient to compensate. hCG causes persistence of the corpus luteum rather than binding insulin receptors; human chorionic somatomammotropin is the hormone that decreases maternal insulin sensitivity and glucose use. The pregnancy hormone that decreases maternal insulin sensitivity and glucose use is human chorionic somatomammotropin, secreted in quantities several times all others combined, not estrogen. Progesterone's pregnancy roles include decidual development and reduced uterine contractility, not hepatic gluconeogenesis; human chorionic somatomammotropin is what decreases maternal insulin sensitivity. Human chorionic somatomammotropin, not relaxin, decreases maternal insulin sensitivity and glucose use, making more glucose available to the fetus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hCS causes gestational insulin resistance → GDM when maternal beta cells cannot compensate with sufficient insulin secretion. GDM is screened at 24–28 weeks (when hCS levels are rising). COMLEX: Insulin resistance is the shared mechanism of type 2 DM and GDM, and women with GDM are at increased risk of later type 2 diabetes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — Growth hormone metabolic effects: protein synthesis, fat utilization, and decreased glucose utilization","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"hCG causes persistence of the corpus luteum rather than bind","B":"The pregnancy hormone that decreases maternal insulin sensit","C":"Progesterone's pregnancy roles include decidual development ","E":"Human chorionic somatomammotropin, not relaxin, decreases ma"}}],"32":[{"id":"sdl32_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacologic effects of short-acting insulin preparations.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best explains why rapid-acting insulin analogs (lispro, aspart, glulisine) are absorbed into the systemic circulation significantly faster than regular human insulin after subcutaneous injection?","choices":{"B":"Amino acid substitutions in the rapid-acting analogs disrupt the intermolecular interactions that stabilize the hexameric crystal form of insulin; after subcutaneous injection the analogs rapidly dissociate into monomers and are absorbed much faster than regular insulin, which must first dissociate from its hexameric state","A":"Rapid-acting analogs contain an additional zinc ion substituted into the hexameric core that actively expels individual monomers into surrounding tissue fluid; this zinc-driven ejection mechanism is absent in regular insulin, whose hexamers passively dissolve before absorption can begin","C":"Rapid-acting analogs are formulated at a lower pH than regular insulin, which causes them to precipitate superficially in the dermis rather than in the subcutaneous layer; the larger surface area of the dermal depot allows greater contact with capillaries and proportionally faster absorption","D":"Rapid-acting analogs carry a net negative charge at physiologic pH that attracts positively charged albumin molecules in interstitial fluid; albumin-bound monomers are then transported by facilitated diffusion across capillary endothelium without requiring passive dissociation from a crystal depot","E":"Rapid-acting analogs are formulated with phenol and meta-Cresol preservatives at higher concentrations than regular insulin; these preservatives actively destabilize hexameric aggregates by competing with zinc for histidine coordination sites on the insulin B-chain surface"},"explanation":"Regular insulin exists as hexamers (stabilized by two zinc ions and phenol/meta-Cresol) in the vial. After SC injection it must first dissociate hexamers → dimers → monomers before absorption — a process that takes 30–60 minutes. Rapid-acting analogs (lispro: B28 Pro↔B29 Lys swap; aspart: B28 Pro→Asp; glulisine: B3 Asn→Lys + B29 Lys→Glu) carry amino acid changes that disrupt the protein-protein contacts holding dimers and hexamers together, causing near-immediate monomer dissociation after injection. This produces onset 5–15 min, peak 1–1.5 h, duration 3–4 h versus regular insulin's 30–60 min onset, 2 h peak, 6–8 h duration. Rapid-acting analogs have amino acid substitutions that weaken the contacts holding hexamers and dimers together; they do not contain extra zinc that ejects monomers. Short-acting insulins, including the rapid analogs, are clear solutions at neutral pH; their speed comes from amino acid changes that destabilize dimers and hexamers, not dermal precipitation. The faster absorption comes from amino acid substitutions that disrupt dimer and hexamer formation, not from albumin binding of a negatively charged analog.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rapid-acting analog mechanism = hexamer disruption → faster monomer absorption. Know the three analogs: lispro (Humalog), aspart (Novolog/Fiasp), glulisine (Apidra). All have ~5% absorption variability vs ~25% for regular. COMLEX: Rapid-acting analogs are the standard for CSII pumps and mimic the physiologic prandial insulin spike — key for insulin pump initiation counseling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — The glucose-stimulated insulin secretion mechanism defines the prandial kinetic profile that short-acting insulin preparations are engineered to replicate at the pharmacologic level","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Rapid-acting analogs have amino acid substitutions that weak","C":"Short-acting insulins, including the rapid analogs, are clea","D":"The faster absorption comes from amino acid substitutions th"}},{"id":"sdl32_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacologic effects of short-acting insulin preparations.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the pharmacokinetic profile of regular human insulin administered subcutaneously, and identifies its primary inpatient clinical use that distinguishes it from the rapid-acting insulin analogs?","choices":{"D":"Regular insulin has an onset of 0.5–1 hour, a flat peakless profile, and a duration of approximately 24 hours; it is formulated at acidic pH to prevent precipitation in the subcutaneous depot, which is why it cannot be mixed with intermediate-acting insulins in the same syringe","A":"Regular insulin has an onset of 5–15 minutes, a peak at approximately 1 hour, and a duration of 3–4 hours; it is preferred over rapid-acting analogs in insulin pumps because its longer duration allows less frequent reservoir refills and reduces the risk of ketoacidosis from pump malfunction","B":"Regular insulin has an onset of 2–4 hours, a peak at 6–7 hours, and a duration of 10–20 hours; its prolonged action profile makes it appropriate for once-daily basal dosing in patients with type 2 diabetes who require predictable overnight glucose control without a pronounced peak","C":"Regular insulin has an onset of 30–60 minutes, a peak at approximately 2 hours, and a duration of 6–8 hours; because it must be given 45 minutes before meals rather than immediately before eating, it is now preferred over rapid-acting analogs in patients with gastroparesis and unpredictable gastric emptying rates","E":"Regular insulin has an onset of 30–60 minutes, a peak at approximately 2 hours, and a duration of 6–8 hours; it is the only insulin preparation approved for intravenous administration, making it the standard for continuous IV insulin infusion in diabetic ketoacidosis and perioperative glucose management"},"explanation":"Regular human insulin: onset 30–60 min, peak ~2 h, duration 6–8 h; given SC 45 min before meals (vs. immediately for rapid-acting analogs). Critically, regular insulin is the only insulin approved for IV use — it is the standard for DKA IV drips and perioperative insulin infusions. Rapid-acting analogs are not approved for IV use. U500 regular insulin is also available for severely insulin-resistant patients. The 5–15 minute, 3–4 hour profile belongs to rapid-acting analogs, the 2–4 hour, 10–20 hour profile to NPH, and the flat 24-hour acidic-pH profile to glargine; the gastroparesis option gives the correct regular-insulin kinetics but falsely claims regular insulin is now preferred in gastroparesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Regular insulin = only insulin for IV use = standard of care for DKA infusion. SC timing: regular = 45 min before meal; rapid-acting = immediately before meal. U500 regular (Humulin R U500) is 5× concentration for severe insulin resistance — requires U500 syringe. COMLEX: In DKA, potassium must be replaced alongside the insulin drip because insulin shifts K+ into cells (Bootcamp).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — The glucose-stimulated insulin secretion mechanism defines the prandial kinetic profile that short-acting insulin preparations are engineered to replicate at the pharmacologic level","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"B":"The 5–15 minute, 3–4 hour prof","C":"The 5–15 minute, 3–4 hour prof"}},{"id":"sdl32_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacologic effects of short-acting insulin preparations.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following most accurately describes the key contraindications and most frequently reported adverse effect of inhaled insulin (technosphere insulin, Afrezza) that distinguish it from subcutaneous insulin preparations?","choices":{"E":"Inhaled insulin is absolutely contraindicated in patients who smoke or have chronic lung disease such as asthma or COPD, and it causes a small but persistent decrease in FEV1 over the first 3 months of use; cough is the most common adverse effect, occurring in approximately 27% of patients during clinical trials","A":"Inhaled insulin is absolutely contraindicated in patients with a history of hypersensitivity to fumaryl diketopiperazine and in those with severe hepatic impairment; the most common adverse effect is oropharyngeal candidiasis due to the high local carbohydrate concentration of the carrier excipient particles in the respiratory tract","B":"Inhaled insulin is absolutely contraindicated in patients with type 1 diabetes and in those who are pregnant, because the non-linear pharmacokinetics of pulmonary absorption make precise glycemic titration impossible; the most common adverse effect is paradoxical bronchodilation that masks early asthma exacerbations","C":"Inhaled insulin is absolutely contraindicated in patients receiving oral corticosteroids or inhaled bronchodilators, because these agents alter pulmonary surfactant composition and accelerate excipient degradation, reducing insulin bioavailability by up to 80% and producing unpredictable hyperglycemia","D":"Inhaled insulin is absolutely contraindicated in patients with renal impairment because the fumaryl diketopiperazine excipient is cleared renally and accumulates to toxic concentrations; the most common adverse effect is reversible interstitial nephritis that resolves within 4 weeks of discontinuation"},"explanation":"Technosphere insulin (Afrezza) is a dry powder inhaled regular insulin formulated on 2–2.5 μm fumaryl diketopiperazine (FDKP) crystals. Peak levels come in 12–15 minutes, maximum effect at about 1 hour, and effect returns to baseline by about 3 hours. Contraindications: smokers and chronic lung disease (COPD, asthma) — spirometry required before initiating. Katzung reports a small decrease in FEV1 in the first 3 months of use that persisted over 2 years; it gives the contraindication and the FEV1 decline side by side without stating that one causes the other. Cough affected 27% of trial participants. Two lung cancer cases were observed in the inhaled insulin arm (none in comparator). It is approved for use in adults with diabetes (Katzung Ch 41).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inhaled insulin (Afrezza) = FDKP excipient, onset 5–15 min, duration 3 h, cough 27%, FEV1 ↓, contraindicated in smokers/COPD/asthma. Get spirometry before prescribing. Faster-onset formulations: FiAsp (aspart + niacinamide, 10 min faster) and Lyumjev (lispro + citrate/treprostinil, 11 min faster). COMLEX: Pulmonary function testing is an osteopathic physical examination skill — spirometry before inhaled insulin connects directly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — The glucose-stimulated insulin secretion mechanism defines the prandial kinetic profile that short-acting insulin preparations are engineered to replicate at the pharmacologic level","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E"},{"id":"sdl32_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pharmacologic effects of long-acting insulin preparations.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which NPH (neutral protamine Hagedorn) insulin achieves its intermediate duration of action, and what is its characteristic onset, peak, and duration profile?","choices":{"A":"Protamine is combined with regular insulin to form an insoluble complex that delays subcutaneous absorption; after injection, proteolytic tissue enzymes degrade the protamine, allowing gradual insulin absorption with an onset of 2–4 hours, peak at 6–7 hours, and duration of 10–20 hours","B":"NPH insulin is formulated at acidic pH 4.0 so that it remains soluble in the vial but precipitates upon exposure to the neutral pH of subcutaneous tissue, forming a crystalline depot; the depot dissolves slowly from its surface to produce a flat, peakless profile over 18–24 hours without a discernible peak action period","C":"NPH insulin contains a C14 myristic acid chain covalently attached to the B29 lysine residue that reversibly binds serum albumin; albumin-bound insulin is gradually released into plasma, producing an onset of 0.5–1 hour with a flat, predictable duration of approximately 17 hours in most clinical settings","D":"NPH insulin is a hexameric crystal preparation stabilized by cobalt ions rather than zinc; after subcutaneous injection, cobalt slowly exchanges for zinc from interstitial fluid, destabilizing the hexamers over several hours and producing a prolonged release with onset of 4–6 hours and duration exceeding 36 hours","E":"NPH insulin is formulated as large multihexameric chains cross-linked by a gamma-L-glutamyl spacer and hexadecanoic acid; subcutaneous tissue lipases cleave the spacer over 25 hours, gradually releasing insulin monomers that produce a flat, peakless profile lasting more than 42 hours"},"explanation":"NPH (isophane) insulin is created by combining regular insulin with protamine (a positively charged fish protein) and zinc at neutral pH, creating an insoluble complex. After SC injection, tissue proteases degrade protamine, allowing gradual absorption. Profile: onset 2–4 h, peak 6–7 h, duration 10–20 h. NPH is cloudy in appearance due to the protamine-insulin complex (unlike clear regular and analog solutions). It is often mixed with regular or rapid-acting analogs. The acidic-pH depot describes glargine, the C14 myristic acid chain describes detemir, and the multihexamer with a hexadecanoic acid spacer describes degludec; NPH is not stabilized by cobalt.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NPH = cloudy (suspension), intermediate-acting, must be resuspended by rolling (not shaking) before injection. Peak at 6–7 h coincides with overnight fasting → nocturnal hypoglycemia risk. NPH is used in premixed formulations (70/30 NPH/Regular). COMLEX: NPH onset/peak timing creates nocturnal hypoglycemia — switching to peakless analogs (glargine, detemir) eliminates this risk; key clinical management decision.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Absolute insulin deficiency from T1D autoimmune beta-cell destruction eliminates endogenous basal insulin secretion, establishing the clinical necessity for peakless long-acting preparations that maintain overnight euglycemia","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"The acidic-pH depot describes ","C":"The acidic-pH depot describes ","D":"The acidic-pH depot describes ","E":"The acidic-pH depot describes "}},{"id":"sdl32_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pharmacologic effects of long-acting insulin preparations.","batch":1,"stem":"A pharmacist explains to a nursing student why insulin glargine must never be drawn into the same syringe as rapid-acting insulin analogs before injection. Which of the following best explains the physicochemical reason for this restriction?","choices":{"C":"Insulin glargine is formulated at pH 4.0 to maintain solubility in the vial; mixing it with other insulins at neutral pH causes the pH to rise, leading to precipitation of the glargine and alteration of the absorption kinetics of both insulins in an unpredictable manner","A":"Insulin glargine contains protamine as a stabilizing agent that binds and inactivates rapid-acting insulin analogs when the two preparations are combined in the same syringe, producing a mixture with prolonged absorption characteristics identical to NPH insulin rather than the expected peak-and-basal profile","B":"Insulin glargine contains hexadecanoic acid chains that covalently cross-link with the free lysine residues of rapid-acting analogs, creating large macromolecular aggregates that are not absorbed subcutaneously but instead undergo local enzymatic degradation, rendering the combined dose ineffective","D":"Insulin glargine requires refrigeration at 2–8°C because it is thermally unstable at room temperature; when combined with rapid-acting analogs that have been brought to room temperature before injection, the thermal differential causes crystalline restructuring that blocks insulin receptor binding","E":"Insulin glargine and rapid-acting analogs bind to the same hexadecanoic acid carrier protein in subcutaneous tissue, and when combined they competitively displace each other from albumin binding sites, causing both preparations to be absorbed simultaneously and negating the intended basal-bolus separation"},"explanation":"Insulin glargine's prolonged action derives from its pH-dependent solubility: it is soluble at pH 4.0 (in vial) but precipitates into a microcrystalline depot at the neutral pH of subcutaneous tissue. Individual molecules slowly dissolve from this depot → flat, peakless profile for ~24 h. Because the formulation is acidic, mixing with any other insulin (which is at neutral pH) raises the pH, causing premature precipitation of the glargine before injection and unpredictable alteration of both components' absorption. Structural changes: A21 Asn→Gly + two Arg residues added to B-chain C-terminus. Unlike NPH, glargine cannot be mixed. Glargine does not contain protamine; it is an acidic solution (pH 4) that precipitates at neutral pH, which is why it cannot be mixed. Glargine differs from human insulin by A21 Gly and two Arg residues on the B chain, not hexadecanoic acid chains; it is the acidic pH that prevents mixing. The restriction stems from glargine's pH 4.0 formulation precipitating when mixed with neutral insulins, not from thermal instability. Glargine has no hexadecanoic acid albumin-binding chain; the incompatibility is due to its acidic pH causing precipitation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Glargine = peakless, ~24h, acidic pH formulation, CANNOT be mixed. Structural modifications: A21 Asn→Gly + 2 Arg at B-chain terminus. Glargine U300 (Toujeo) = 3× concentration. Less immunogenic than human insulin in animal studies. COMLEX: Given once daily at bedtime (or any consistent time); the timing flexibility improves patient adherence — an osteopathic patient-centered care point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Absolute insulin deficiency from T1D autoimmune beta-cell destruction eliminates endogenous basal insulin secretion, establishing the clinical necessity for peakless long-acting preparations that maintain overnight euglycemia","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Glargine does not contain protamine; it is an acidic solutio","B":"Glargine differs from human insulin by A21 Gly and two Arg r","D":"The restriction stems from glargine's pH 4.0 formulation pre","E":"Glargine has no hexadecanoic acid albumin-binding chain; the"}},{"id":"sdl32_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pharmacologic effects of long-acting insulin preparations.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly identifies the structural mechanism by which insulin degludec achieves a duration of action exceeding 42 hours after subcutaneous injection, and how does this differ from the mechanism used by insulin detemir?","choices":{"B":"Degludec has threonine removed at B30 and hexadecanoic acid (C16) attached to the B29 lysine via a gamma-L-glutamyl spacer; after injection it self-assembles into large multihexameric chains in subcutaneous tissue that slowly dissolve into monomers, giving a half-life of 25 hours and duration exceeding 42 hours — whereas detemir uses a shorter C14 myristic acid chain that provides reversible albumin binding rather than multihexamer formation","A":"Degludec achieves its ultra-long duration by incorporating two additional arginine residues at the B-chain C-terminus and substituting asparagine for glycine at position A21, the same modifications used by glargine; the difference from detemir is that degludec is formulated at a lower pH of 3.5, producing a denser crystalline depot that dissolves more slowly than glargine's pH 4.0 formulation","C":"Degludec extends its duration of action through covalent attachment of a polyethylene glycol chain at the B29 lysine that increases the hydrodynamic radius of the molecule; this modification prevents glomerular filtration and dramatically extends the serum half-life, whereas detemir relies on non-covalent albumin binding that can be displaced by fatty acids in fasted patients","D":"Degludec achieves its ultra-long action by incorporating an omega-3 fatty acid chain at B29 that is too long for standard albumin binding but forms stable liposome-like structures in subcutaneous tissue; these structures gradually release insulin monomers over more than 48 hours — whereas detemir uses a shorter omega-6 chain that binds albumin without forming supramolecular aggregates","E":"Degludec contains a protamine-hexadecanoic acid dual modification at B29 that combines the protamine-delayed absorption of NPH with the fatty acid albumin-binding of detemir; the combination of both mechanisms produces a duration exceeding 42 hours, whereas detemir uses protamine alone without any fatty acid conjugation at the B29 lysine position"},"explanation":"Insulin degludec: B30-Thr removed; B29-Lys conjugated to hexadecanoic acid (C16) via a gamma-L-glutamyl linker. In solution (with phenol/zinc), it forms dihexamers; after SC injection the phenol disperses and it self-assembles into massive multihexameric chains (thousands of molecules). These chains slowly dissolve at their ends, releasing monomers. Half-life ~25 h, duration >42 h, flat profile. Detemir: B30-Thr dropped; B29-Lys acylated with myristic acid (C14) — shorter chain → self-aggregation and reversible albumin binding → duration of action ~17 h. Both are peakless, but degludec is longer-acting and available in U100 and U200. The two added arginines and A21 Gly are glargine's modifications, not degludec's; degludec forms multihexameric chains and has a C16 fatty acid on B29. Degludec has a hexadecanoic acid attached via a gamma-L-glutamyl linker, not a polyethylene glycol chain. Degludec is acylated with hexadecanoic acid (C16) and forms multihexameric chains, not an omega-3 fatty acid forming liposome-like structures. Degludec does not contain protamine; its duration comes from the C16 chain and multihexamer formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Degludec = hexadecanoic acid (C16), multihexamers, >42h, half-life 25h, U100/U200. Detemir = myristic acid (C14), albumin binding, ~17h. Glargine = pH 4.0 precipitation, ~24h. All three are peakless (flat). Icodec (weekly) = C20 diacid, strongest albumin binding. COMLEX: Degludec U200 and glargine U300 allow injection of 2-3× more insulin in the same volume — key for patients needing large doses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Absolute insulin deficiency from T1D autoimmune beta-cell destruction eliminates endogenous basal insulin secretion, establishing the clinical necessity for peakless long-acting preparations that maintain overnight euglycemia","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"The two added arginines and A21 Gly are glargine's modificat","C":"Degludec has a hexadecanoic acid attached via a gamma-L-glut","D":"Degludec is acylated with hexadecanoic acid (C16) and forms ","E":"Degludec does not contain protamine; its duration comes from"}},{"id":"sdl32_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the types of insulin delivery systems.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best explains why rapid-acting insulin analogs rather than regular insulin are used in continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion (CSII) pump therapy?","choices":{"A":"Rapid-acting analogs have approximately 5% absorption variability compared with approximately 25% for regular insulin, and their consistently short, predictable duration of action is essential for accurate algorithmic dosing in pumps; regular insulin's prolonged and highly variable absorption would make precise pump-delivered dosing unreliable","B":"Rapid-acting analogs are used in CSII pumps because their hexameric formulation resists degradation by subcutaneous tissue proteases for up to 72 hours in the pump reservoir, whereas regular insulin degrades within 24 hours in the reservoir and would require daily cartridge changes that reduce pump utility","C":"Rapid-acting analogs are preferred in CSII pumps because they are formulated without zinc, preventing the buildup of zinc crystals that would otherwise occlude the infusion catheter tubing over time and lead to delivery failure; regular insulin's high zinc content makes it incompatible with standard CSII catheter materials","D":"Rapid-acting analogs must be used in CSII pumps because they are the only insulins cleared renally rather than hepatically; renal clearance provides a consistent half-life independent of liver function, ensuring that patients with coexisting hepatic impairment receive accurate and reproducible insulin delivery throughout the day","E":"Rapid-acting analogs are mandated in CSII pumps because their negatively charged molecular surface prevents adhesion to the inner walls of polyurethane infusion catheters; regular insulin's neutral charge causes it to adsorb onto catheter surfaces and lose up to 30% of its dose before reaching the subcutaneous injection site"},"explanation":"CSII (insulin pumps) rely on precise, predictable insulin delivery for algorithmic basal rate adjustment and bolus calculation. Rapid-acting analogs (lispro, aspart, glulisine) have ~5% absorption variability vs ~25% for regular insulin, and their short, consistent 3–4 h duration allows the pump algorithm to accurately track active insulin on board. Regular insulin's 6–8 h duration and high variability would lead to insulin stacking and unpredictable glucose responses. All modern insulin pumps use rapid-acting analogs exclusively. Pumps use rapid-acting analogs for their short, predictable duration and lower absorption variability, not for a hexameric formulation resisting degradation for 72 hours. The course gives lower absorption variability (about 5% vs 25%) and short predictable duration as the reason, not a zinc-free formulation preventing catheter occlusion. The analogs' amino acid changes leave their circulating half-life identical to regular insulin's; pumps use them for lower absorption variability and a consistent duration. The course cites absorption variability and short duration allowing accurate algorithmic dosing, not catheter wall adhesion of regular insulin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CSII = only rapid-acting analogs; never NPH, glargine, detemir, or degludec. Complications of CSII: DKA (no long-acting backup if catheter fails), skin infections, cost/complexity. Advantage: matches physiologic insulin secretion pattern, best HbA1c reduction. COMLEX: CSII appropriate for motivated T1D patients willing to monitor glucose 4-6×/day and carbohydrate count — patient education is a COMLEX patient management objective.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — The physiologic pulsatile pattern of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion during meals provides the kinetic template that closed-loop delivery systems attempt to replicate through continuous glucose monitoring and algorithmic dose adjustment","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Pumps use rapid-acting analogs for their short, predictable ","C":"The course gives lower absorption variability (about 5% vs 2","D":"The analogs' amino acid changes leave their circulating half","E":"The course cites absorption variability and short duration a"}},{"id":"sdl32_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the types of insulin delivery systems.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes how FDA-approved closed-loop insulin delivery systems differ from conventional open-loop continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion pumps, and what glucose targets do two major systems use?","choices":{"B":"Closed-loop systems integrate real-time CGM glucose data with an automated algorithm that adjusts basal insulin delivery every few minutes without patient input; the MiniMed 770G targets a sensor glucose of 120 mg/dL while the Tandem Control-IQ targets 112.5 mg/dL, contrasted with open-loop pumps where only the patient initiates basal rate changes","A":"Closed-loop systems differ from open-loop pumps by delivering both insulin and glucagon simultaneously; insulin is delivered when the CGM detects hyperglycemia and glucagon is automatically delivered when hypoglycemia is predicted, whereas open-loop pumps deliver only insulin and rely on patients to self-treat hypoglycemia with carbohydrate ingestion","C":"Closed-loop systems use a dual-reservoir design that simultaneously infuses basal insulin through one cannula and delivers concentrated dextrose through a second cannula when hypoglycemia is detected; open-loop pumps use a single reservoir and require patients to program all glucose corrections through the pump keypad manually","D":"Closed-loop systems target a fixed sensor glucose of 80 mg/dL in all patients regardless of clinical condition, because reducing CGM glucose to this level has been shown to eliminate nocturnal hypoglycemia entirely; open-loop pumps allow patient-programmed glucose targets that vary between 100 and 200 mg/dL based on individual hypoglycemia awareness","E":"Closed-loop systems automatically suspend all insulin delivery when the CGM glucose drops below 70 mg/dL and restart delivery when glucose recovers above 100 mg/dL; this threshold-suspend feature is the only difference from open-loop pumps, which do not have any automatic insulin suspension capability regardless of glucose level"},"explanation":"Closed-loop systems (\"artificial pancreas\") combine a CGM sensor, a CGM-linked algorithm, and an insulin pump that automatically adjusts basal delivery: (1) MiniMed 770G — adjusts basal rates every 5 min, targets sensor glucose 120 mg/dL (6.7 mmol/L); suspends delivery when sensor glucose falls or is predicted to fall below its target; (2) Tandem Control-IQ — targets 112.5 mg/dL (6.25 mmol/L); (3) Omnipod 5 — user-set target 100–150 mg/dL. Open-loop pumps require the patient to manually program all basal rate changes. Open-source systems (Loop, OpenAPS) are not FDA-approved. Approved closed-loop systems combine CGM, an algorithm and an insulin pump to adjust basal insulin automatically; the course does not describe a glucagon-delivering approved system. The described closed-loop systems use CGM and an algorithm to adjust basal insulin, not a dual-reservoir dextrose infusion. The systems have specific targets (for example 120 mg/dL for the MiniMed 770G), not a fixed 80 mg/dL for all patients. Closed-loop systems adjust basal delivery every few minutes using CGM data; threshold suspend alone is not the course's distinction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Closed-loop = CGM + algorithm + pump. Know the three FDA-approved systems. All use rapid-acting analogs. The Omnipod 5 is tubeless (no catheter). Benefits: reduced nocturnal hypoglycemia, lower HbA1c. COMLEX: Patient selection for closed-loop: mechanically inclined, educated, motivated T1D patient — aligns with osteopathic patient-centered care and technology literacy assessment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — The physiologic pulsatile pattern of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion during meals provides the kinetic template that closed-loop delivery systems attempt to replicate through continuous glucose monitoring and algorithmic dose adjustment","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Approved closed-loop systems combine CGM, an algorithm and a","C":"The described closed-loop systems use CGM and an algorithm t","D":"The systems have specific targets (for example 120 mg/dL for","E":"Closed-loop systems adjust basal delivery every few minutes "}},{"id":"sdl32_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the types of insulin delivery systems.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient using a continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion pump calls the emergency line reporting that his pump failed 6 hours ago after the infusion catheter kinked. He takes only rapid-acting insulin aspart in his pump and has no long-acting insulin at home. He is now feeling nauseated with a blood glucose of 340 mg/dL and urine ketones are large. Which of the following best explains why this patient developed ketoacidosis so rapidly, within only 6 hours of pump failure?","choices":{"C":"Because CSII pumps exclusively use rapid-acting insulin analogs with a duration of only 3–4 hours, complete catheter failure eliminates all insulin delivery with no long-acting depot to provide basal coverage; without any insulin, unopposed glucagon immediately activates hormone-sensitive lipase and hepatic ketogenesis, producing ketoacidosis within hours","A":"Rapid-acting insulin analogs suppress glucagon secretion from alpha cells more potently than long-acting insulin preparations; when the pump fails, the cessation of glucagon suppression triggers a rebound hyperglucagonemia that is more severe than the hyperglucagonemia seen in untreated type 1 diabetes, accelerating ketoacidosis beyond what would be expected from insulin deficiency alone","B":"Insulin pump patients have higher baseline insulin requirements than those on multiple daily injections because subcutaneous infusion reduces first-pass hepatic insulin extraction; when the pump fails, the higher baseline insulin requirement means that even small interruptions in delivery create larger relative insulin deficits that drive faster ketogenesis","D":"CSII pumps use concentrated U500 insulin to minimize reservoir refill frequency; when catheter failure occurs, any residual insulin leaking from the kinked catheter is so concentrated that it paradoxically activates counterregulatory hormone release and stimulates rather than inhibits hepatic ketogenesis through a dose-dependent toxic mechanism","E":"Rapid-acting insulin analogs are phosphorylated on the B29-lysine residue during subcutaneous absorption, and this phosphorylation creates a biologically inactive form that accumulates in the interstitium; when delivery stops, the accumulated inactive insulin is rapidly dephosphorylated to an active form that causes rebound hypoglycemia before the subsequent ketoacidotic phase begins"},"explanation":"CSII's key vulnerability: only rapid-acting insulin is used (duration 3–4 h), so there is zero long-acting insulin buffer. When catheter occlusion occurs, all insulin delivery stops. Within 3–4 hours after the last effective dose, insulin levels fall below the threshold needed to suppress hormone-sensitive lipase in adipose tissue. Unopposed glucagon activates ketogenesis and glycogenolysis. DKA develops rapidly — within hours — compared to the multi-day process in a patient on once-daily long-acting insulin (who still has a subcutaneous depot absorbing during any delivery interruption). Site rotation and catheter inspection are critical teaching points. Ketoacidosis is a known pump complication when delivery is interrupted, because the pump supplies only rapid-acting insulin; no analog-specific rebound hyperglucagonemia is needed to explain it. The vulnerability is that interrupted pump delivery leaves no insulin, since pumps use only rapid-acting analogs; a higher baseline requirement from reduced hepatic extraction is not the reason. Pumps use rapid-acting analogs, here insulin aspart, not concentrated U500 regular insulin, and the ketoacidosis reflects interrupted delivery with no basal depot. Rapid-acting analogs keep a half-life identical to regular insulin and act for about 4 hours, so once delivery stops insulin simply runs out rather than reactivating from an inactive depot.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DKA is a known complication of CSII. Mechanism: no long-acting backup → rapid insulin deficiency. Other CSII complications: skin infections at infusion sites, cost, need for frequent glucose monitoring. Teach patients: if glucose rises unexpectedly, check for kinked/occluded catheter before treating with pump bolus. COMLEX: OMT does not directly treat DKA, but viscerosomatic reflexes (T5–T9, alternate T6–T10, OMM course map; reflex T5–T9 with sympathetic T5–T11, OMM atlas Ch 15) are associated with pancreatic function — know this for written boards.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — The physiologic pulsatile pattern of glucose-stimulated insulin secretion during meals provides the kinetic template that closed-loop delivery systems attempt to replicate through continuous glucose monitoring and algorithmic dose adjustment","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"Ketoacidosis is a known pump complication when delivery is i","B":"The vulnerability is that interrupted pump delivery leaves n","D":"Pumps use rapid-acting analogs, here insulin aspart, not con","E":"Rapid-acting analogs keep a half-life identical to regular i"}},{"id":"sdl32_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the immunopathology of insulin therapy.","batch":1,"stem":"Which immunoglobulin class mediates insulin allergy, and what is the cellular effector mechanism responsible for the immediate hypersensitivity reaction observed after insulin injection?","choices":{"B":"IgE anti-insulin antibodies sensitize tissue mast cells; upon re-exposure to insulin, cross-linking of mast cell surface IgE triggers degranulation and histamine release, producing the urticaria and — in severe cases — anaphylaxis that characterize the immediate hypersensitivity reaction to insulin","A":"IgG anti-insulin antibodies form immune complexes that activate the complement cascade through the classical pathway, leading to C3a and C5a anaphylatoxin generation that triggers mast cell degranulation indirectly; the resultant urticaria and bronchospasm are complement-dependent and can be blocked by C5a receptor antagonists","C":"IgM anti-insulin antibodies agglutinate insulin molecules in the subcutaneous depot, creating macroaggregates that activate innate immune toll-like receptors on dermal dendritic cells; these dendritic cells migrate to regional lymph nodes and initiate a T-cell-dependent delayed hypersensitivity reaction peaking 48–72 hours after injection","D":"IgA anti-insulin antibodies secreted into subcutaneous interstitial fluid bind to insulin dimers and recruit eosinophils via IgA Fc receptor (FcαRI) signaling; eosinophil-derived major basic protein then damages local capillary endothelium and produces the localized wheal-and-flare reaction characteristic of insulin allergy","E":"IgD anti-insulin antibodies are the dominant mediators of insulin allergy because they are exclusively expressed on the surface of basophils in insulin-treated patients; IgD cross-linking by insulin triggers basophil leukotriene C4 release rather than histamine, producing a delayed bronchospasm and angioedema reaction that peaks at 4–6 hours post-injection"},"explanation":"Insulin allergy is a Type I (immediate) hypersensitivity reaction mediated by IgE. Mechanism: first exposure → IgE production → IgE binds to Fcε receptors on mast cells and basophils (sensitization). Second exposure → insulin cross-links IgE → mast cell/basophil degranulation → histamine, tryptase, leukotrienes released → urticaria, angioedema, bronchospasm, anaphylaxis. Sensitivity is often to non-insulin protein contaminants (not insulin itself), which is why purified human recombinant insulin and analogs have markedly reduced allergy incidence. Five immunoglobulin classes may be produced: IgA, IgD, IgE, IgG, IgM — but only IgE mediates the immediate allergic reaction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Insulin allergy = Type I hypersensitivity = IgE-mediated = immediate. Contrast with immune resistance (IgG, delayed). Switch to purified human analog insulin if allergic. Severe systemic allergy: desensitization protocols exist. COMLEX: Anaphylaxis treatment = epinephrine IM (1:1000, 0.3-0.5 mg); diphenhydramine + steroids are adjuncts, not first-line — high-yield emergency medicine point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Lifelong exogenous insulin dependence imposed by T1D autoimmune beta-cell destruction creates repeated antigenic exposure to a foreign protein, establishing the immunological substrate for IgE-mediated insulin allergy and IgG-mediated immune resistance","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Five immunoglobulin classes ma","C":"Five immunoglobulin classes ma","D":"Five immunoglobulin classes ma","E":"Five immunoglobulin classes ma"}},{"id":"sdl32_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the immunopathology of insulin therapy.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes immune insulin resistance, including the immunoglobulin class responsible, the clinical significance in most patients, and the conditions with which it may be associated?","choices":{"A":"Immune insulin resistance is caused by low-titer IgG anti-insulin antibodies that develop in most insulin-treated patients; in the vast majority this is clinically inconsequential, but in rare cases high antibody titers neutralize administered insulin and require very high insulin doses to achieve glycemic control, and this condition may be associated with other systemic autoimmune diseases such as lupus erythematosus","B":"Immune insulin resistance is caused by high-titer IgM anti-insulin antibodies that form in virtually all insulin-treated patients within 6 months of initiating therapy; these antibodies invariably cause clinically significant insulin resistance requiring dose escalation in all affected patients, and the condition resolves spontaneously once insulin therapy is discontinued for at least 3 months","C":"Immune insulin resistance is mediated by IgE anti-insulin antibodies identical to those responsible for insulin allergy, but at concentrations below the threshold required to trigger mast cell degranulation; these low-level IgE antibodies bind injected insulin in the subcutaneous depot and delay its absorption rather than neutralizing its receptor binding capacity","D":"Immune insulin resistance develops exclusively in patients with type 1 diabetes because the autoimmune destruction of beta cells generates a broad anti-islet antibody response that cross-reacts with exogenous insulin; patients with type 2 diabetes do not produce anti-insulin IgG antibodies because their endogenous insulin production prevents antigenic presentation of the exogenous preparation","E":"Immune insulin resistance is caused by IgA anti-insulin secretory antibodies produced by gut-associated lymphoid tissue during subcutaneous insulin absorption; these antibodies are transported to the systemic circulation via the mesenteric lymphatics and neutralize insulin in the portal blood before it can reach hepatic insulin receptors"},"explanation":"Immune insulin resistance: most insulin-treated patients develop a low titer of IgG anti-insulin antibodies — this is nearly universal but clinically insignificant in the vast majority. Rarely, titers rise high enough to neutralize significant portions of administered insulin, causing clinically relevant resistance requiring very high doses. This high-titer form may occur in the setting of other systemic autoimmune conditions (SLE mentioned in Katzung). Human recombinant and analog insulins are far less immunogenic than older animal-derived preparations and have greatly reduced the incidence of clinically significant immune resistance. Immune insulin resistance involves low-titer IgG antibodies that are usually inconsequential, not high-titer IgM antibodies that invariably cause resistance. The course describes IgG anti-insulin antibodies, not low-level IgE antibodies related to insulin allergy. Low-titer IgG anti-insulin antibodies develop in most insulin-treated patients, so it is not limited to type 1 diabetes. Immune insulin resistance is caused by IgG anti-insulin antibodies, not IgA secretory antibodies from gut-associated lymphoid tissue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Immune insulin resistance = IgG anti-insulin antibodies (usually clinically silent). Distinguish from receptor-level insulin resistance (T2D) — antibody-mediated is immune. Rare but testable: high IgG titer → massive insulin requirements. Switch to human analog. If severe: consider short course steroids or immunosuppression. COMLEX: Insulin antibodies are also used diagnostically — if a hypoglycemic patient has insulin antibodies, suspect surreptitious exogenous insulin use (low C-peptide); insulinoma raises C-peptide and does not cause anti-insulin antibodies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Lifelong exogenous insulin dependence imposed by T1D autoimmune beta-cell destruction creates repeated antigenic exposure to a foreign protein, establishing the immunological substrate for IgE-mediated insulin allergy and IgG-mediated immune resistance","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Immune insulin resistance involves low-titer IgG antibodies ","C":"The course describes IgG anti-insulin antibodies, not low-le","D":"Low-titer IgG anti-insulin antibodies develop in most insuli","E":"Immune insulin resistance is caused by IgG anti-insulin anti"}},{"id":"sdl32_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the immunopathology of insulin therapy.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes lipohypertrophy from lipoatrophy as complications of insulin injection therapy, including the mechanism of each and the appropriate management?","choices":{"D":"Lipohypertrophy is a non-immune complication caused by the local lipogenic and mitogenic effects of repeatedly injected insulin stimulating subcutaneous fat and connective tissue proliferation at a single site; lipoatrophy was an immune complication of injected animal insulin preparations that caused local fat atrophy and is now almost never seen with neutral-pH human and analog preparations","A":"Lipohypertrophy is an immune complication caused by IgE-mediated mast cell activation at repeated injection sites, which promotes local fibroblast proliferation and fatty tissue expansion through histamine-induced growth factor release; lipoatrophy is the non-immune counterpart that results from excessive insulin-mediated lipolysis at injection sites that exceeds the local lipogenic response","B":"Lipohypertrophy and lipoatrophy are both immune complications, but they differ in the immunoglobulin class involved: lipohypertrophy is mediated by IgG antibodies forming immune complexes that stimulate local adipocyte hyperplasia via complement-mediated cytokine release, while lipoatrophy is mediated by IgE antibodies triggering mast cell degranulation and local tissue atrophy","C":"Both lipohypertrophy and lipoatrophy result from the same underlying immune mechanism: IgM-mediated complement activation at insulin injection sites; lipohypertrophy occurs in patients with complement C3 deficiency, which redirects the inflammatory response toward adipocyte proliferation, while lipoatrophy occurs in complement-sufficient patients in whom C5b-9 membrane attack complex destroys local adipocytes","E":"Lipohypertrophy occurs because human recombinant insulin analogs have been engineered to bind IGF-1 receptors on subcutaneous adipocytes, stimulating de novo lipogenesis; lipoatrophy is an immune complication unique to insulin glargine because its acidic formulation pH 4.0 activates the NLRP3 inflammasome in local macrophages, causing IL-1β-mediated adipocyte apoptosis at injection sites"},"explanation":"Lipoatrophy: atrophy of subcutaneous fat at injection sites — Katzung (course reading) calls it an immune complication of injected animal insulin preparations that is almost never seen since neutral-pH human and analog preparations were developed; it does not name the antibody or complement step. Injecting the newer preparations directly into the atrophic area often restores normal contours. Lipohypertrophy: hypertrophy of subcutaneous fat from repeated injection at the same site — a non-immune, pharmacodynamic effect of insulin's local lipogenic and proliferative actions on adipocytes. Corrected by site rotation. Lipohypertrophy is actually the more clinically common complication today and can cause erratic insulin absorption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lipoatrophy = immune, animal-derived insulin, rare with human analogs. Lipohypertrophy = non-immune, site rotation corrects it, common, causes variable absorption. Lipohypertrophy is a common reason for unexplained poor glycemic control in insulin-dependent patients who never rotate sites. COMLEX: Site rotation is a key patient education point — alternating between abdomen, thigh, upper arm, and buttock; fastest absorption from abdomen.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Lifelong exogenous insulin dependence imposed by T1D autoimmune beta-cell destruction creates repeated antigenic exposure to a foreign protein, establishing the immunological substrate for IgE-mediated insulin allergy and IgG-mediated immune resistance","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Lipohypertrophy: hypertrophy o","B":"Lipohypertrophy: hypertrophy o","C":"Lipohypertrophy: hypertrophy o"}},{"id":"sdl32_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacologic effects of short-acting insulin preparations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes mellitus visits her endocrinologist to review her insulin regimen. She works as a restaurant server and reports that her meal timing is unpredictable — she often cannot eat for 2–3 hours after her insulin injection, and on other days she eats within 15 minutes of finishing her injection. She has been using regular insulin before meals and reports repeated hypoglycemic episodes on days when her meal is delayed and persistent postprandial hyperglycemia on days when she eats quickly. Which of the following insulin changes would best address her pharmacokinetic problem?","choices":{"C":"Switch to insulin glargine given once daily before bed and eliminate premeal insulin entirely, since the peakless 24-hour coverage of glargine provides sufficient postprandial glucose control without any prandial insulin in a patient whose carbohydrate intake is highly variable across working days","A":"Switch to NPH insulin given twice daily, since its intermediate duration of 10–20 hours would smooth out the glucose fluctuations caused by unpredictable meal timing and eliminate the need for pre-meal insulin injections that must be precisely timed relative to carbohydrate consumption","B":"Switch to inhaled insulin (technosphere insulin) given at each meal, since its dry-powder formulation is absorbed directly across alveolar membranes without subcutaneous variability and its 1-hour peak action can be precisely controlled by the patient through adjustment of inhalation depth and volume","D":"Switch to a rapid-acting insulin analog (insulin lispro, aspart, or glulisine) that can be administered immediately before or even immediately after meal onset, as its 5–15 minute onset and 3–4 hour duration more closely match actual meal timing and reduce hypoglycemia risk during meal delays","E":"Continue regular insulin but adjust the pre-meal injection timing to 60 minutes before the anticipated meal on days when she expects delays, since extending the pre-injection interval beyond the standard 45 minutes produces a lower but more sustained early peak that provides better coverage for delayed carbohydrate absorption"},"explanation":"This case highlights the key clinical advantage of rapid-acting analogs over regular insulin: flexible meal timing. Regular insulin's 30–60 min onset requires patients to eat within ~45 min of injection — delayed meals cause hypoglycemia (peak without glucose substrate); rapid eating after a recent injection causes postprandial hyperglycemia (glucose comes before insulin peak). Rapid-acting analogs (onset 5–15 min) can be given immediately before or right at the start of a meal because they act almost simultaneously with carbohydrate absorption, making them far better suited for unpredictable schedules. Lispro, aspart, and glulisine are all equally appropriate choices. NPH peaks at 6 to 7 hours and is usually combined with regular or rapid-acting insulin before meals, so it does not remove the need for mealtime insulin. Inhaled insulin is dosed with fixed 4-, 8- or 12-unit cartridges taken just before the meal, not adjusted by inhalation depth and volume. In type 1 diabetes, physiologic replacement combines long-acting basal insulin with rapid-acting mealtime analogs, so glargine alone cannot cover meals. Regular insulin is optimally injected about 45 minutes before the meal, so it cannot match unpredictable meal times; rapid analogs can be injected immediately before eating.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clinical advantage of rapid-acting analogs = flexible timing relative to meals. Regular insulin: 45 min before meal (hypoglycemia if meal delayed). Rapid-acting: give immediately before eating (or with first bite). FiAsp (aspart + niacinamide) starts acting about 10 minutes faster than standard aspart (Katzung Ch 41). COMLEX: Lifestyle-compatible insulin regimens improve adherence — patient-centered scheduling is an osteopathic principle reflected in COMLEX clinical scenarios.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 1 — Short-Acting Insulin Preparations: Clinical advantages of rapid-acting analogs for unpredictable meal timing","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"NPH peaks at 6 to 7 hours and is usually combined with regul","B":"Inhaled insulin is dosed with fixed 4-, 8- or 12-unit cartri","C":"In type 1 diabetes, physiologic replacement combines long-ac","E":"Regular insulin is optimally injected about 45 minutes befor"}},{"id":"sdl32_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacologic effects of short-acting insulin preparations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man with type 1 diabetes mellitus is brought to the emergency department by his roommate with a 12-hour history of vomiting, abdominal pain, and confusion. He missed his insulin for 2 days. Vital signs: HR 118 bpm, RR 26 breaths/min (deep and labored), BP 96/60 mmHg, temperature 37.3°C. Blood glucose is 522 mg/dL. ABG: pH 7.17, pCO2 16 mmHg, HCO3 6 mEq/L. Serum K+ is 4.2 mEq/L. Anion gap is 28. Urine ketones are 4+. The team prepares to initiate insulin therapy. Which of the following insulin preparations and route of administration is most appropriate for the initial management of this patient?","choices":{"A":"Regular human insulin administered as a continuous intravenous infusion is the appropriate choice; it is the only insulin preparation approved for IV use, allowing precise titration of dose in real time as glucose levels fall and the anion gap closes during DKA treatment","B":"Insulin glargine administered subcutaneously once daily is the most appropriate initial treatment because its peakless 24-hour profile prevents hypoglycemia during the recovery phase of DKA, and its subcutaneous route avoids the risk of venous complications associated with prolonged IV catheter placement in hemodynamically unstable patients","C":"Insulin lispro administered as a subcutaneous injection every 4 hours is the most appropriate choice because its rapid onset of 5–15 minutes provides faster correction of hyperglycemia than IV regular insulin and its short 3–4 hour duration eliminates the risk of prolonged hypoglycemia if the glucose correction overshoots the target range","D":"Inhaled regular insulin (technosphere insulin) administered every 2 hours is the most appropriate choice for initial DKA management because pulmonary absorption bypasses the erratic subcutaneous absorption seen in dehydrated patients with reduced peripheral perfusion, ensuring consistent bioavailability throughout the acute resuscitation phase","E":"Insulin degludec administered subcutaneously is the most appropriate initial treatment in this patient because its ultra-long duration of more than 42 hours would maintain a continuous basal insulin level throughout the DKA treatment period, eliminating the need for nursing staff to manage an IV insulin infusion that carries risks of catheter infection"},"explanation":"Regular insulin is the only insulin approved for IV use. In DKA, a continuous IV insulin infusion allows precise, titrateable delivery — the infusion rate can be adjusted every hour based on glucose trajectory and anion gap closure. SC rapid-acting analogs have variable absorption in DKA due to poor peripheral perfusion from dehydration and acidosis, making IV the preferred route. Critical DKA management pearls: (1) IV fluids first (isotonic saline); (2) Replace K+ along with insulin (insulin drives K+ intracellularly, worsening hypokalemia); (3) Transition to SC insulin only once patient can eat and DKA is resolved. Glargine is a once-daily subcutaneous basal insulin; DKA treatment uses intravenous regular insulin, usually starting at about 0.1 U/kg/h, with aggressive IV fluids. DKA is treated with intravenous regular insulin at about 0.1 U/kg/h; inhaled insulin is a mealtime insulin taken immediately before meals. Degludec is a once-daily subcutaneous basal insulin lasting over 42 hours; DKA uses intravenous regular insulin with frequent reevaluation and dose modification.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DKA treatment: IV regular insulin. Only regular insulin is given IV. Replace K+ as insulin drives it into cells; typical starting rate about 0.1 U/kg/h (Katzung Ch 41); give glucose with insulin as glucose falls while the anion gap closes (Bootcamp). COMLEX: Kussmaul breathing (deep, labored) = respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis; pCO2 drops to offset the low HCO3, but the pH stays acidemic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 1 — Short-Acting Insulin Preparations: Regular insulin as the only IV-approved preparation for DKA management","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"Glargine is a once-daily subcutaneous basal insulin; DKA tre","C":"SC rapid-acting analogs have v","D":"DKA is treated with intravenous regular insulin at about 0.1","E":"Degludec is a once-daily subcutaneous basal insulin lasting "}},{"id":"sdl32_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the pharmacologic effects of short-acting insulin preparations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes mellitus, currently managed with insulin glargine at bedtime, tells her endocrinologist she is interested in switching to inhaled insulin (technosphere insulin, Afrezza) to avoid subcutaneous injections. She has a 6-year smoking history but stopped 3 years ago. She has no current respiratory symptoms. BMI is 28 kg/m². Which of the following is the most critical evaluation step that must be completed before prescribing inhaled insulin for this patient?","choices":{"D":"Arterial blood gas analysis with measurement of the A-a gradient must be performed before initiating inhaled insulin, because the fumaryl diketopiperazine excipient causes progressive alveolar-capillary block that reduces pulmonary oxygen diffusion, and only patients with an A-a gradient below 15 mmHg are considered safe candidates for inhaled insulin therapy","A":"Measurement of serum IgE and skin prick testing to the fumaryl diketopiperazine excipient must be performed before prescribing inhaled insulin, because the carrier excipient (not insulin itself) is the primary allergen responsible for Type I hypersensitivity reactions, and untested patients risk anaphylaxis with the first cartridge use","B":"Bronchial provocation testing with methacholine must be performed to detect occult bronchial hyperresponsiveness before initiating inhaled insulin, because even a single inhalation in patients with subclinical asthma can trigger life-threatening bronchospasm that is not predicted by clinical history or spirometry","C":"Pulmonary function testing (spirometry) measuring FEV1 must be performed before initiating inhaled insulin to identify potential lung disease, because inhaled insulin causes a small but persistent decline in FEV1 and is contraindicated in persons who currently smoke or who have chronic lung disease such as asthma or COPD","E":"High-resolution CT of the chest must be performed before initiating inhaled insulin in all patients regardless of smoking history, because the drug label requires confirmation of normal alveolar architecture on imaging before the first dose, given that two cases of lung cancer were observed in the inhaled insulin arm of clinical trials"},"explanation":"Katzung (course reading) says spirometry should be performed to identify potential lung disease before inhaled insulin (technosphere insulin, Afrezza) is started. Katzung reports a small decrease in FEV1 in the first 3 months of use that persisted over 2 years of follow-up, and says inhaled insulin is contraindicated in persons who smoke or who have chronic lung disease such as asthma and COPD. Cough was the most common adverse effect (27% of trial patients), and there were two cases of lung cancer in the inhaled insulin arm and none in the comparator arm; Katzung does not require chest CT, methacholine testing, blood gases, or allergy testing. This patient stopped smoking 3 years ago, so baseline spirometry is the step that decides whether she is a candidate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inhaled insulin requires spirometry before prescribing. Contraindicated: current smokers, COPD, asthma. Former smokers: evaluate with spirometry. Cough = most common AE (27%). Two lung cancer cases in trial (none in comparator). Peak levels in 12–15 minutes, back to baseline in 3 hours (Katzung Ch 41). COMLEX: Spirometry is a standard pulmonary function test — FEV1/FVC ratio differentiates obstructive (asthma, COPD) from restrictive disease; knowing the rationale for pre-prescribing spirometry tests pulmonary pharmacology integration.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 1 — Short-Acting Insulin Preparations: Pre-prescribing spirometry requirement for inhaled insulin and pulmonary safety monitoring","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C"},{"id":"sdl32_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pharmacologic effects of long-acting insulin preparations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes mellitus managed with twice-daily NPH insulin and pre-meal regular insulin presents complaining of recurrent nighttime hypoglycemic episodes that wake her at 2–3 AM with diaphoresis and palpitations. Her fasting morning glucoses are frequently below 60 mg/dL despite reducing her bedtime NPH dose. Her daytime glucose control is otherwise acceptable. Which pharmacokinetic feature of NPH insulin is most directly responsible for her nocturnal hypoglycemia, and which medication change would best address it?","choices":{"C":"NPH insulin given at bedtime peaks 6–7 hours after injection, coinciding with the early morning fasting period (2–4 AM) when no carbohydrate intake buffers the peak effect; switching to a peakless long-acting analog such as insulin glargine or insulin detemir eliminates the pronounced peak and provides consistent overnight basal coverage without hypoglycemia","A":"NPH insulin given at bedtime has an onset of 30–60 minutes that coincides with the start of the nocturnal growth hormone surge at 10 PM; the combination of early insulin peak and growth hormone counter-regulation produces rebound hyperglycemia at midnight followed by hypoglycemia as growth hormone levels fall, and switching to rapid-acting lispro at bedtime would prevent this pattern","B":"NPH insulin has a duration of only 4–5 hours, which means the bedtime dose wears off by 2–3 AM and exposes the patient to a counterregulatory hormone surge without basal insulin coverage; switching to regular insulin given at 1 AM would provide continuous overnight coverage and prevent the nocturnal episodes caused by NPH's insufficient duration of action","D":"NPH insulin is absorbed erratically from the subcutaneous depot during sleep because the supine position reduces peripheral blood flow to injection sites; the accumulated depot that failed to absorb during sleep is rapidly released upon waking when perfusion is restored, producing early morning hypoglycemia that is misperceived as nocturnal hypoglycemia by the patient","E":"NPH insulin binds to hepatic insulin receptors at 2–3 AM and activates glycogen synthase, causing excessive hepatic glycogen deposition that depletes circulating glucose; switching to an insulin that does not reach the liver in its peak-action state would prevent the transient hepatic glucose sequestration that underlies this patient's nocturnal hypoglycemia pattern"},"explanation":"NPH given at bedtime (typically 10–11 PM) reaches its peak action at 6–7 hours post-injection — placing peak insulin effect in the early morning hours (about 2–6 AM, depending on when it is injected), during the deepest fasting state when there is no food intake to prevent hypoglycemia. This is the classic nocturnal hypoglycemia pattern of NPH. Peakless long-acting analogs (glargine, detemir, degludec) produce a flat insulin profile without this pharmacodynamic peak, eliminating overnight hypoglycemia risk. This was one of the primary clinical advantages that drove adoption of long-acting analogs over NPH. NPH has an onset of 2 to 4 hours and peaks at 6 to 7 hours, so a bedtime dose peaks in the early-morning fast; it does not act within 30 to 60 minutes. NPH lasts 10 to 20 hours, and the early-morning hypoglycemia comes from its 6 to 7 hour peak, not from the dose wearing off. The hypoglycemia comes from NPH's peak 6 to 7 hours after a bedtime dose falling in the overnight fast, not from delayed release of an unabsorbed depot. The hypoglycemia reflects NPH's 6 to 7 hour peak falling during the overnight fast, not hepatic glycogen deposition depleting circulating glucose.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NPH peak 6–7h → nocturnal hypoglycemia at 2–4 AM if given at bedtime. Switch to glargine or detemir to eliminate peak. Glargine = ~24h, peakless; detemir = ~17h, peakless; degludec = >42h, peakless. Somogyi effect (rebound hyperglycemia from nocturnal hypoglycemia) vs. dawn phenomenon (hyperglycemia from GH/cortisol rise). COMLEX: Sleeping patient cannot detect or treat hypoglycemia → safety advantage of peakless analogs is critical patient safety content.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 2 — Long-Acting Insulin Preparations: NPH nocturnal hypoglycemia and the clinical rationale for switching to peakless analogs","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"NPH has an onset of 2 to 4 hours and peaks at 6 to 7 hours, ","B":"NPH lasts 10 to 20 hours, and the early-morning hypoglycemia","D":"The hypoglycemia comes from NPH's peak 6 to 7 hours after a ","E":"The hypoglycemia reflects NPH's 6 to 7 hour peak falling dur"}},{"id":"sdl32_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pharmacologic effects of long-acting insulin preparations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes takes insulin detemir once each morning plus rapid-acting insulin with meals. Her daytime readings are at goal, but her glucose on waking is persistently high. Which best explains this pattern, based on detemir's structure and duration?","choices":{"D":"Its B29 myristic acid chain slows absorption by self-aggregation and albumin binding, but action lasts about 17 hours, so a second dose may be needed","A":"Detemir is a protamine suspension that peaks 6 to 7 hours after injection, so a morning dose causes daytime lows and leaves high glucose overnight","B":"Detemir is formulated at pH 4 and precipitates under the skin into a 24-hour plateau, so her waking highs come from a surge of insulin at dawn","C":"Detemir forms multihexamer chains under the skin that act for more than 42 hours, so her waking highs reflect stacking of doses, not waning","E":"Detemir binds the IGF-1 receptor more strongly than the insulin receptor, so its glucose-lowering effect fades as IGF-1 receptors downregulate"},"explanation":"Katzung (Ch 41) describes insulin detemir as an analog in which the terminal threonine is dropped from B30 and myristic acid, a C14 fatty acid, is attached to the B29 lysine. These changes prolong action by increasing self-aggregation in subcutaneous tissue and reversible albumin binding. Its duration is about 17 hours at therapeutic doses, and it is recommended once or twice a day for stable basal coverage, with lower within-subject variability than NPH or glargine. A single morning dose can therefore wane before the next morning. Protamine with a peak at 6 to 7 hours describes NPH. Formulation at pH 4 with a 24-hour plateau describes glargine. Multihexamer chains lasting more than 42 hours describe degludec. Katzung notes increased IGF-1 receptor binding for glargine, not detemir, and does not link it to loss of effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Basal insulins (Katzung Ch 41): NPH (protamine; peak 6 to 7 h; lasts 10 to 20 h), glargine (pH 4 precipitate; about 24 h), detemir (C14 myristic acid; albumin binding; about 17 h), degludec (multihexamers; more than 42 h), icodec (C20 diacid; once weekly). COMLEX: Glargine and detemir cannot be mixed in the same syringe with other insulins (Katzung Ch 41).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — rapid-acting insulin analogs cover meals on top of basal insulin","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D"},{"id":"sdl32_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the pharmacologic effects of long-acting insulin preparations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus and severe insulin resistance requires 320 units of basal insulin glargine U100 daily for adequate glycemic control. He reports that each injection volume is extremely large and uncomfortable, and his endocrinologist is considering switching him to a concentrated basal insulin preparation. Which of the following concentrated long-acting insulin formulations reduces the injection volume while maintaining equivalent glycemic effect, and what structural modification enables its ultra-long duration of action?","choices":{"E":"Insulin degludec U200 delivers the same dose in half the injection volume compared to U100; its ultra-long duration exceeding 42 hours is achieved through conjugation of hexadecanoic acid (C16) to B29-Lys via a gamma-L-glutamyl spacer, enabling multihexameric chain formation in subcutaneous tissue that produces slow, sustained monomer release","A":"Insulin glargine U100 combined with insulin detemir U100 in a 1:1 ratio can be administered in a single injection at half the volume of either preparation alone; the complementary albumin-binding mechanisms of glargine and detemir create a synergistic interaction that triples the effective duration compared with either preparation used in isolation","B":"Regular insulin U500 provides the concentration benefit at 5× the standard concentration, and when used as a basal preparation it achieves an intermediate duration of 10–20 hours through protamine binding; the concentrated formulation reduces injection volume by 80% and its duration is sufficiently prolonged to provide twice-daily basal coverage in most severely insulin-resistant patients","C":"Insulin NPH can be concentrated to U300 by increasing the protamine concentration during manufacturing, achieving a duration exceeding 30 hours through the denser protamine-insulin complex; this concentrated NPH U300 delivers the same dose in one-third the volume and eliminates the peak action that limits standard NPH use in conventional dosing regimens","D":"Insulin lispro U200 provides the concentrated formulation for severely resistant patients, and its ultra-long basal effect is achieved by adding two arginine residues to the B-chain C-terminus at the time of manufacture, which causes lispro to form stable hexamers in subcutaneous tissue that dissolve slowly over 36–48 hours before becoming monomers"},"explanation":"For severely insulin-resistant patients requiring large basal doses, concentrated formulations reduce injection volume: glargine U300 (Toujeo) = 3× concentration, so the U100 volume is reduced by 2/3; degludec U200 (Tresiba) = 2× concentration, >42 h duration, volume halved. Degludec's ultra-long action comes from C16 hexadecanoic acid conjugated at B29-Lys via a gamma-L-glutamyl spacer, enabling formation of massive multihexameric chains in SC tissue that slowly dissolve (half-life 25 h). U500 regular insulin is available for severely resistant patients needing high prandial doses but is NOT a basal preparation and has a different duration profile similar to NPH. Glargine and detemir cannot be mixed in the same syringe with other insulins; the concentrated basal options are glargine U300 and degludec U200. NPH is available only as U100; the concentrated basal options are glargine U300 and degludec U200. Lispro is a rapid-acting analog; the two arginine residues belong to glargine, and lispro U200 is not the basal option asked about.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Concentrated insulins: Glargine U300 (Toujeo), Degludec U200 (Tresiba), Lispro U200 (Humalog/Lyumjev), Regular U500 (Humulin R). Only use the correct device/syringe for each concentration to avoid dosing errors. U500 requires a U500 syringe. COMLEX: Volume reduction matters for patient comfort and adherence — large injection volumes cause pain, bruising, and poor absorption, all addressable with concentrated formulations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 2 — Long-Acting Insulin Preparations: Concentrated insulin formulations for severe insulin resistance and the structural basis of degludec ultra-long action","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"Glargine and detemir cannot be mixed in the same syringe wit","B":"U500 regular insulin is availa","C":"NPH is available only as U100; the concentrated basal option","D":"Lispro is a rapid-acting analog; the two arginine residues b"}},{"id":"sdl32_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the types of insulin delivery systems.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man with type 1 diabetes on multiple daily injections has frequent overnight lows, high readings before breakfast, and lows after afternoon soccer practice. He switches to a standard insulin pump that is not linked to automated sensor control. Which pump feature most directly addresses his problems?","choices":{"D":"A programmable basal profile tailored to him, so that rapid-acting insulin delivery can differ overnight, between meals, and with exercise","A":"A depot of long-acting insulin in the pump reservoir, which gives a steady 24-hour background without any need for programmed infusion rates","B":"Automatic suspension of insulin when sensor glucose falls below target, which prevents his lows without any input from him","C":"Intraperitoneal delivery through the cannula, which sends insulin to the liver first and removes the need for glucose monitoring","E":"Intravenous delivery of regular insulin through the catheter, which acts within minutes and removes the risk of ketoacidosis if it stops"},"explanation":"Katzung (Ch 41) states that a major advantage of an insulin pump is the establishment of a basal profile tailored to the patient, allowing better overnight and between-meal glucose control and management of the glycemic excursions that occur with exercise. Pumps deliver short-acting insulin throughout the day and night, and almost all patients use rapid-acting analogs; software helps calculate boluses and tracks time since the last bolus. Long-acting insulin is not used in the reservoir. Automatic suspension when sensor glucose falls below target is a feature of closed-loop systems, which this pump lacks. Standard pumps deliver into subcutaneous tissue, not the peritoneum or a vein. Pump users must still monitor glucose four to six times a day, and interrupted delivery can cause ketoacidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CSII uses rapid-acting analogs; advantage = programmable basal rates; risks = ketoacidosis when delivery is interrupted and skin infections (Katzung Ch 41). Closed-loop systems add CGM-driven basal adjustment and suspension. COMLEX: Good pump candidates are motivated, educated patients willing to check glucose four to six times a day (Katzung Ch 41).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — rapid-acting insulin analogs and their short duration of action","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Long-acting insulin is not use","C":"Standard pumps deliver into su","E":"Standard pumps deliver into su"}},{"id":"sdl32_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the types of insulin delivery systems.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy with type 1 diabetes mellitus is transitioning from multiple daily injections (insulin glargine once daily + insulin aspart before meals) to a continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion pump. His diabetes educator is reviewing the insulin that will be loaded into his new pump. Which of the following best identifies the correct insulin type to load into the pump reservoir and explains why long-acting insulin preparations are not appropriate for use in CSII systems?","choices":{"E":"Any insulin preparation approved for subcutaneous injection is equally appropriate for use in CSII pumps as long as the pump delivery rate is adjusted to account for differences in onset and duration; insulin glargine can be used by simply programming a lower hourly basal rate to compensate for its prolonged duration relative to the rapid-acting analogs used in most other patients","A":"Only insulin glargine is approved for use in CSII pumps because its pH 4.0 formulation resists bacterial contamination in the subcutaneous cannula for up to 7 days without reservoir changes; rapid-acting analogs have neutral pH formulations that support bacterial growth in the catheter tubing, making them unsafe for continuous subcutaneous delivery over multi-day reservoir fill cycles","B":"Only NPH insulin is approved for CSII pump use because its protamine-based formulation prevents crystallization within pump tubing at body temperature; rapid-acting insulin analogs form zinc-stabilized hexamers in pump reservoirs at 37°C that can occlude the infusion catheter and trigger the pump's over-pressure alarm, requiring emergency catheter replacement","C":"Insulin regular (U100) is the standard choice for CSII pumps because its well-established safety profile includes the longest post-marketing history of any insulin used subcutaneously; rapid-acting analogs are not approved for continuous infusion because their hexamer-disrupting amino acid modifications increase their immunogenicity and risk of immune reactions at the infusion site","D":"Only rapid-acting insulin analogs (insulin lispro, aspart, or glulisine) are used in CSII pumps; long-acting insulins (glargine, detemir, degludec) are not suitable for pump use because their prolonged subcutaneous pharmacokinetics are incompatible with the frequent, algorithm-driven microdose adjustments required for dynamic basal rate modulation throughout the day"},"explanation":"CSII pump reservoir: only rapid-acting analogs (lispro, aspart, glulisine). The rationale: (1) predictable, short 3–4 h duration allows accurate \"insulin on board\" tracking; (2) ~5% absorption variability enables reliable algorithm-driven dosing; (3) the pump provides both continuous basal delivery and patient-triggered meal boluses using the same rapid-acting insulin — the pump algorithm simulates the body's physiologic dynamic insulin secretion. Long-acting insulins' slow, unpredictable pharmacokinetics in the pump reservoir would make basal rate adjustments meaningless (the insulin would already be delivered slowly regardless of pump program changes). When transitioning to pump therapy, long-acting insulin must be discontinued. Pumps deliver short-acting insulin, with rapid-acting analogs the insulin of choice; glargine is a basal insulin that precipitates at body pH to form a slow depot. Pumps deliver short-acting insulin, almost always rapid-acting analogs; NPH is an intermediate-acting protamine insulin. Rapid-acting analogs are the insulin of choice for pumps, giving lower HbA1c than regular insulin there, and their immunogenicity is identical to regular insulin's.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CSII = rapid-acting analogs ONLY. Discontinue long-acting insulin when starting pump. The pump provides basal delivery + meal boluses from ONE insulin type. Key pump calculations: total daily dose (TDD) split into basal and bolus insulin. COMLEX: Starting pump therapy requires extensive patient education — carb counting, pump operation, sick day management, catheter site care; this is COMLEX patient management content.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 3 — Insulin Delivery Systems: Rationale for exclusive use of rapid-acting analogs in CSII pump therapy","isHighYield":true,"correct":"D","whyNot":{"A":"Pumps deliver short-acting insulin, with rapid-acting analog","B":"Pumps deliver short-acting insulin, almost always rapid-acti","C":"Rapid-acting analogs are the insulin of choice for pumps, gi","E":"Long-acting insulins' slow, un"}},{"id":"sdl32_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the types of insulin delivery systems.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with long-standing type 2 diabetes and severe insulin resistance has been prescribed regular insulin U500 (Humulin R U-500). His physician gave him a verbal instruction to inject \"30 units\" using whatever syringe is available at home. At his next visit, he reports repeated episodes of profound hypoglycemia. His caregiver reports she drew up \"30 marks\" using a standard U100 insulin syringe from a local pharmacy. Which of the following best explains the dosing error that occurred and what instruction should have been given?","choices":{"A":"A standard U100 syringe calibrated in units reads \"30 marks\" as 30 units of a U100 solution (0.3 mL); when used to draw U500 insulin, the same 0.3 mL volume actually contains 150 units — five times the intended dose; the prescription should have specified use of a dedicated U500 insulin syringe or provided the correct volume in milliliters to avoid this five-fold concentration error","B":"The dosing error occurred because U500 insulin has an onset of only 5 minutes, which is 25 times faster than regular U100 insulin; when injected subcutaneously with a standard syringe, the accelerated absorption of concentrated U500 produces serum insulin levels that overwhelm the counterregulatory capacity of the liver and adrenal glands, causing hypoglycemia at any dose regardless of volume accuracy","C":"The caregiver inadvertently administered 30 units of U500 insulin intravenously rather than subcutaneously because the short needle of the U100 insulin syringe penetrated the subcutaneous layer and entered a dermal capillary; intravenous delivery of 30 units bypassed the subcutaneous depot and caused immediate peak serum insulin concentrations fivefold higher than the SC route would have produced","D":"U500 regular insulin is a protamine-based suspension similar to NPH that must be resuspended by rolling before injection; the caregiver failed to resuspend the vial, and the concentrated protamine-insulin precipitate was withdrawn instead of the properly suspended solution, creating a bolus that was fivefold more concentrated than the uniformly mixed preparation would have been","E":"The error occurred because the patient's pharmacy dispensed a U500 prefilled pen that automatically delivers doses in units without volume conversion; the caregiver pressed the pen plunger 30 times rather than setting the dose dial to 30 units, delivering 30 injections of the default 2-unit increment and administering a cumulative 60 units rather than the intended 30"},"explanation":"U500 regular insulin is 5× the concentration of U100 (500 units/mL vs 100 units/mL). When a U100 syringe is used to draw U500 insulin, the volume markings are wrong: \"30 units\" on a U100 syringe = 0.3 mL, but 0.3 mL of U500 contains 150 units — a 5-fold overdose. The FDA requires a dedicated U500 syringe for U500 vials, or the prescriber must specify the dose in BOTH units AND volume (in mL) if a tuberculin syringe is used. U500 prefilled pens (Humulin R U500 pen) automatically deliver units correctly and are safer. U500 has a different pharmacokinetic profile than U100 regular — its duration resembles NPH (prolonged) due to the high concentration creating a slow-dissociating depot. U500 regular insulin has a time-action profile similar to NPH, not a faster onset; the hypoglycemia came from measuring five-fold concentrated insulin with a U100 syringe. The overdose came from measuring U500 insulin with a U100 syringe without converting units to volume, not from accidental intravenous injection. U500 regular insulin is a concentrated clear solution, not a protamine suspension requiring resuspension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: U500 regular insulin = 5× concentration. U100 syringe marks are WRONG for U500. Must use U500-specific syringe or specify mL volume. U500 duration resembles NPH (longer than U100 regular). Available as prefilled pen (safer). COMLEX: Medication concentration errors are a patient safety topic; pharmacovigilance — U500 is a high-alert medication per ISMP (Institute for Safe Medication Practices) guidelines.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 3 — Insulin Delivery Systems: U500 insulin concentration error and the requirement for dedicated U500 syringes","isHighYield":true,"correct":"A","whyNot":{"B":"U500 regular insulin has a time-action profile similar to NP","C":"The overdose came from measuring U500 insulin with a U100 sy","D":"U500 regular insulin is a concentrated clear solution, not a"}},{"id":"sdl32_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the immunopathology of insulin therapy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman who has had type 1 diabetes since childhood has shallow, depressed areas of lost subcutaneous fat on her thighs at sites she injected decades ago, when she used animal-derived insulin. She now uses an analog insulin and rotates sites. Which best explains these lesions and their likely course?","choices":{"B":"Immune-mediated lipoatrophy from animal insulin preparations, now rare, and injecting newer insulins into the area often restores the contour","A":"Lipohypertrophy from the local lipogenic effect of repeated same-site injections, which resolves if she keeps injecting into the same area","C":"IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation at the injection sites, which progresses with each analog injection and requires desensitization","D":"High-titer IgG anti-insulin antibodies neutralizing insulin in the skin, which causes insulin resistance and requires concentrated insulin","E":"Pressure necrosis of fat from injecting cold insulin, which is permanent because adipocytes cannot be replaced once they have died"},"explanation":"Katzung (Ch 41) states that injection of animal insulin preparations sometimes led to atrophy of subcutaneous fatty tissue at the injection site; since the development of human and analog insulins of neutral pH, this type of immune complication is almost never seen, and injecting these newer preparations directly into the atrophic area often restores normal contours. Hypertrophy of subcutaneous fat remains a problem when insulin is injected repeatedly at the same site and is corrected by avoiding that site, so it raises rather than depresses the skin and does not resolve with continued same-site use. Insulin allergy is an IgE-mediated immediate hypersensitivity with urticaria, not a slowly formed depression. Low-titer IgG antibodies are common and usually harmless, and high titers cause resistance rather than local fat loss. Cold insulin injection is not described as a cause.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Insulin immunopathology (Katzung Ch 41): IgE allergy (rare; often to non-insulin contaminants), IgG antibodies (low titer common; high titer rarely causes resistance), lipoatrophy (animal insulins; now almost never seen). Lipohypertrophy is non-immune and comes from repeated same-site injection. COMLEX: Rotate injection sites to avoid lipohypertrophy and erratic absorption (Katzung Ch 41).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 3 — insulin delivery with syringes and pens, including injection site rotation","isHighYield":true,"correct":"B","whyNot":{"A":"Hypertrophy of subcutaneous fa","C":"Insulin allergy is an IgE-medi","E":"Cold insulin injection is not "}},{"id":"sdl32_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the immunopathology of insulin therapy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus has been on insulin for 8 years and injects insulin glargine once daily in his left abdomen — always the exact same spot. He presents to clinic because of worsening glycemic control despite no changes in diet, activity, or medication doses. His HbA1c has risen from 7.1% to 9.4% over the past year. On physical examination, the left periumbilical area has a rubbery, thickened, painless subcutaneous mass approximately 3 cm in diameter. His right abdomen and thighs have no abnormalities. Which of the following best explains this finding and its contribution to his worsening glycemic control?","choices":{"E":"Repeated injection at the same anatomic site causes lipohypertrophy from local insulin-stimulated subcutaneous fat proliferation; the hypertrophied tissue has altered vascularity and reduced absorption, causing erratic and delayed insulin delivery from the site, which explains the deteriorating glycemic control despite unchanged doses","A":"Repeated injection at the same site causes lipoatrophy through an IgG immune complex reaction against the glargine formulation; complement activation destroys subcutaneous adipocytes, but the dermis is spared and becomes thickened and fibrotic; injection into the atrophic area produces paradoxically faster insulin absorption rather than slower absorption, causing postprandial hypoglycemia alternating with fasting hyperglycemia","B":"The subcutaneous mass represents a foreign body granuloma formed by macrophage aggregation around the needle tip fragment that broke off during a prior injection; the granuloma has incorporated neighboring adipocytes into a fibrous capsule that traps injected insulin within the capsule rather than releasing it into circulation, effectively reducing the bioavailable dose by approximately 40%","C":"Repeated injection at the same site causes progressive insulin degradation by locally recruited eosinophils that secrete insulin-specific proteases; the protease activity increases proportionally with the number of injections at a single site, so that after 8 years of single-site injection, the effective dose reaching systemic circulation is less than 20% of the injected amount","D":"The subcutaneous mass represents a localized amyloid deposit formed by insulin glargine molecules that aggregate at acidic pH when the glargine depot forms at the injection site; the amyloid slows insulin release by creating a diffusion barrier, and the mass has expanded over time as glargine continues to deposit, progressively reducing the effective absorbed dose"},"explanation":"Lipohypertrophy: repeated injection at the same site → local insulin-stimulated proliferation of subcutaneous fat and connective tissue → indurated, rubbery mass. The hypertrophied tissue has abnormal microvasculature and altered fibrous structure, producing erratic, delayed, and reduced insulin absorption from that site. Patients often prefer injecting into hypertrophied areas because they are less painful (reduced nerve density), creating a vicious cycle. Management: rotate injection sites systematically (abdomen, thighs, upper arms, buttocks) and avoid the hypertrophied area entirely. This is one of the most common causes of unexplained glycemic deterioration in long-term insulin users. A thickened mass at one repeatedly used site is fat hypertrophy, whereas lipoatrophy was an immune complication of animal insulins that is almost never seen with neutral-pH human and analog insulins. Hypertrophy of subcutaneous fat from repeated injection at the same site explains the mass and the impaired absorption, not a granuloma around a needle fragment. Lipohypertrophy from repeated injection at one site impairs insulin absorption; eosinophil proteases degrading insulin are not the mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lipohypertrophy = non-immune, repeated-site injection, rubbery mass, altered absorption → erratic glucose control. Lipoatrophy = immune, animal insulin, now rare with human analogs. Management: strict site rotation using a systematic pattern. Fastest absorption: abdomen > arms > thighs > buttocks. COMLEX: Physical exam of injection sites is part of the diabetes physical exam — examining the abdomen for lipohypertrophy is high-yield clinical skills content.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 4 — Insulin Immunopathology: Lipohypertrophy from repeated injection site use causing erratic insulin absorption and glycemic deterioration","isHighYield":true,"correct":"E","whyNot":{"A":"A thickened mass at one repeatedly used site is fat hypertro","B":"Hypertrophy of subcutaneous fat from repeated injection at t","C":"Lipohypertrophy from repeated injection at one site impairs "}},{"id":"sdl32_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the immunopathology of insulin therapy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes mellitus has been on insulin therapy for 22 years. Over the past 18 months, her insulin dose requirements have escalated from 60 to 420 units per day despite no change in diet or activity level and no weight gain. Fasting insulin levels are high, but C-peptide is undetectable. Anti-insulin IgG antibody titers are markedly elevated. Her HbA1c is 11.8% on the current regimen. She reports no symptoms of anaphylaxis or urticaria after injections. She is known to have systemic lupus erythematosus diagnosed 3 years ago. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of her escalating insulin requirements?","choices":{"C":"High-titer IgG anti-insulin antibodies neutralize a significant proportion of the administered insulin by binding it in the circulation before it can reach insulin receptors; this immune insulin resistance requires dramatically higher doses to deliver enough free (unbound) insulin for glycemic control, and its association with SLE reflects a shared predisposition to autoantibody production","A":"The elevated anti-insulin IgG antibodies are acting as carrier molecules that extend insulin's circulatory half-life rather than neutralizing it; the paradoxically high insulin requirements result from accelerated renal insulin clearance triggered by IgG-insulin complex glomerular trapping, which reduces net insulin bioavailability proportionally to the degree of immune complex formation","B":"The elevated IgG anti-insulin antibodies are cross-reacting with the patient's insulin receptors and causing receptor downregulation by competing with native IgE for the Fcε receptor on adipocytes, which reduces GLUT4 translocation independently of IgG antibody titer; the SLE connection is incidental because lupus nephritis has reduced her ability to excrete the anti-insulin IgG produced by her B cells","D":"The patient has developed lipoatrophy at all potential injection sites due to the high-titer IgG immune complexes activating complement at the subcutaneous depot, resulting in near-complete failure of insulin absorption from subcutaneous tissue; the measured serum anti-insulin IgG titer is a consequence rather than a cause of the resistance, reflecting the accumulation of unabsorbed insulin antigen in the subcutaneous depot","E":"The high insulin requirements reflect pharmacodynamic rather than pharmacokinetic resistance; the IgG anti-insulin antibodies have stabilized a conformationally inactive form of the insulin receptor by binding to the extracellular alpha subunit, preventing the conformational change required for receptor autophosphorylation and downstream IRS-1 tyrosine phosphorylation in both hepatic and peripheral tissues"},"explanation":"Immune insulin resistance: circulating IgG anti-insulin antibodies bind exogenous insulin in the bloodstream, forming insulin-IgG complexes that cannot bind insulin receptors. The \"free\" (unbound) fraction of administered insulin is drastically reduced, requiring massive dose escalation to achieve adequate glycemic control. This is distinct from receptor-level insulin resistance in T2D (which is post-binding). The association with SLE reflects the shared immune dysregulation (B-cell hyperactivation, autoantibody production) of autoimmune diseases. Management: switch to human recombinant insulin (less immunogenic), high-dose insulin therapy, and in refractory cases, immunosuppression. High-titer IgG anti-insulin antibodies neutralize insulin's action, which rarely produces insulin resistance; they do not act as carriers that speed renal clearance. Immune insulin resistance comes from IgG antibodies against insulin that neutralize its action; IgE antibodies cause insulin allergy, which she lacks. Lipoatrophy was an injection-site complication of animal insulins and is almost never seen now; her resistance comes from high-titer IgG antibodies neutralizing insulin. Antibodies to the insulin receptor are a separate cause of insulin resistance; her antibodies are anti-insulin IgG that neutralize insulin itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Immune insulin resistance = IgG anti-insulin antibodies (pre-receptor, not post-receptor like T2D). Rare but dramatic dose escalation. Associated with autoimmune diseases (SLE, Hashimoto's). Distinguish: (1) C-peptide low → confirms exogenous insulin dependence (not endogenous hyperinsulinism); (2) IgG titer elevated confirms immune etiology; (3) no IgE-mediated symptoms confirms this is NOT allergy. COMLEX: The \"insulin edema\" phenomenon is a separate complication — fluid retention with insulin initiation, particularly in previously uncontrolled T1D.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 4 — Insulin Immunopathology: IgG-mediated immune insulin resistance mechanism and its association with systemic autoimmune disease","isHighYield":true,"correct":"C","whyNot":{"A":"High-titer IgG anti-insulin antibodies neutralize insulin's ","B":"Immune insulin resistance comes from IgG antibodies against ","D":"Lipoatrophy was an injection-site complication of animal ins","E":"Antibodies to the insulin receptor are a separate cause of i"}}]};
